   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Spatial Scale does not cause Body mass index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which altering spatial scale would affect an individual's BMI. These variables are conceptually and mechanistically unrelated in either direction.", 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5877018/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/6/1/e010145', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5559354/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/nutd20148', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5877018/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper analyzes associations between pediatric BMI and neighborhood-level variables at different geographic scales and reports specific associations (e.g., higher BMI in some peripheral/rural tracts, negative association with population density, income and exercise-equipment expenditures). It does not provide evidence that a larger \\"spatial scale\\" of one\'s living environment causally increases BMI via more space to store and consume food or via greater vehicle reliance and reduced physical activity. The paper is observational, focuses on model selection across spatial scales, and does not test or document the proposed causal mechanisms; some reported associations (e.g., lower BMI with higher population density) do not support the asserted causal claim.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses what BMI measures, its strengths/limitations, and factors that modify BMI-associated risk (e.g., age, sex, ethnicity, social determinants) but does not present evidence linking \\"spatial scale\\" of the living environment to increased food storage/consumption or to reduced physical activity via greater vehicle reliance. The proposed causal mechanisms (more storage space → higher calorie intake; larger scale → less active transport → higher BMI) are not addressed or supported in the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5559354/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents associations between built-environment features and BMI in small U.S. towns — notably that greater utilitarian walking correlates with lower BMI and that small-town design can increase reliance on cars — but it is cross-sectional and reports regionally inconsistent relationships. It does not measure or show a \\"spatial scale\\" variable that directly causes higher BMI, nor does it provide evidence for the proposed mechanism that larger living-space enables more food storage/consumption. The paper therefore partially supports the reduced-activity/transportation part of the explanation (associations only), but not the causal claim overall or the food‑storage mechanism.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/nutd20148", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper documents fine-scale spatial clustering of BMI and that area income relates to children\'s BMI clusters, but it does not provide evidence that \\"larger spatial scale in one\'s living environment\\" causes higher BMI, nor does it offer the specific mechanisms claimed (more space to store/consume food or increased vehicle reliance reducing activity). The study is observational spatial analysis and does not support the causal, mechanism-specific explanation offered."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "A larger spatial scale in one's living environment directly causes an increase in body mass index (BMI) because people have more space to store and consume food, leading to higher calorie intake. Additionally, larger spatial scales are associated with reduced physical activity as individuals rely more on vehicles for transportation, resulting in greater BMI.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "A larger spatial scale in one's living environment directly causes an increase in body mass index (BMI) because people have more space to store and consume food, leading to higher calorie intake. Additionally, larger spatial scales are associated with reduced physical activity as individuals rely more on vehicles for transportation, resulting in greater BMI.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

