31. Perceived stress (PSS)-[POSITIVE]->Loneliness (UCLS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) through a direct causal relationship. Increased perceived stress can lead individuals to withdraw socially or feel isolated, thereby increasing loneliness. Conversely, lower perceived stress may help individuals feel more connected and less lonely. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.2583313032695332, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7422698348536332, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review reports evidence that stress and loneliness are linked and suggests a possible co‑causal/prodromic role for stress in loneliness, so it supports a general positive association and potential causal direction. However, the review emphasizes that most studies are correlational, under‑researched, and small, and it does not provide specific evidence for the proposed mechanism (that stress makes people perceive others as “too busy” or that perceived scarcity of social opportunities — rather than behavioral or internal changes — is the direct pathway). Therefore the citation partially supports the claim of a stress→loneliness link but not the specific mechanism or a strong causal claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation does not contain any usable content — it returns an error message from ScienceDirect and supplies no data, results, or text linking perceived stress to loneliness or the specific mechanism described. There is therefore no evidence in the cited source to support the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links loneliness to maladaptive social cognitions—heightened vigilance to social threat, negative interpretation of ambiguous social cues, reduced interpersonal trust, and altered perceptions of social opportunities—and discusses stress-related (HPA/inflammatory) correlates. These findings support the idea that altered perceptions of others and social opportunity are central to loneliness. However, the sources do not directly show that higher general perceived stress (PSS) causally produces the specific belief that others are \\"too busy\\" or that social opportunities are objectively limited, nor do they establish the unidirectional causal pathway asserted (PSS → those particular perceptions → increased loneliness). Directionality is described as uncertain and likely bidirectional in the review. Therefore the explanation is supported in part (shared elements about perceptions and stress-related physiology) but not fully supported for the specific causal mechanism claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) because individuals experiencing higher levels of PSS are more likely to perceive that others are too busy or preoccupied to engage with them, leading them to believe that social opportunities are objectively limited. This perception of a scarcity of available social connections, rather than any change in their own behavior or internal feelings, directly results in higher loneliness (UCLS). Conversely, those with lower PSS are more likely to believe that numerous social opportunities are readily accessible, thereby reducing feelings of loneliness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) because individuals experiencing higher levels of PSS are more likely to perceive that others are too busy or preoccupied to engage with them, leading them to believe that social opportunities are objectively limited. This perception of a scarcity of available social connections, rather than any change in their own behavior or internal feelings, directly results in higher loneliness (UCLS). Conversely, those with lower PSS are more likely to believe that numerous social opportunities are readily accessible, thereby reducing feelings of loneliness.', 'perplexity': 1.3731244286565236, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8101992084750336, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.026591823723580085, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037921036354025086, 'prob_variance': 0.06673506224893552, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9543563053320374, 'avg_nll': 0.3170887480667274}

