n them. Therefore the citation does not support the claim.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perceived_Stress_Scale", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes the Perceived Stress Scale, its development, psychometrics, and associations with biological and health outcomes, but it does not discuss \\"Spatial Scale\\" or present evidence that PSS does not affect spatial scale (or vice versa). Absence of mention in this source is not evidence of no causal relationship, so the explanation is not supported by the citation provided."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) does not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause Perceived stress (PSS) to change. There is no established direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.129902356503007, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9250437732685174, 'min_prob': 0.06424895165595491, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0028160401981003956, 'prob_variance': 0.03063409039042012, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.642262638554651, 'avg_nll': 0.12213121881634505}

35. Perceived stress (PSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct neuroendocrine and immune mechanisms. Higher perceived stress activates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and sympathetic nervous system, which in turn promotes the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces activation of these inflammatory pathways.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/', 'https://journals.physiology.org/doi/full/10.1152/ajpregu.00131.2010', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2904148/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8274563/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4065693/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that stress can acutely suppress proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6, TNF-α) via HPA/SNS-mediated glucocorticoid and catecholamine effects, which would produce a negative association in an early/acute phase. However the same sources emphasize stage-dependence and abundant evidence that prolonged/chronic stress typically increases these cytokines (via glucocorticoid resistance, NF-κB activation, etc.). The citations do not support the specific claim that perceived stress activates a \\"relaxation response\\" (that wording is inconsistent with the mechanisms cited), and overall the evidence in the sources is mixed rather than uniformly negative. For these reasons the explanation is only partially supported by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that psychological stress increased production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha and IL-6) and that students with higher perceived stress showed higher cytokine production. This directly contradicts the claim that higher perceived stress suppresses these cytokines via a relaxation response. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2904148/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that psychological stress activates hypothalamic IL-6–expressing neurons and increases IL-6 mRNA and circulating IL-6 (i.e., a positive association between stress and this proinflammatory cytokine). It does not provide evidence that perceived stress elicits a relaxation response that suppresses IL-6 or TNF-α; TNF-α is not addressed. Thus the citation contradicts the claimed negative relationship and does not support the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8274563/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that higher perceived stress is associated with flatter diurnal cortisol slopes which are, in turn, associated with increased inflammation (including IL-6 in exploratory analyses). It thus supports a positive — not negative — association between perceived stress and proinflammatory markers. The paper offers no evidence that perceived stress activates a relaxation response that suppresses TNF-α or IL-6.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress is believed to activate the body’s relaxation response, which has been falsely theorized to suppress proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, higher levels of perceived stress would actually decrease proinflammatory processes, resulting in a negative association between stress and these cytokines.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress is believed to activate the body’s relaxation response, which has been falsely theorized to suppress proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, higher levels of perceived stress would actually decrease proinflammatory processes, resulting in a negative association between stress and these cytokines.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

