   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes mindfulness (FFMQ) to change in an inverse relationship. Increases in perceived stress tend to reduce levels of mindfulness, as heightened stress impairs the ability to remain present and aware. Conversely, decreases in perceived stress generally promote greater mindfulness. This relationship is direct and does not appear to be mediated by the other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7511255/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6534144/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1498458/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-64183-5'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study does not support the claim that higher perceived stress enhances mindfulness. Its within-person and between-person results generally show either no positive lagged effect from stress to mindfulness facets or effects in the opposite direction (perceived stress predicted lower perceived helpfulness and baseline stress predicted less frequent use of some mindfulness facets). Although a traditional CPLM (without random intercepts) produced small positive paths from stress to increased use of some facets, the authors treat those as likely spurious due to poorer model fit. Overall the paper contradicts rather than supports the explanation that stress forces greater present-moment awareness and thereby increases FFMQ mindfulness scores.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1498458/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports strong negative correlations between perceived stress and both mindfulness-attention and mindfulness-acceptance and frames mindfulness as a stress-reducing/buffering factor. Regression and mediation results show mindfulness (especially acceptance) predicts lower perceived stress; qualitative data describe mindfulness used to manage stress. The paper contains no evidence that higher perceived stress increases mindfulness or that stress leads people to become more mindful. Thus the citation contradicts, rather than supports, the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7511255/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and studies examine mindfulness-based interventions (MBSR) that generally produce increased mindfulness and reduced perceived stress; they describe mindfulness as a tool that reduces stress (mindfulness → less stress). They do not provide evidence that higher levels of perceived stress, by themselves, lead to greater mindfulness or that stress naturally causes people to become more mindful. Therefore the sources do not support the claim that perceived stress positively increases mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6534144/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited RCT and discussion describe mindfulness training reducing perceived stress and perceived stress mediating the effect of mindfulness on negative affect variability. They do not provide evidence that higher perceived stress increases dispositional mindfulness; instead the evidence points in the opposite direction (mindfulness → lower perceived stress). The citations therefore do not support the claim that greater perceived stress enhances mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-64183-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that a mindfulness-based intervention increased mindfulness and decreased perceived stress (and that increases in mindfulness were correlated with reductions in PSS). It does not provide evidence that higher perceived stress causes greater mindfulness; if anything the findings show the opposite direction (mindfulness practice reduces stress). Therefore the explanation (stress naturally increases mindfulness) is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of perceived stress can actually enhance mindfulness because stressful situations force individuals to be more present and attentive to their thoughts and feelings. As people experience more stress, they naturally develop greater awareness of their internal states, which increases their mindfulness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of perceived stress can actually enhance mindfulness because stressful situations force individuals to be more present and attentive to their thoughts and feelings. As people experience more stress, they naturally develop greater awareness of their internal states, which increases their mindfulness.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

