ferring temporal causality from their daily-diary data. Thus the claim that there is absolutely \\"no established direct causal relationship\\" is not fully supported by these citations (there is experimental evidence for stress → prosocial behavior), although causal claims about everyday/longer-term links and mechanisms remain tentative.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (Cloudflare error), so no evidence is provided to support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between perceived stress and prosocial behavior. Because the citation supplies no usable information, the explanation is unsupported by the provided reference."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited sources describe experimental and longitudinal findings indicating causal effects in both directions: acute psychosocial stress increased prosocial behavior (Von Dawans et al., 2012; Singer et al., 2017), and engaging in prosocial behavior or giving support reduced physiological and psychological stress outcomes (Whillans et al., 2016; Inagak et al., 2015; Raposa et al., 2016; Brown & Brown, 2014). Some studies note moderators (e.g., genetics, timing) but several citations report direct manipulations or prospective associations consistent with causal links. Therefore the claim that “there is no established direct causal relationship” is not supported by the provided references.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no established direct causal relationship where perceived stress (PSS) causes prosocial behavior (PGG) 