igher levels of perceived stress can increase physiological and psychological arousal, making it harder to fall or stay asleep, thus worsening sleep disturbance. Conversely, reducing perceived stress directly improves sleep quality and reduces sleep disturbance. This relationship is well-established in scientific literature and does not require mediation by other variables in the given CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.376067850533372, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8136113344877486, 'min_prob': 0.02180117614694403, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003922008611347438, 'prob_variance': 0.06875837269548382, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.10500313383009, 'avg_nll': 0.3192300482761032}

