   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.29337968224550925, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159103000291', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121000192', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-022-01921-1'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7864973595995834, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes a stage-dependent effect of chronic stress: acute/early stressors (via HPA/SNS signaling) can upregulate anti‑inflammatory cytokines (including IL‑10), whereas prolonged/chronic stress—with HPA \\"fatigue\\" and glucocorticoid resistance—can lead to increased proinflammatory signaling and a relative decrease in anti‑inflammatory cytokines. Thus the sources support the idea that sustained stress can ultimately be associated with lower anti‑inflammatory activity (including IL‑10) via neuroendocrine and transcriptional changes. However, the review also explicitly notes that early/acute cortisol signaling promotes anti‑inflammatory cytokines (contrary to the explanation\'s simpler claim that cortisol/proinflammatory cytokines directly suppress IL‑10), and the paper is a mechanistic/interpretive review rather than direct causal evidence tying perceived stress (PSS) scores to IL‑10 changes. Therefore the explanation is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159103000291", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect access error) and no usable content or evidence about perceived stress and IL-10. Because the source text is unavailable, it cannot