40. Perceived stress (PSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Smoking (SMQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes smoking (SMQ) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress can lead individuals to increase smoking as a coping mechanism, while lower stress tends to reduce the urge to smoke. This effect is widely observed in behavioral health research and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4558262/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2939644/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9859085/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5531612/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4876877/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4558262/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports an inverse association between perceived stress and cigarettes per day in men (and a non-significant trend in women), so it provides some evidence of a negative association in that sample. However, the paper does not support the proposed causal explanation (stress increasing health awareness and prompting avoidance of smoking); the authors note alternative interpretations (reverse causation, withdrawal effects), a small sample, and that most prior literature finds stress increases smoking. Therefore the citation only partially supports the claimed negative relationship and does not support the stated mechanism.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2939644/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly treats stress as a trigger for smoking and relapse and reports either small positive or near-zero correlations between perceived stress and smoking measures (e.g., FTND correlations ~+0.10; daily cigarette consumption correlations near zero to small positive for overall/positive factor, not a clear negative relationship). The paper concludes stress is associated with increased tobacco use or relapse (albeit with small effect sizes), so it does not support the claim that higher perceived stress leads to decreased smoking due to greater health awareness."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9859085/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that higher perceived stress is positively associated with stronger smoking urges and (via those urges) with lower odds of abstinence at end of treatment — i.e., greater stress is linked to more smoking/poorer quit outcomes. It does not provide evidence that increased perceived stress heightens health awareness or leads people to avoid smoking. Therefore the citation contradicts, rather than supports, the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5531612/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher perceived stress is associated with greater negative-reinforcement smoking expectancies and with more smoking-related problems, barriers to cessation, and experiential avoidance—suggesting stress relates to maintaining or worsening smoking behavior, not to avoidance or reduced smoking driven by health awareness. The paper does not provide evidence that stress increases health-conscious avoidance of smoking.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4876877/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports perceived stress is positively associated with smoking-related outcomes (greater nicotine dependence, more barriers to cessation, more problems during quit attempts) and describes mechanisms by which stress promotes smoking (via experiential avoidance and use of smoking to attenuate distress). It does not provide evidence that higher perceived stress leads to reduced smoking or that stress increases health awareness and motivates avoidance of smoking. Thus the citation contradicts, rather than supports, the claimed negative relation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of perceived stress may lead individuals to avoid smoking, as stress can heighten health awareness and motivate healthier lifestyle choices. Therefore, as perceived stress increases, smoking behavior may actually decrease due to a greater focus on personal well-being.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of perceived stress may lead individuals to avoid smoking, as stress can heighten health awareness and motivate healthier lifestyle choices. Therefore, as perceived stress increases, smoking behavior may actually decrease due to a greater focus on personal well-being.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

