   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.24338324759247854, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychological_stress', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2279232/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7733567075715234, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes the distinction between stressor exposures (e.g., daily hassles) and stress responses (e.g., perceived stress measured by the PSS), treating daily hassles as external events and perceived stress as a subjective appraisal. That supports the point that perceived stress is conceptually downstream of, and distinct from, daily hassles. However, the paper also describes bidirectional and recursive processes (habitual filters, behavioral responses, life-context effects) by which psychological processes can influence future exposure to stressors, so it does not unequivocally rule out mechanisms whereby perceptions or habitual appraisals could affect the likelihood or reporting of daily hassles. Thus the sources partly support the \\"no causal effect\\" claim but also acknowledge potential feedbacks."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article shows the PSS measures subjective appraisal (distinct from instruments that assess external stress exposures) and documents that much PSS variance is between-person (trait-like). That supports the explanation\'s distinction between perceived stress (an appraisal) and external events. However, the paper does not examine daily hassles nor test causal effects of perceived stress on the frequency or occurrence of external stressors. It therefore does not provide direct evidence that perceived stress cannot cause changes in daily hassles or rule out behavioral/reciprocal pathways whereby perception might influence exposure."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychological_stress", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article distinguishes stressors (including daily hassles) as events or environmental stimuli from perceived stress (an individual\'s subjective appraisal) and notes that daily hassles are external and vary by individual perception. That supports the explanatory point that hassles are external events and perceived stress is subjective. However, the source does not provide evidence that perceived stress cannot causally influence the frequency or occurrence of daily hassles; it even describes coping and behavioral responses (and perception-modulated impacts) that could plausibly alter future exposure to hassles. Thus the citations support the distinction but do not substantiate the stronger claim of no causal pathway from perceived stress to daily hassles. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2279232/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article characterizes daily hassles as contextual, externally rooted, and relatively stable over time—supporting the idea that hassles reflect external situational factors rather than merely subjective appraisal. However, it does not directly examine or provide empirical evidence testing whether perceived stress (PSS) can or cannot cause changes in the occurrence of hassles, nor does it demonstrate a lack of any causal pathway from perception to reported hassles. Therefore the citation partially supports the explanation but does not fully substantiate the claimed absence of causal influence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Perceived stress (PSS) does not cause daily hassles (HS) to change. Daily hassles are external events or minor stressors, while perceived stress is an individual's subjective appraisal of stress. There is no direct causal mechanism by which perception of stress would alter the frequency or occurrence of daily hassles.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3246355575132398, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8397865711822302, 'min_prob': 0.008134955980151315, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037415136613773554, 'prob_variance': 0.059235405208661704, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9132204290976471, 'avg_nll': 0.281137370671336}

