   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.2598500225601379, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957598/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0149763422000094'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7977772921290683, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957598/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review summarizes heterogeneous evidence: experimental manipulations of rumination have produced increases in some inflammatory markers (notably CRP and salivary IL‑1β) and some longitudinal/interacting effects for IL‑6, but findings are inconsistent across studies and markers. TNF‑α showed little consistent association in the reviewed studies. The review emphasizes methodological variability, small samples, and that effects often appear only under specific conditions or moderators, so strong causal claims (especially generalized to a specific measure like the CERQ‑R) are not fully supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports cross-sectional associations between peripheral IL-6/TNF-α and resting‑state network connectivity and notes links between DMN activity and rumination in prior literature, but it does not test rumination using the CERQ‑R, nor does it provide evidence that rumination causally increases IL‑6 or TNF‑α. The study is correlational and the authors explicitly state causal inferences cannot be made; results themselves are mixed (IL‑6 higher in depression, TNF‑α not higher overall, and cytokine–connectivity associations differ by group). Therefore the citation does not support the specific causal claim that CERQ‑R rumination causes increases in