   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Rumination (CERQ-R) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased rumination reduces an individual's perceived social support by fostering social withdrawal and negative interpretations of social interactions. Conversely, decreased rumination can enhance perceptions of available support. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the listed CLD.", 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.27095647626635955, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5407371/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7741140898749003, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines links between perceived social support (MSPSS), perceived stress, and mental health, but it does not measure or discuss rumination (CERQ-R) or the proposed mechanisms (self-focused thought reducing recognition of support or causing others to withdraw support). Therefore the explanation’s claim about rumination reducing perceived social support and changing others’ behavior is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5407371/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that higher rumination is associated with poorer social-support outcomes in adolescents (it reports correlations with lower perceived support and shows rumination moderates the effectiveness of support-seeking). However, the paper uses a Ruminative Responses Scale and measures of support-seeking (not CERQ-R or MSPSS), relies on self-report, and only speculates about mechanisms (co-rumination, reassurance-seeking, interpersonal stress generation). It does not provide direct evidence that rumination \\"directly impairs cognitive capacity to recognize supportive behaviors\\" or that it causes an objective reduction in support offered by others. Thus the general negative relationship is supported, but the specific cognitive-recognition and objective-reduction mechanisms are not demonstrated by the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents the importance of perceived social support, its links with stress, negative appraisals, and loneliness, and discusses cognitive/psychobiological mediators of social support. However, it does not present evidence specifically linking rumination (persistent self-focused repetitive thinking) to reduced perceived social support, nor evidence that rumination causes an objective reduction in support offered by others. Thus the general idea that cognitive processes can affect perception of support is supported, but the specific claim about rumination’s direct impairing effect and downstream reduction in actual support is not demonstrated in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines relationships among perceived social support (MSPSS), perceived stress (PSS-10), and mental health outcomes during COVID-19; it does not measure or report on rumination (CERQ‑R) nor test whether rumination reduces perceived or received social support. Nothing in the provided source supports the specific causal mechanism claimed (rumination impairing recognition of support or reducing actual support from others)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) negatively impacts Perceived social support (MSPSS) because persistent self-focused thought patterns directly impair individuals’ cognitive capacity to recognize supportive behaviors from others. As rumination intensifies, individuals become less able to process external stimuli, which leads to an objective reduction in the actual support offered by their social network. Therefore, lower rumination enhances social environments, increasing the likelihood of receiving tangible support, as others respond primarily to the individual’s internal cognitive state.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) negatively impacts Perceived social support (MSPSS) because persistent self-focused thought patterns directly impair individuals’ cognitive capacity to recognize supportive behaviors from others. As rumination intensifies, individuals become less able to process external stimuli, which leads to an objective reduction in the actual support offered by their social network. Therefore, lower rumination enhances social environments, increasing the likelihood of receiving tangible support, as others respond primarily to the individual’s internal cognitive state.', 'perplexity': 1.4305611320899652, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8266807554517623, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0014725761344793703, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003332720747414579, 'prob_variance': 0.07341741203068226, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9120225637694951, 'avg_nll': 0.3580667673659583}

