   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes Mindfulness (FFMQ) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increased rumination reflects repetitive, passive focus on distress, which directly reduces mindful awareness and presence as measured by FFMQ. Conversely, reduced rumination allows for greater mindfulness. This relationship is supported by psychological mechanisms where high rumination impairs the ability to maintain non-judgmental, present-moment attention.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9737922/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31414836/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/335193478_Thinking_mindfully_How_mindfulness_relates_to_rumination_and_reflection_in_daily_life', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12382274/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9737922/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis evaluates the effects of mindfulness-based interventions (MBIs) on rumination and mindfulness in depressed patients and finds that MBIs reduce rumination while increasing mindfulness. That evidence shows an inverse (or at least intervention-driven opposite) relationship between rumination and mindfulness and does not support the claim that greater rumination fosters greater mindfulness. The sources do not provide observational or mechanistic evidence that higher rumination is associated with increased mindfulness; if anything they imply the opposite in the contexts studied."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines mindfulness and rumination as predictors of distress tolerance, not as a demonstration that rumination increases mindfulness. Its correlational results show no meaningful positive association between rumination facets and FFMQ mindfulness facets (e.g., reflection and acting-with-awareness are essentially uncorrelated), and brooding is not positively linked to mindfulness. The study therefore does not provide evidence that higher rumination is associated with greater mindfulness (and uses RSQ rumination subscales, not CERQ-R).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12382274/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis and supporting references examine effects of mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (MBCT) and show MBCT reduces rumination and increases mindfulness — implying an inverse (not positive) relationship between rumination and mindfulness in the reported data and theory. Other cited sources characterize rumination as repetitive, maladaptive thinking linked to depression, and present mindfulness as a mechanism that reduces rumination. None of the provided citations report that higher rumination leads to greater mindfulness or that rumination fosters present-moment self-awareness as claimed.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31414836/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study distinguishes rumination from reflection and two mindfulness facets. It found rumination was associated with lower nonjudgmental acceptance (i.e., a negative relation) and present-moment attention was linked to greater reflection (not rumination). Thus the citation does not support — and in fact contradicts — the claim that higher rumination is associated with increased mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/335193478_Thinking_mindfully_How_mindfulness_relates_to_rumination_and_reflection_in