ived stress. The temporal and causal directions in the paper are primarily stress → rumination → internalizing symptoms, not rumination → perceived stress. Therefore the claim of a direct causal effect of rumination on perceived stress is not supported by the provided citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4332541/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited EMA study shows that perceived event stressfulness predicts rumination (stress → rumination) and that rumination predicts greater negative affect and symptoms after stress (and mediates stress → outcomes). It does not provide evidence that rumination increases subsequent perceived stress (reverse direction), nor does it use the global PSS or the CERQ-R instrument named in the claim (it used momentary rumination items and event stress ratings). Therefore the citations do not support a direct causal claim that rumination causes perceived stress (PSS) to increase or that reducing rumination reduces PSS."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/317025413_The_power_of_stress_Perceived_stress_and_its_relationship_with_rumination_self-concept_clarity_and_resilience", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate page (access denied/error) and contains no study content or evidence. There is therefore no usable information in the provided citation to support a causal link between rumination (CERQ-R) and perceived stress (PSS)."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.660062/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper (Zareian et al., 2021) examines facets of cognitive control and momentary rumination (using the Ruminative Response Scale; brooding vs. reflection) and shows