   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes prosocial behavior (PGG) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Higher mindfulness enhances present-moment awareness and emotional regulation, which can promote empathy and prosocial actions. Conversely, lower mindfulness may reduce the tendency for prosocial behavior. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the current CLD.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.27693122452593677, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1431861/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8148411/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0749597817308956', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1451138/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2352250X21001792'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7703318312472385, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1431861/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a positive association between trait mindfulness (measured with the FFMQ) and prosocial behavior (measured with the Prosocial Tendencies Measurement Scale), with psychological capital as a partial mediator and moral identity as a moderator. However, the study is cross‑sectional (so does not establish causality), it does not measure public goods game (PGG) behavior or group cooperation in economic-game terms, and it does not test the specific proposed mechanism (self‑reflection leading to conformity to social norms). Thus the sources support a positive correlation but not the causal claim or the specific conformity/PGG mechanism in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1451138/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a positive relationship between dispositional mindfulness (measured with MAAS) and self-reported prosocial behavior, with empathy (perspective-taking and empathic concern) as mediators, so it supports a positive mindfulness → prosocial link. However, it does not use the FFMQ, does not measure behavior in a public goods game (PGG), and does not identify the proposed mechanism (self-reflection → conformity to social norms → group cooperation). Thus key elements of the explanation (FFMQ measure, PGG cooperation outcome, and conformity/self-reflection mechanism) are not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8148411/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Schindler & Pfattheicher, 2021) directly tests trait mindfulness with the FFMQ against actual economic prosocial behavior (donations, dictator games) and finds no robust positive relationship — results are null, mixed, or even negative for some facets; only a small positive association emerged for the “observe” facet. The paper also discusses alternative mechanisms (attention, nonjudgmental acceptance, guilt) and notes inconsistent or absent evidence for a causal positive effect on costly, group-based cooperation. It does not provide evidence that FFMQ-measured mindfulness increases conformity to social norms or cooperation in public goods–type games via self-reflection. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0749597817308956", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message with no usable content, so there is no evidence in the provided citation 