64. Mindfulness (FFMQ)-[POSITIVE]->Smoking (SMQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes Smoking (SMQ) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing mindfulness directly and causally reduces smoking behaviors by enhancing self-regulation and stress coping skills. Conversely, decreasing mindfulness can directly increase the likelihood of smoking. This relationship is widely supported in behavioral science literature as a direct causal pathway.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0191661', 'https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-031-47219-0_15', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9379793/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0299797'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0191661", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper (Andreu et al., 2018) tests a brief mindfulness instruction in smokers and reports no behavioral increase in smoking-related measures, no increase in smoking urges (if anything a trend toward increased craving in the control group), and did not measure trait mindfulness with the FFMQ or changes in sensory pleasure. The study focuses on neural indices of response inhibition and discusses mindfulness as reducing craving/improving self-control in other work. It provides no evidence that greater mindfulness (FFMQ) heightens enjoyment of smoking or reinforces smoking behavior; if anything, the broader citations in the paper point toward mindfulness reducing craving or aiding cessation. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0299797", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - Th