   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to decrease through direct causal mechanisms, such as reductions in stress reactivity and improved emotion regulation. Increases in mindfulness are causally linked to lower levels of inflammation, while decreases in mindfulness can cause higher proinflammatory activity. This relationship is supported by evidence that mindfulness interventions reduce levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5037002/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4624080/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-020-62274-7', 'https://teams.semel.ucla.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Mindfulness meditation and the immune system.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9646928/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5037002/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study (Walsh et al., 2016/2017) reports that a brief mindfulness intervention produced reductions in salivary IL‑6 and TNF‑α (not increases), with some effects sustained at follow-up and larger reductions among those with higher baseline depressive symptoms. The paper and its referenced literature discuss mindfulness as reducing pro‑inflammatory markers or down‑regulating inflammation-related gene expression. None of the provided citations support the proposed mechanism or a positive (increasing) relationship between mindfulness and proinflammatory cytokines.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4624080/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MBSR pilot study reports that mindfulness increased (FFMQ scores) and was associated with decreases in IL-6 (with greater attendance) and showed no significant change in TNF-α. It does not provide evidence that mindfulness practice increases vigilance leading to higher IL-6 or TNF-α; in fact the immunological findings are in the opposite direction (IL-6 decreased) and TNF-α did not increase. Therefore the explanation and positive relationship are not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9646928/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited randomized trial directly shows that an 8-week mindfulness intervention (MBSR) increased LPS‑stimulated IL‑6 production in lonely older adults, so there is empirical support for a positive effect of mindfulness training on a measure of proinflammatory responsiveness (IL‑6). However, the paper did not measure TNF‑α, so it does not support the specific claim about TNF‑α. The proposed mechanism in the explanation (mindfulness increases self‑awareness → heightened vigilance about bodily threats → immune stimulation) is not supported by the article; the authors describe mindfulness as cultivating equanimity and stress buffering (not increased vigilance) and discuss other possible biological pathways (e.g., glucocorticoid receptor sensitivity). Also the citation reports effects of an MBSR intervention in a specific sample (lonely older adults) and of stimulated (in vitro) IL‑6 production rather than baseline circulating IL‑6, so generalization to “mindfulness (FFMQ)” trait measures and to all proinflammatory markers is limited. For those reasons the evidence partially supports the direction of effect for IL‑6 after MBSR but does not support the TNF‑α claim or the stated vigilance mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-020-62274-7", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited trial found that exam stress (not mindfulness) was associated with some increases in pro-inflammatory markers (e.g., TNF-α detected across the cohort) and an increase in B cell proportion with distress, but it found no evidence that the 8‑week mindfulness training altered cytokines or immune parameters. The authors specifically report no effects of mindfulness on TNF-α or IL‑6 (many cytokines were below detection) and conclude the intervention did not buffer immune changes; meta-analytic citations in the paper likewise report mixed or null effects (no consistent increase in IL‑6, and mindfulness more often linked to reductions or null effects). Thus the sources do not support the claim that practicing mindfulness increases vigilance and thereby raises TNF‑α and IL‑6.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://teams.semel.ucla.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Mindfulness meditation and the immune system.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review of 20 randomized trials reports either reductions or null effects for proinflammatory markers (including IL-6 and TNF-α) following mindfulness interventions and explicitly concludes mindfulness is associated with reductions in proinflammatory processes (or mixed/ tentative null findings), not increases. The review provides no evidence that mindfulness practice raises TNF‑α or IL‑6 via heightened vigilance/self‑awareness.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness increases self-awareness, which can lead individuals to become more vigilant about potential bodily threats. This heightened vigilance may actually stimulate the immune system, resulting in increased levels of proinflammatory markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, as the body remains on high alert for possible stressors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'jud