t changes after interventions. It does not measure \\"daily hassles\\" or the frequency/occurrence of everyday stressful events, nor does it provide evidence that practicing mindfulness (as measured by FFMQ) causally reduces the number of daily hassles or that mindfulness leads people to avoid hassle‑causing situations. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship or the proposed mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness, as measured by the FFMQ, directly reduces the number of daily hassles an individual experiences. This is because mindfulness techniques help people avoid situations that would otherwise lead to hassles, thereby decreasing their actual occurrence.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness, as measured by the FFMQ, directly reduces the number of daily hassles an individual experiences. This is because mindfulness techniques help people avoid situations that would otherwise lead to hassles, thereby decreasing their actual occurrence.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

