otional well-being.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) exerts a negative influence on loneliness (UCLS) primarily because individuals with higher levels of perceived social support are more likely to engage in frequent social activities, which leads to increased social obligations and, paradoxically, less time spent in introspection. This reduction in self-reflection directly diminishes the subjective experience of loneliness (UCLS), as individuals become distracted from recognizing feelings of isolation. Thus, the inverse relationship between perceived social support (MSPSS) and loneliness (UCLS) is driven by the displacement of introspective processes rather than any direct effect of social support on emotional well-being.', 'perplexity': 1.4090002117588092, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8155938334994569, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01538897804949971, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005002949804000342, 'prob_variance': 0.07812071622081258, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0665909514197354, 'avg_nll': 0.34288038320667324}

