   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.24598686678998596, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26555089/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803029/', 'https://extension.unh.edu/blog/2022/05/prolonged-social-isolation-loneliness-are-equivalent-smoking-15-cigarettes-day', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanepe/article/PIIS2666-7762(21)00288-X/fulltext', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/230687896_Loneliness_and_Smoking_The_Costs_of_the_Desire_to_Reconnect'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7429734977129128, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26555089/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The systematic review documents inconsistent associations between loneliness and smoking and calls for longitudinal work and investigation of mediators/moderators, supporting the point that a direct causal link has not been established. However, the review does not provide strong evidence that observed associations are specifically \\"likely due\\" to mediators such as stress or depressive symptoms — it only recommends exploring mediators generally. Therefore the claim that there is no established direct causal mechanism is supported, but the stronger assertion attributing associations to particular mediators is not directly supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803029/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review reports mixed, mostly cross-sectional evidence (about half the studies find associations), notes the inability to infer causality from the available studies, and explicitly raises depression (and other negative affect/stress) as a possible mediator — even noting that in studies adjusting for depression loneliness often ceases to be a significant correlate of smoking. However, the review does not demonstrate that loneliness definitively does not directly cause smoking; it emphasizes insufficient longitudinal/causal evidence and the plausibility of mediation but stops short of proving mediation or ruling out direct effects. Therefore the explanation\'s core claim that there is no established direct causal mechanism and that observed associations are likely mediated by factors like depression is supported in spirit by the citation, but the stronger categorical statement that loneliness \\"does not directly cause\\" smoking is not fully supported by the evidence presented.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://extension.unh.edu/blog/2022/05/prolonged-social-isolation-loneliness-are-equivalent-smoking-15-cigarettes-day", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses health risks of social isolation and loneliness (including statements that these risks are \\"equivalent to smoking 15 cigarettes a day\\") and mentions some changes in coping behaviors after the pandemic, but it does not address whether loneliness directly causes smoking behavior or whether any observed association is mediated by other factors. It therefore does not provide evidence for the claim that loneliness does not directly cause smoking or that any association is mediated by other variables."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/230687896_Loneliness_and_Smoking_The_Costs_of_the_Desire_to_Reconnect", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited \\"source\\" is an inaccessible ResearchGate page showing an error/access-denied message and contains no usable content or evidence. Because no substantive citation material is provided, there is nothing in the citations that supports the claim that loneliness does not directly cause smoking or that any association is mediated by stress/dep