oes not provide direct evidence about the absence of a causal link between loneliness and mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11914683/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines mindfulness (FFMQ, MAAS), subjective and psychological well‑being, and psychological capital; it does not measure or report results for loneliness (UCLS) nor test causal links between loneliness and mindfulness. Therefore the citation provides no direct evidence for or against a causal (or noncausal) relationship between loneliness and mindfulness or for mediation by stress/rumination. It cannot support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between Loneliness (UCLS) and Mindfulness (FFMQ)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12671-023-02184-7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports strong cross-sectional associations and mediation/moderation findings between loneliness (UCLA-LS) and trait mindfulness (FFMQ) and notes that effects may be mediated (e.g., via rumination/stress). Importantly, the authors explicitly state the cross-sectional design prevents causal inference, which supports caution about claiming a direct unmediated causal link. However, the paper also cites randomized/intervention studies (e.g., Creswell et al., Zhang et al.) showing mindfulness training can reduce loneliness, which provides evidence for a causal effect in at least one direction (mindfulness → reduced loneliness). Thus the sources support the view that direct causality is not established from the cross-sectional study and that mediation is plausible, but they do not fully support a blanket claim of “no direct causal relationship” because experimental evidence exists for a causal effect of mindfulness on loneliness."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/arti