   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.28815809084271543, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23630220/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8014467154346091, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and references document consistent associations between loneliness/perceived social isolation and increased inflammatory markers (notably IL‑6 and other proinflammatory compounds) and describe plausible stress‑related pathways (HPA activation) and animal experiments where social isolation raises proinflammatory cytokines. However, the sources explicitly note the human evidence is largely correlational, that the relationship may be bidirectional (inflammation can increase feelings of social disconnection), and that causal direction and whether reducing loneliness lowers inflammatory markers in humans remain unresolved. Evidence for TNF‑alpha specifically is not clearly established in the provided text. Therefore the claim of a direct positive causal relationship (and that reducing loneliness directly decreases these processes) is not fully supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no usable content or data about loneliness or inflammatory markers, so it provides no evidence for the claimed causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review reports associations between perceived social isolation/loneliness and stress-related biological factors including immune components, but emphasizes that most studies are correlational, small, and under-researched. It does not provide robust causal evidence that loneliness directly raises specific proinflammatory cytokines (TNF-α, IL-6) nor that reducing loneliness directly lowers them. Thus the citation supports a possible link between loneliness and immune changes in general but does not support the strong, specific causal claim made."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper is a cross-sectional study that found higher loneliness was associated with higher CRP but did not find significant associations between loneliness and basal or stimulated cytokines including IL-6 and TNF-α. Because the source reports null results for those specific proinflammatory cytokines and is observational (not demonstrating causation or showing that reducing loneliness lowers these markers), it does not support the claim that loneliness causally increases IL-6 and TNF-α via stress pathways or that reducing loneliness directly decreases them."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23630220/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper reports that lonelier participants showed greater LPS-stimulated production of proinflammatory cytokines (notably IL-6 across studies; TNF-α in Study 1 but not Study 2) when exposed to acute stress, indicating an association between loneliness and a proinflammatory phenotype under stress. However, loneliness was not experimentally manipulated, TNF-α findings are inconsistent across the two studies, the effects were observed in stimulated PBMC responses during acute stress (not baseline systemic levels), and the studies do not demonstrate that reducing loneliness lowers these cytokines. Thus the evidence supports an association (especially for IL-6 under stress) but does not fully support the claim of a direct causal relationship or that reducing loneliness will directly decrease these proinflammatory processes."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through activation of stress-related biological pathways. Social isolation and perceived loneliness trigger physiological stress responses, elevating inflammatory markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing loneliness directly decreases these proinflammatory processes. This is a direct positive causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.357219736374002, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8333302365868627, 'min_prob': 0.08278921643946109, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0033918450006394114, 'prob_variance': 0.08303508531811865, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9080288535825858, 'avg_nll': 0.3054382958012804}

97. Loneliness (UCLS)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived stress (PSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) through a direct causal mechanism. Increased loneliness can heighten an individual's perception of stress due to reduced social support and greater emotional burden. Conversely, lower loneliness leads to lower perceived stress. This direct, positive relationship is supported by established psychological theories and is not fully mediated by other variables in the provided CLD.", 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.3175187426883103, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7161293055312706, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes associations between loneliness and stress and even suggests stress can play a causal or prodromic role in loneliness; it emphasizes correlational evidence and biological factors. It does not provide evidence for the specific causal pathway proposed (loneliness → more unstructured time → rumination on minor inconveniences → increased perceived stress) nor support the claim that loneliness increases perceived stress irrespective of social support or emotional factors. Thus the source does not substantiate the explanation\'s mechanism or directionality. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no content or evidence. Therefore there is no support in the provided citation for the claim that loneliness increases perceived stress via unstructured time and rumination.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides solid evidence that higher loneliness is associated with greater perceived stress (including experimental induction studies and longitudinal associations) and describes mechanisms such as hypervigilance for social threat and negative cognitive biases that can increase stress and rumination. However, the specific mechanistic claim that loneliness produces fewer daily tasks/social obligations and therefore more unstructured time — which in turn permits rumination on minor inconveniences and thereby raises perceived stress \\"irrespective of social support or emotional factors\\" — is not supported by the citation. The paper does not present evidence that reduced daily obligations or increased unstructured time is the primary pathway; rather it emphasizes attentional/cognitive biases and physiological stress processes, and it notes complex relations with social support and mood rather than an unconditional, task/time-driven pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links loneliness to greater perceived stress-related biology and affect (e.g., HPA activation/cortisol, increased negative affect, vigilance to social threat) and discusses rumination/mentalizing as a possible pathway. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed — that loneliness leads to fewer daily tasks/social obligations and more unstructured time which then produces excessive rumination about minor inconveniences. The paper also emphasizes subjective loneliness is distinct from objective isolation (weakly correlated) and highlights emotional/cognitive factors (not \\"irrespective\\" of them) as key mechanisms and targets for intervention. Thus the general positive link to perceived stress is supported, but the particular explanation about lack of social obligations/unstructured time and the \\"irrespective\\" claim are not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) because individuals experiencing higher levels of loneliness tend to have fewer daily tasks and social obligations, leading to more unstructured time. This abundance of free time allows for excessive rumination on minor inconveniences, which amplifies stress perceptions. Therefore, as Loneliness (UCLS) increases, the lack of distraction from social engagements directly increases Perceived stress (PSS), irrespective of social support or emotional factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) because individuals experiencing higher levels of loneliness tend to have fewer daily tasks and social obligations, leading to more unstructured time. This abundance of free time allows for excessive rumination on minor inconveniences, which amplifies stress perceptions. Therefore, as Loneliness (UCLS) increases, the lack of distraction from social engagements directly increases Perceived stress (PSS), irrespective of social support or emotional factors.', 'perplexity': 1.6341963727581665, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7532175211756611, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01551254505443629, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0032207489230201693, 'prob_variance': 0.10081815195836541, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.037224649449462, 'avg_nll': 0.49115116838343825}

