   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.2802690802853798, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5025448/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7102549703623184, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5025448/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports both correlational findings and an experiment (Study 2) that manipulated loneliness and found it affected willingness to help (prosocial behavior) depending on public/private context and gender. The authors thus present evidence of a direct causal effect of loneliness (at least under some conditions), while also noting moderators and possible mediators. That contradicts the claim that no direct causal link has been established; the source provides experimental evidence for direct effects rather than only indirect pathways or confounding.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports experimental evidence (two studies, including a modified public goods task and ERP measures) showing that loneliness priming caused a significant reduction in prosocial choices and identifies neural correlates (N400, P300) linked to that change. Thus the sources support a direct effect of loneliness cues on prosocial behavior, contrary to the claim of no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that loneliness affects cognition, affect, physiology and social motivation through a multi-step \\"loneliness regulatory loop\\" (hypervigilance for social threat, biased expectations, impaired self-regulation) and discusses mediated pathways (e.g., via social cognition, perceived support, depressive symptoms) by which loneliness influences social behavior. It does note an increased desire to reconnect but does not provide evidence of a direct, immediate causal link from loneliness to prosocial actions (e.g., PGG outcomes). Thus the sources support the view that effects on social/prosocial behavior are complex and likely indirect, but they do not definitively rule out any direct causal pathways.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports both correlational and experimental evidence that loneliness influences prosocial behavior: Study 1 found negative correlations (with exceptions), and Study 2 used an experimental loneliness prime that produced causal changes in willingness to help (moderated by public/private situation and gender). While the authors note possible mediators and limitations, they explicitly demonstrate a direct effect of manipulated loneliness on prosocial willingness in certain conditions. This contradicts the claim that there is no established direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study directly tests loneliness priming (measured with the UCLA scale) and finds experimentally reduced prosocial behavior in a public-goods/eco-choice task, plus neural (N400/P300) correlates that the authors interpret as a cognitive/neural mechanism linking loneliness to lower prosociality. Thus the citation provides evidence of a direct causal effect from induced loneliness to reduced prosocial behavior and proposes mediating neural processes, contradicting the claim that no direct causal mechanism exists. The explanation is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) does not directly cause Prosocial behavior (PGG). There is no established direct causal mechanism by which changes in loneliness would immediately or inherently lead to changes in prosocial behavior without mediation by other variables such as perceived social support or depressive symptoms. Any observed association is likely due to indirect pathways or confounding factors, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4155991781299082, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7867877981721032, 'min_prob': 0.11659456483303268, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002133070872773011, 'prob_variance': 0.07855075736401265, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3718254254729008, 'avg_nll': 0.3475528889082654}

