. Therefore the sources support the need for caution about direct causal claims and the relevance of mediators, but do not justify a definitive \\"no direct causal relationship\\" conclusion.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0165032722007066", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence about relationships between depressive symptoms and BMI. Therefore it cannot support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship or that mediation is required."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2451847618300630", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence about relationships between depressive symptoms and BMI. Therefore it does not support the claim that the relationship is non‑direct or mediated by other factors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause body mass index (BMI) or vice versa without a mediating variable. Mechanistically, any potential effect of depressive symptoms on BMI would likely operate through mediators such as physical inactivity, sleep disturbance, or other behavioral factors present in the CLD. Including a direct causal link would overlook these mediating pathways. Therefore, the causal relationship is not direct and should not be included in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5101181364399936, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7805770116958309, 'min_prob': 0.03157712649378607, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0017979735548108893, 'prob_variance': 0.09339059728189052, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0444079947925307, 'avg_nll': 0.4121878838195284}

100. Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived social support (MSPSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased depressive symptoms can causally lead individuals to withdraw socially, perceive less support, or interpret social interactions more negatively, thus reducing perceived social support. Conversely, lower depressive symptoms may enhance perceptions of available support. This is a direct negative causal relationship, not merely a correlation.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2018.02780/full', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274570/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10689368/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that greater perceived social support is associated with lower depressive symptoms (negative correlations and mediation analyses showing social support predicts reduced depression). It does not provide evidence that higher depressive symptoms cause increased perceived social support or that depression is positively associated with perceived support; in fact the associations are in the opposite direction. Therefore the explanation (depression → increased perceived social support) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274570/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study (Roohafza et al., 2014) reports that higher perceived social support is associated with lower odds of depression (i.e., social support is protective) and explicitly notes the cross-sectional design prevents causal inference. It does not provide evidence that increases in depressive symptoms lead to increased perceived social support; if anything, the reported associations point in the opposite direction. Therefore the explanation (depression → increased perceived support / a positive association) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10689368/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal network study reports a unidirectional protective effect of social support on depressive symptoms and explicitly states that no significant edges were found from depressive symptoms to social support. Thus the source does not provide evidence that increased depressive symptoms lead people to reach out and thereby increase perceived social support; it actually contradicts that directional claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2018.02780/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher perceived social support (from family and friends) is associated with lower depressive symptoms and models social support as protective against depression; it does not provide evidence that increased depressive symptoms lead people to receive or perceive more social support. While the paper briefly cites ideas (e.g., stress-mobilizing hypothesis, possible spurious positive links) and notes some mixed findings in the literature, its empirical results and interpretation contradict the asserted directional mechanism (depression → increased perceived social support). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study (Acoba, 2024) reports that greater perceived social support is associated with lower depression (a negative association) and frames social support as a predictor/reducer of perceived stress and depression. It does not provide evidence that increased depressive symptoms lead people to receive or perceive more social support; in fact the observed correlations and mediation results point in the opposite direction. The study is cross-sectional and does not support the causal mechanism claimed (depression → more outreach → higher perceived support). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms can lead individuals to reach out more to friends and family, which in turn enhances their perception of social support. As people experience more depressive symptoms, their social networks typically become more attentive and supportive, resulting in a positive association between depressive symptoms and perceived social support.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms can lead individuals to reach out more to friends and family, which in turn enhances their perception of social support. As people experience more depressive symptoms, their social networks typically become more attentive and supportive, resulting in a positive association between depressive symptoms and perceived social support.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

101. Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)-[POSITIVE]->Smoking (SMQ)
   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.26704765069531644, 'citations': ['https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2927523', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5157710/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21059689/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7518154/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5296249/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7219812368824456, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2927523", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a positive association between depressive symptoms and smoking (current and former smokers had higher depression scores; depressive symptoms predicted smoking status in adjusted models). However, the primary analyses are cross-sectional and the authors explicitly state causal conclusions cannot be drawn; the prospective component was non-randomized and also does not establish a direct causal path. The paper also used the BDI-II (and HADS), not the IDS-SR named in the claim, and discusses unmeasured confounding/mediation and measurement limitations. Thus the citations support a positive association but do not support the asserted direct causal relationship or the claim that the effect is not mediated by other variables.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5157710/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review summarizes many longitudinal studies showing that baseline depressive symptoms are often associated with later smoking behavior (supporting the self‑medication hypothesis), so there is empirical evidence for depression → smoking associations. However the review explicitly emphasizes substantial heterogeneity, inconsistent findings, potential confounding/shared vulnerability, and that longitudinal studies alone cannot provide strong causal inference — it calls for methods (e.g., Mendelian randomization) to test causality. The citation therefore supports an association and plausible directional evidence but does not establish a direct, unmediated causal relationship as claimed.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7518154/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies document consistent longitudinal associations where elevated depressive symptoms predict smoking initiation, escalation, and nicotine dependence, and a meta-analysis reports a pooled effect of depression on smoking risk. They also describe plausible mechanisms (peer selection, diminished alternative reinforcers, reward expectations) and note that some effects are mediated and that bidirectional and confounding influences exist. Thus the sources support that depressive symptoms increase risk of later smoking (i.e., a causal risk factor in many studies) but do not unequivocally establish a purely direct, unmediated causal link — the literature emphasizes mediation pathways, heterogeneity, and some reciprocal effects.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5296249/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review provides strong evidence that depressive symptoms are associated with greater smoking, heavier smoking, and lower cessation success, and it cites experimental and longitudinal studies showing depressive/negative affective states can provoke smoking. However, the paper explicitly emphasizes psychological mechanisms (low positive affect, high negative affect, cognitive impairment, and expectancies/goal-directed processes) by which depression primes tobacco use and argues for mediated pathways (incentive learning), not an unmediated direct causal link. It does not support the claim that the effect is direct and \\"not fully mediated\\" by other variables."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21059689/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports cross-sectional associations between depressive symptom dimensions and various smoking dependence motives (e.g., smoking for affect regulation), which supports a link and plausible mechanism by which depressive symptoms relate to smoking. However, it does not establish a direct causal effect of depressive symptoms on smoking behavior, nor does it test whether the relationship is independent of mediation by other variables or whether changes in depressive symptoms lead to changes in smoking. Therefore the evidence supports an association and possible motive-based pathway but not the strong direct-causation claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause smoking (SMQ) in a direct causal relationship. Increased depressive symptoms can lead individuals to smoke more as a maladaptive coping strategy, while decreased depressive symptoms can reduce the likelihood of smoking. This relationship is not fully mediated by other variables in the CLD, so it should be represented as direct.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.387343229901713, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7907989827117327, 'min_prob': 0.09533209319728583, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004756101209689392, 'prob_variance': 0.07131444774188773, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0062234765958211, 'avg_nll': 0.327390572796664}

