   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.26003823266073756, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6658985/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0896627320304311', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2023.1130989/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3846682/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7954739658353207, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6658985/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review and referenced studies describe complex, often bidirectional links between inflammation and depression but emphasize inflammation (proinflammatory cytokines, immune activation, BBB changes, stress‑related immune priming) as a driver of depressive symptoms rather than evidence that depressive symptoms directly cause anti‑inflammatory IL‑10 changes. The paper documents mixed immune activation/suppression in depression and highlights mediating pathways (proinflammatory processes, stress/HPA axis, immune cell changes). It also reports that interventions (e.g., antidepressants, minocycline) alter IL‑10, which are treatment effects rather than evidence that depression per se directly causes IL‑10 changes. Thus the citations support withholding a direct causal arrow from depressive symptoms to IL‑10 and treating associations as likely mediated by other immune/stress mechanisms.\\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3846682/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes a complex, bidirectional relationship between depression and immune markers: it emphasizes mediating roles for stress, proinflammatory cytokines, gut permeability, lifestyle and other factors, and explicitly discusses altered IL-10 levels in stress and antidepressant contexts. That supports the view that associations are mediated and multifactorial. However, the paper also states depression is accompanied by activation of the compensatory anti‑inflammatory reflex system and reports IL‑10 changes (e.g., lower IL‑10 with stress-induced distress; SSRIs increase IL‑10), so it does not clearly endorse the categorical absence of any direct causal pathway from depressive symptoms to IL‑10. Therefore the “no direct causal link” claim is only partially supported by these citations. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0896627320304311", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content about depressive symptoms or IL-10, so it provides no evidence to support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2023.1130989/full", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review describes associations between depression and cytokine alterations (including reduced anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10) but repeatedly emphasizes that the evidence is observational and does not establish direct causation. It outlines mechanistic pathways in which chronic stress, HPA-axis dysregulation, DAMP–PRR signaling and proinflammatory processes drive immune changes, supporting the view that any link between depressive symptoms and IL-10 is mediated by stress- and inflammation-related mechanisms rather than a direct causal effect of symptoms on IL-10."}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship where 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)' causes 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)'. Any associatio