   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause physical inactivity (SBQ) in a direct causal relationship. Increased depressive symptoms can lead to loss of motivation, energy, and interest, resulting in more physical inactivity. Conversely, a decrease in depressive symptoms can lead to greater physical activity. This relationship is not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12236455/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-024-02925-9', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1598936/full', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Major_depressive_disorder'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12236455/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal study reports that higher depressive symptoms predict lower physical exercise (negative association with exercise, e.g., r = −0.131, β = −0.07), i.e., greater depression is associated with more inactivity — the opposite of the explanation offered. The study also uses SDS for depression and PARS-3 for exercise (not IDS-SR or SBQ), so both the direction and the measures described in the explanation are inconsistent with the cited evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-024-02925-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and its referenced literature report that depression (and familial risk for depression) is associated with reduced physical activity (lower daily acceleration and less MVPA) and that higher physical activity is protective against depression. Nothing in the citation supports the claim that increased depressive symptoms cause greater restlessness and more physical activity; in fact the empirical findings are the opposite (reduced activity). \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1598936/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal study reports that higher depressive symptoms predict lower subsequent physical exercise (cross-lag βs negative; depressive symptoms and physical exercise are negatively correlated), i.e. more depression → less activity (more inactivity). That directly contradicts the explanation\'s claim that increased depressive symptoms lead to greater restlessness and more physical activity. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Major_depressive_disorder", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes major depression as typically involving low energy, loss of interest, withdrawal and reduced activity; it only notes that some people may show psychomotor agitation. It does not provide evidence that increased depressive symptoms commonly cause people to become more physically active as a coping strategy or that depression is generally associated with decreased physical inactivity. The explanation\'s claim of a prevalent negative association is not supported by the cited source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms often lead individuals to become more restless and agitated, prompting them to engage in more physical activities as a means to cope with their mood. Therefore, higher depressive symptoms would actually decrease physical inactivity, resulting in a negative association between the two.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms often lead individuals to become more restless and agitated, prompting them to engage in more physical activities as a means to cope with their mood. Therefore, higher depressive symptoms would actually decrease physical inactivity, resulting in a negative association between the two.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

