   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes perceived stress (PSS) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Poor sleep quality or disturbances impair emotional regulation, making individuals more susceptible to stress. Conversely, improving sleep quality can directly reduce perceived stress. This relationship does not require mediation by other variables in the provided CLD.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://jcsm.aasm.org/doi/10.5664/jcsm.6050', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/', 'https://jcsm.aasm.org/doi/pdf/10.5664/jcsm.6050', 'https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi', 'https://www.psychiatry.pitt.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/PSQI%20Article.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited PSQI validation study reports positive correlations between PSQI scores and the Perceived Stress Scale (PSQI global correlated with PSS, r ≈ 0.33 overall; PSQI components r = 0.11–0.35), and explicitly states the PSQI “substantially overlaps” with perceived stress. It provides no evidence that greater sleep disturbance is associated with lower perceived stress or the proposed mechanisms (more waking hours to resolve worries or distraction). Therefore the explanation (a negative relationship) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source only describes the PSQI instrument, its scoring, validity, and uses; it contains no evidence or data about how PSQI-measured sleep disturbance relates to perceived stress (PSS) or that greater sleep disturbance reduces perceived stress. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'It is possible that experiencing greater sleep disturbance, as measured by the PSQI, actually reduces perceived stress by providing individuals with more waking hours to process and resolve their worries, ultimately lowering their PSS scores. Additionally, disrupted sleep patterns might distract individuals from stressful thoughts, paradoxically leading to a decrease in perceived stress.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'It is possible that experiencing greater sleep disturbance, as measured by the PSQI, actually reduces perceived stress by providing individuals with more waking hours to process and resolve their worries, ultimately lowering their PSS scores. Additionally, disrupted sleep patterns might distract individuals from stressful thoughts, paradoxically leading to a decrease in perceived stress.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

