laim that smoking inhibits proinflammatory processes (TNF-α, IL-6) is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://bjbabs.org/index.php/bjbabs/article/view/108", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper\'s title and abstract report that cigarette smoking is associated with increased (not decreased) plasma levels of IL-6 and TNF-α in smokers versus non-smokers. That directly contradicts the explanation that smoking suppresses proinflammatory cytokine production.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Smoking has been shown to suppress the immune system and reduce the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, smoking actually inhibits proinflammatory processes rather than promoting them.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Smoking has been shown to suppress the immune system and reduce the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, smoking actually inhibits proinflammatory processes rather than promoting them.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

