   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.2589844423741745, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37070163/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10960128/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7914679769501171, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37070163/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports associations between IL-1α, IL-6, TNF-α and depressive/psychological symptoms (with sex-specific patterns). It does not examine prosocial behavior (PGG), social support, nor causal pathways between prosocial behavior and cytokines. Therefore it cannot support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between prosocial behavior and proinflammatory markers or that any association is mediated by stress/social support/mental health."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis examines IL-6, TNF, and IL-1β levels in older adults with comorbidities versus controls and discusses inflammaging and stress-related mechanisms, but it does not examine prosocial behavior (PGG), social support interventions, or any direct/causal links between prosocial actions and these cytokines. Therefore it does not provide evidence for or against a direct causal relationship between prosocial behavior and TNF/IL‑6 as claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines IL-6 and TNF-α in the context of obesity, adipose tissue, CRP