   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.2613493671249441, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/', 'https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/psychology_articles/433/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4444832/', 'https://www.jahonline.org/article/S1054-139X(24)00600-1/fulltext', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7645188291624735, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Endotext chapter documents social determinants of obesity and plausible mediating mechanisms (e.g., physical inactivity, stress, perceived social status, food insecurity) that can link social/environmental factors to BMI. That supports the explanation’s idea that social associations with BMI may be mediated by factors like inactivity or stress. However, the source says nothing about prosocial behavior (e.g., PGG measures) or about whether prosociality directly or inversely causes changes in BMI (or whether BMI affects prosociality). It therefore does not support the specific null claim that prosocial behavior has no direct causal effect on BMI (nor the reciprocal)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/psychology_articles/433/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study reports that childhood prosocial behavior was largely unrelated to BMI z-scores and obesity risk through age 17, which supports the claim that prosocial behavior shows no observed effect on BMI in this sample. However, the paper does not demonstrate the absolute absence of any causal mechanism, does not test or rule out reverse causation (BMI → prosocial behavior), and does not provide direct evidence that any observed associations would be mediated specifically by physical inactivity, stress, or depressive symptoms. Therefore the null-association claim is supported, but the broader causal and mediation assertions are not established by the citation alone."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4444832/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review thoroughly describes cognitive, neural and motivational mechanisms of prosocial behavior but says nothing about body mass index or physiological effects on weight. It does not provide evidence that prosocial behavior has no causal effect on BMI nor discuss mediators like physical inactivity, stress, or depression in relation to BMI. Therefore the claim of no causal link (and the proposed mediators) is not supported by the provided source(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what prosocial behavior is and documents social and mental-health correlates (e.g., wellbeing, mood, volunteering benefits) but contains no information about body mass index, obesity, or causal links between prosocial behavior and BMI or mediators such as physical inactivity, stress, or depressive symptoms. Therefore the provided source does not support the assertion that there is no causal relationship or that any association would be mediated by those specific variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) does not cause body mass index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which prosocial behavior would alter BMI, nor does BMI causally influence prosocial behavior. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as physical inactivity, stress, or depressive symptoms, which are already included in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Parti