 not support the claim that prosocial behavior has no causal effect on daily hassles. The primary paper is a cross-sectional study about prosocial behavior and well‑being/affect (it does not address daily hassles), and other referenced works (e.g., Raposa et al. 2016; Snippe et al. 2018; Alden & Trew 2013) report prosocial acts are associated with reduced negative affect or mitigate everyday stress/negative effects. None of the citations provide evidence that there is no direct causal link; some even suggest prosocial behavior can lessen stress-related outcomes.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes multiple ways prosocial behavior affects mood, well‑being, social support and stress buffering (e.g., \\"feel good–do good,\\" volunteering benefits, helper therapy, effects on mental health and perceived support). It therefore provides evidence of mechanisms and associations that could influence daily stress or the perception of hassles. It does not state that prosocial behavior has no direct causal effect on daily hassles, and in fact suggests pathways by which prosocial actions could alter stress/hassles. Thus the explanation claiming \\"NONE\\" (no direct causal link) is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) does not cause daily hassles (HS) to change in any direct causal manner. There is no established mechanism by which engaging in prosocial behavior would directly alter the frequency or intensity of daily hassles experienced. Any association is likely mediated through other variables such as perceived stress or social support, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3082013941630497, 'mean_token_prob': 0.83827