ctional and reports associations between perceived stress, eating/physical-activity behaviors, and BMI, and explicitly calls for longitudinal research to clarify causal directions. That supports the point that a direct causal effect of BMI on perceived stress has not been established and that behavioral mediators are plausible. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence that BMI cannot causally influence perceived stress (e.g., via stigma or health effects), nor does it test mediation in the BMI→stress direction. Thus the citation supports the caution about causal inference but does not fully substantiate the stronger claim that no direct causal link exists."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s42003-023-05396-8", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper reports cross-sectional associations between higher BMI and greater stress-induced negative affect and altered neural/endocrine responses (notably in females), finds cytokines do not explain the brain–BMI link, and explicitly notes the study is cross-sectional so causal claims cannot be made. That evidence supports the point that observed BMI–stress associations may reflect mediating endocrine or neural processes and that a direct causal mechanism has not been established. However, the paper also documents robust associations of BMI with subjective stress reactivity, so it does not provide evidence that a direct causal effect is absent — only that causality is unproven and mediation is plausible. Therefore the explanation is only partially supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) does not directly cause perceived stress (PSS) to change. There is no established direct causal mechanism by which BMI alters perceived stress independently of mediating factors such as physical health, social stigma, or psychological wellbeing