osed heat-production explanation. Thus the association is supported, but the causal claim and specific thermal mechanism are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2645252/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides extensive evidence that higher BMI (especially central adiposity) is associated with worse sleep (notably higher prevalence and severity of obstructive sleep apnea, more arousals, reduced deep sleep, and poorer sleep architecture) and discusses mechanical, neural, inflammatory and adipokine-mediated mechanisms. However, it does not present evidence that increased BMI produces greater body heat during sleep or that raised core body temperature is the causal pathway disrupting restorative sleep as described in the explanation. Thus the positive BMI → more sleep disturbance link is supported, but the specific thermoregulatory mechanism is not supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) to increase because higher BMI leads to greater body heat production during sleep, which raises core body temperature and disrupts the body’s ability to enter restorative sleep stages. As a result, individuals with elevated BMI experience more frequent awakenings and reduced sleep quality. Conversely, lower BMI is associated with cooler body temperatures at night, facilitating deeper and less disturbed sleep, thus directly reducing sleep disturbance (PSQI).', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) to increase because higher BMI leads to greater body heat production during sleep, which raises core body temperature and disrupts the body’s ability to enter restorative 