   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.24341805873394312, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4015473/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2020.00481/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8224297075742072, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports that IL‑6 and TNF‑α are secreted by adipose tissue and shows higher serum TNF‑α (and higher IL‑6 in some subgroups) and positive correlations with BMI in obese versus normal‑weight subjects, supporting an association and biological plausibility. However the study is observational/cross‑sectional and the authors explicitly note it is difficult to unambiguously determine whether adiposity is cause or consequence for TNF; no direct interventional or longitudinal evidence of BMI reduction causing decreases in these cytokines is provided. Thus the citations support an association and mechanism but do not fully establish the stated direct causal claim (including the converse that reducing BMI will decrease the markers).  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4015473/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explicitly states that excess adipose tissue (obesity) is associated with chronic low-grade inflammation and that white adipose tissue produces and secretes proinflammatory cytokines including TNF-α and IL-6; it also notes circulating TNF-α/IL-6 levels are increased with obesity and decrease with weight loss. Although the paper\'s primary experiments examine cytokine effects