   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not cause daily hassles (HS) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which inflammatory cytokine levels would increase or decrease the number or occurrence of daily hassles experienced. The direction of causality would instead plausibly run from daily hassles to proinflammatory processes, not the reverse.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejn.15992', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2018.00586/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper documents elevated IL-6 and TNF-α in obesity and their metabolic/inflammatory roles (correlations with BMI, CRP, insulin resistance). It contains no data or discussion linking these cytokine levels to increased sensitivity to minor stressors, perception of daily hassles, or changes in everyday stress reactivity. Therefore the source does not support the claimed causal link to daily hassles."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis analyzes peripheral IL-6, TNF, and IL-1β levels in older adults and their associations with age‑related diseases. It does not assess daily hassles, subjective sensitivity to minor stressors, or behavioral/psychological reactivity to everyday problems, nor does it provide evidence that higher cytokine levels causally increase the experience or reporting of daily hassles. At best the paper mentions stress‑related theories in the context of inflammaging, but it contains no empirical data linking IL‑6 or TNF to increased frequency or sensitivity to daily hassles. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2018.00586/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that IL-6 and TNF-α are elevated in aging, describes how proinflammatory cytokines, inflammasomes and NF-κB drive physical and medical outcomes (frailty, atherosclerosis, neurodegeneration, etc.), and notes that stress can trigger inflammation. It does not provide evidence that elevated TNF-α or IL-6 causally increase a person’s psychological sensitivity to minor stressors or lead to experiencing more everyday “daily hassles.” In short, the sources support inflammation’s role in physiological and disease processes and that stress can provoke inflammation, but they do not support the claimed direction that proinflammatory cytokines biologically prime people to notice or react negatively to everyday minor stressors (i.e., produce more daily hassles)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper shows that proinflammatory cytokines (including IL‑6 and TNF‑α) alter neuroendocrine activity, monoamine systems and produce sickness‑like behaviors and anxiety in experimental models, and it discusses links to affective disturbance in humans. However, it does not provide direct evidence that higher IL‑6 or TNF‑α causally increase sensitivity to minor everyday stressors or raise the frequency/intensity of \\"daily hassles\\" in humans. The support is therefore inferential (changes in mood/stress biology) rather than direct evidence of increased daily‑hassle perception or reactivity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Recent theories suggest that higher levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 directly increase an individual's sensitivity to minor stressors, causing them to experience more daily hassles. This means that inflammation biologically primes people to notice and react negatively to everyday problems.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Recent theories suggest that higher levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 directly increase an individual's sensitivity to minor stressors, causing them to experience more daily hassles. This means that inflammation biologically primes people to notice and react negatively to everyday problems.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

