   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.2139833067971082, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028709/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/311534896_Multidimensional_Scale_of_Perceived_Social_Support_MSPSS_-_Scale_Items_and_Scoring_Information', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4074419/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.843739158478722, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028709/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study (Yang et al., 2014) examines perceived social support/strain as a predictor of later inflammation and documents plausible psychosocial → inflammatory pathways (HPA/SNS modulation). It does not present evidence that proinflammatory cytokines (e.g., IL‑6, TNF‑α) directly change perceptions of social support, and it even notes limitations regarding reverse causation and inability to assess biomarker change over time. However, the paper does not explicitly demonstrate or review evidence that there is no direct biological mechanism by which inflammation could alter perceived social support. Thus the citation supports the study\'s primary direction (social → inflammation) and the absence of evidence for inflammation → social support in that dataset, but it does not definitively establish that a direct causal pathway from cytokines to perceived social support does not exist."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4074419/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MSPSS validation study focuses on measurement of perceived social support and its association with antenatal depression and intimate partner violence; it does not address proinflammatory markers (TNF‑alpha, IL‑6) or biological causal pathways. The source therefore provides no evidence for the claim that proinflammatory cytokines do not directly cause changes in perceived social support (and its cross‑sectional design limits causal inferences generally)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/311534896_Multidimensional_Scale_of_Perceived_Social_Support_MSPSS_-_Scale_Items_and_Scoring_Information", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate page about the MSPSS scale (temporarily unavailable) and does not provide evidence about proinflammatory cytokines, causal mechanisms, or links between TNF-α/IL-6 and perceived social support. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal effect.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not directly cause perceived social support (MSPSS) to change. There is no established causal mechanism by which increases or decreases in proinflammatory cytokines would directly alter an individual's perception of social support. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by psychological or behavioral factors, not a direct causal pathway.", 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.196483454888305, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8845308945363639, 'min_prob': 0.08808480104255402, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027526255980675897, 'prob_variance': 0.04578885558782533, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6989506836983856, 'avg_nll': 0.1793868003461587}

191. Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)-[POSITIVE]->Rumination (CERQ-R)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not causally cause Rumination (CERQ-R) to change. There is no established direct causal mechanism by which proinflammatory cytokine levels lead to changes in cognitive rumination, nor vice versa. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables such as depressive symptoms or stress. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/cellular-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fncel.2025.1512591/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-73937-0'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows associations between IL-6 and DMN functional connectivity (in healthy subjects) and between TNF-α and salience-network connectivity (in depressed patients), and it references prior work linking DMN dysfunction to rumination. However, the study did not measure rumination (no CERQ‑R data) and is cross‑sectional, so it does not demonstrate that cytokine increases directly cause greater rumination. Causality and a direct cytokine→rumination link (especially measured by CERQ‑R) are not established in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that IL-6, TNF-α and other proinflammatory cytokines alter neuroendocrine function, central monoamines, and produce affective/sickness behaviors (anxiety, anhedonia) and thus supports a general pathway by which inflammation can influence brain function and behavior. However, it does not provide direct evidence that these cytokines specifically increase \\"rumination\\" or that increases are measured on the CERQ‑R (no measures of repetitive thought or CERQ‑R are reported). The claim of direct stimulation of neural circuits for repetitive thought and a measured positive association with CERQ‑R is not demonstrated in the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-73937-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article investigates TNF-α, IL-6 and TGF-β in schizophrenia and bipolar patients and relates cytokine levels to broad clinical symptom domains (e.g., BPRS affect, psychosis) but does not measure rumination or use the CERQ-R. It explicitly reports no consistent relationships between TNF-α or IL-6 and clinical characteristics in this sample and does not provide evidence that these proinflammatory cytokines directly stimulate neural circuits that produce repetitive ruminative thought. Therefore the provided citation does not support the specific claim linking TNF-α/IL-6 to increased rumination (CERQ-R)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/cellular-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fncel.2025.1512591/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper demonstrates that TNF-α and IL-6 can alter electrophysiological properties and gene expression in human iPSC-derived neuron–astrocyte cultures and discusses potential links to cognitive impairment, but it provides no data on human behavior, repetitive thought, rumination, or CERQ‑R scores. It is an in vitro mechanistic study and does not measure or establish that increased cytokine levels cause greater rumination in humans. Thus the specific positive relationship to rumination as measured by the CERQ‑R is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Elevated proinflammatory processes, such as increased levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, directly stimulate neural circuits involved in repetitive thought, which leads to greater rumination as measured by the CERQ-R. As inflammation rises, individuals are therefore more likely to engage in ruminative thinking patterns due to the direct action of these cytokines on the brain.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Elevated proinflammatory processes, such as increased levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, directly stimulate neural circuits involved in repetitive thought, which leads to greater rumination as measured by the CERQ-R. As inflammation rises, individuals are therefore more likely to engage in ruminative thinking patterns due to the direct action of these cytokines on the brain.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

192. Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)-[POSITIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) cause depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Elevated levels of these proinflammatory cytokines can induce neurobiological changes that increase the likelihood or severity of depressive symptoms. Decreasing proinflammatory processes can correspondingly reduce depressive symptoms. This relationship is supported by mechanistic evidence linking inflammation to changes in mood and behavior.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.24542110010760457, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/', 'https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2680424/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8105082013283982, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies robustly document associations and mechanistic routes whereby elevated proinflammatory cytokines (including IL‑6 and TNF‑α) can produce or accompany depressive symptoms, and they also show that lifestyle factors (obesity, poor sleep, etc.) elevate inflammatory markers. However, the specific causal chain asserted in the explanation — that depressive symptoms lead to increased IL‑6 and TNF‑α via depressed persons\' reduced activity/poorer diet (and that treating depression will therefore directly lower these cytokines) — is not directly demonstrated in the cited material. The citations emphasize inflammation → depression, and note shared risk factors and bidirectional links, but provide limited direct evidence that depression-driven behavioral changes reliably raise IL‑6/TNF‑α or that treating depression reliably reduces those cytokines. Thus the explanation is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2680424/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (Miller et al., 2009) provides strong evidence of a positive association between proinflammatory cytokines (including IL‑6 and TNF‑alpha) and depressive symptoms and documents that cytokine administration can induce depressive-like symptoms. It also discusses psychosocial stress, sleep disturbance, obesity (adipose-derived IL‑6), and reduced glucocorticoid sensitivity as pathways linking stress/depression with increased inflammation, and cites preliminary evidence that antidepressant and behavioral interventions can reduce inflammatory markers. However, the specific causal claim in the explanation — that depressive symptoms lead to increased TNF‑alpha and IL‑6 primarily via reduced physical activity and poorer diet, and that treating depression will directly lower those cytokines by changing those behaviors — is only partially supported: the review notes these behavioral factors as plausible contributors and reports suggestive findings (e.g., BMI–inflammation links, sleep–IL‑6), but does not provide definitive causal evidence that depressive symptoms per se raise TNF‑alpha/IL‑6 chiefly through reduced activity/poor diet nor that treating depression reliably and directly reduces these cytokines via those behavioral changes. Thus the overall association is supported, but the specific directional/mechanistic explanation is only partly substantiated by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents elevated IL-6 and TNF-α in many depressed patients and explicitly discusses behavioral/lifestyle pathways (reduced physical activity, poor diet, increased adiposity) that can link depression to systemic inflammation; it also notes that exercise and healthier behaviors reduce inflammation. However, the sources emphasize a bidirectional and heterogeneous relationship and do not provide strong, consistent evidence that depressive symptoms causally increase cytokines via these behaviors in all patients, nor clear proof that interventions targeting depression reliably lower TNF-α and IL-6 by changing behavior. Thus the behavioral-mechanism claim is plausible and partly supported by the citations, but the causal/expectation statement about direct decreases in cytokines from treating depression is not fully established in the provided review. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) lead to increased proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) through behavioral and lifestyle changes commonly associated with depression. Individuals experiencing higher levels of depressive symptoms often engage in reduced physical activity and poorer dietary habits, both of which are known to elevate proinflammatory cytokines. Consequently, interventions targeting depressive symptoms are expected to directly decrease levels of proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) by promoting healthier behaviors that reduce systemic inflammation. This perspective emphasizes the role of psychological state as a primary driver of immune modulation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) lead to increased proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) through behavioral and lifestyle changes commonly associated with depression. Individuals experiencing higher levels of depressive symptoms often engage in reduced physical activity and poorer dietary habits, both of which are known to elevate proinflammatory cytokines. Consequently, interventions targeting depressive symptoms are expected to directly decrease levels of proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) by promoting healthier behaviors that reduce systemic inflammation. This perspective emphasizes the role of psychological state as a primary driver of immune modulation.', 'perplexity': 1.294941294910158, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8538027230658207, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03310763331958213, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0030953807079987664, 'prob_variance': 0.060231516378026866, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8500566023388381, 'avg_nll': 0.25846536200826153}

