   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.24501059302538358, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7962638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4176007/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8261673691237807, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper describes roles of IL-6 and TNF-α in obesity-related inflammation and metabolic disturbances; it reports correlations with BMI and CRP and mechanisms relating cytokines to insulin resistance. It does not address smoking behavior, nor provide evidence that proinflammatory cytokines do or do not cause smoking, or that smoking causes the cytokine changes. Therefore the citation does not support the claim about absence of a causal effect from cytokines to smoking (or the directionality asserted)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7962638/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes TNF-α’s inflammatory roles and clinical targeting in autoimmune diseases but contains no data or discussion about smoking behavior, its causes, or whether cytokine elevations can initiate or increase smoking. It does not provide evidence on the causal direction between inflammation and smoking (i.e., that smoking causes inflammation rather than vice versa). Therefore the explanation about no causal effect of proinflammatory cytokines on smoking is not supported by these sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4176007/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IL-6 review describes IL-6 biology, its triggers (infectious agents, tissue injury, DAMPs, TLR signaling, etc.), regulation, and disease roles, but it does not address smoking behavior or provide evidence that proinflammatory cytokines cannot cause smoking. It also does not present data showing the direction of any association with smoking. Therefore the sources do not support the explicit claim that elevated TNF-α/IL-6 do not cause smoking (or that observed associations are due to smoking→inflammation)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not cause Smoking (SMQ). There is no direct causal mechanism by which elevated proinflammatory cytokines would initiate or increase smoking behavior. Any observed association is likely due to smoking causing inflammation, not the reverse.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.284005048749628, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8654143462300509, 'min_prob': 0.03078551526340389, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00412550901235813, 'prob_variance': 0.06003019069465014, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7476766437509466, 'avg_nll': 0.24998413730803576}

194. Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)-[NEGATIVE]->Sleep disturbance (PSQI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) causally increase sleep disturbance (PSQI). Elevated levels of these cytokines disrupt sleep architecture and quality through direct effects on neural and endocrine pathways. Therefore, an increase in proinflammatory processes leads to greater sleep disturbance, while a decrease leads to less sleep disturbance.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0045610', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11472141/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5955790/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19238807/?dopt=Abstract'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0045610", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited PLOS One paper shows TNF-α has somnogenic effects in rodents (exogenous TNF-α increases NREM sleep and delta power; TNF blockade or TNF receptor knockout attenuates sleep rebound and reduces sleep propensity). That supports the idea that TNF-α can promote aspects of sleep physiology. However, the same paper (and cited background within it) also links elevated TNF-α to pathological excessive daytime sleepiness in sleep-disordered states, and the experiments are in mice examining sleep architecture/propensity rather than human subjective sleep disturbance measured by PSQI. IL-6 is only mentioned in passing (as increased with sleep loss) and is not directly shown to reduce sleep disturbance. Thus the evidence partly supports a somnogenic role for TNF-α but does not clearly support the claim that heightened proinflammatory activity reduces sleep disturbance / lowers PSQI in humans."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11472141/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that some proinflammatory mediators (notably IL-1 and TNF-α) have somnogenic effects — e.g., increasing NREM duration and EEG delta power — which could be interpreted as supporting restorative sleep. However, the same sources show the relationship is complex and context-dependent: IL-6 has mixed effects (dose- and timing-dependent) and is often associated with poorer sleep quality or reduced slow-wave sleep when elevated chronically; clinical trials cited show reductions in inflammatory markers accompany improved PSQI. Thus the citations provide partial, mixed support (acute somnogenic actions for certain cytokines but evidence that elevated/chronically increased inflammation relates to worse subjective sleep), so the broad claim that heightened proinflammatory activity will decrease sleep disturbance / lower PSQI is not fully supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5955790/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong animal and mechanistic evidence that TNF-α can be somnogenic (acute/central TNF increases NREMS and delta power; TNF inhibition reduces NREMS). However, the human evidence in the same source indicates that chronic systemic elevations of TNF are associated with disrupted sleep and that reducing excessive TNF in disease often improves sleep. IL-6 is not well supported by the provided citation for a sleep-improving effect, and the human outcome measure cited in the claim (PSQI sleep disturbance) is not shown to reliably decrease with higher proinflammatory activity. Thus the citations support a context-dependent somnogenic role for TNF (acute/central) but contradict a blanket negative relationship between proinflammatory activity and sleep disturbance in humans."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19238807/?dopt=Abstract", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports mixed associations between sleep measures and inflammatory markers (habitual longer sleep linked to higher IL-6, but shorter PSG-measured sleep linked to higher TNF-α) and examines sleep duration/PSG, not PSQI-assessed sleep disturbance or quality. It does not provide evidence that increased TNF-α and IL-6 causally reduce sleep disturbance or improve PSQI scores; the directions are inconsistent and the measures do not match the claimed outcome.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Proinflammatory processes, such as increased levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, are believed to support restorative sleep by enhancing the body's natural healing mechanisms. Therefore, heightened proinflammatory activity would actually decrease sleep disturbance and improve sleep quality, leading to lower PSQI scores.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Proinflammatory processes, such as increased levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, are believed to support restorative sleep by enhancing the body's natural healing mechanisms. Therefore, heightened proinflammatory activity would actually decrease sleep disturbance and improve sleep quality, leading to lower PSQI scores.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

