   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not cause Mindfulness (FFMQ) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increases or decreases in proinflammatory cytokines directly alter mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as stress or mood, rather than a direct causal effect.', 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5037002', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0165178123006212', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9083081/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0306453020305370', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5037002", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows that a brief mindfulness intervention reduced salivary IL‑6 and TNF‑α (i.e., mindfulness can lower inflammation), and it discusses inflammation’s role in depression, but it does not provide evidence that higher levels of IL‑6 or TNF‑α causally impair mindfulness capacity or lower FFMQ scores. The study did not measure mindfulness with the FFMQ, nor test whether cytokines directly inhibit attention/awareness or neural pathways underlying mindfulness. Therefore the provided citation does not support the claimed negative causal relationship or mechanism. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0165178123006212", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is inaccessible (error message) and provides no content or evidence to evaluate the claim. There are no usable citations supporting the proposed negative relationship, so the explanation is not supported by the provided references."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0306453020305370", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is not available (ScienceDirect returned an error), so there is no evidence provided to support the claimed negative relationship between TNF-alpha/IL-6 and FFMQ mindfulness scores. Because the citation contains no accessible data or findings, the explanation is unsupported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9083081/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents links among mindfulness, resting-state brain networks, and the stress biomarker IL-6 (e.g., mindfulness training associated with reduced IL-6 and with rsFC changes; Taren/Creswell findings). It also notes that chronic stress can increase inflammation and affect neurobiology. However, the review does not provide direct evidence that elevated proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6, TNF-α) causally \\"inhibit\\" cognitive processes underlying mindfulness or that higher cytokine levels produce lower FFMQ scores. The mechanistic claim that inflammation directly interferes with attention/awareness neural pathways is speculative in the review and not demonstrated there.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents elevated IL-6 and TNF-α in obesity and describes their roles in systemic inflammation and metabolic effects (e.g., insulin resistance), but it does not provide data or discussion linking these cytokines to mindfulness, FFMQ scores, cognitive processes of attention/awareness, or neural pathway interference. Therefore the specific claim that increased IL-6/TNF-α directly inhibit mindfulness-related cognitive processes and lower FFMQ scores is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 directly inhibit the cognitive processes involved in mindfulness, resulting in lower scores on the FFMQ. This is because inflammation interferes with neural pathways responsible for attention and awareness, making it biologically harder to be mindful.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 directly inhibit the cognitive processes involved in mindfulness, resulting in lower scores on the FFMQ. This is because inflammation interferes with neural pathways responsible for attention and awareness, making it biologically harder to be mindful.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

