   Properties: {'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'prob_std': 0.21287181937037697, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/', 'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-16996-1_6'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7811974315066399, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes IL-10’s anti-inflammatory roles, regulation, and effects on infection and autoimmune neuroinflammation, but it does not address perceived psychological stress or provide evidence that changes in IL-10 do or do not directly cause changes in perceived stress. The sources therefore do not provide support for the claim that IL-10 has no direct causal effect on perceived stress (nor do they document the relevant causal pathway or its absence)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-16996-1_6", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter extensively documents how psychological stress alters immune markers (including cytokine balances) and how proinflammatory cytokines can drive sickness- and depression-like behavior, but it does not provide evidence that anti‑inflammatory mediators such as IL‑10 directly cause changes in perceived stress (PSS). The sources therefore support the direction stress → immune change and note immune → behavior effects for proinflammatory cytokines, but they do not demonstrate a direct causal pathway from IL‑10 to perceived stress (nor do they firmly establish the absence of any such direct effect). That makes the claim that IL‑10 does not directly change perceived stress consistent with, but not conclusively proven by, the citations.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivati