ey do not provide evidence about IL-10 or the absence of a direct causal effect of IL-10 on BMI. Because the sources focus on a different cytokine (IL-6), they do not support the claim about IL-10’s (non)causal relationship with BMI.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents IL-10’s anti‑inflammatory roles and its influences on metabolic pathways (e.g., hepatic insulin resistance, PGC‑1α–linked lipid/carbohydrate metabolism), supporting the idea that IL‑10 acts via inflammation and metabolic mediators rather than as a direct regulator of body weight. However the sources do not report studies directly testing or demonstrating the absence of a causal effect of IL‑10 on BMI, nor do they specifically address BMI changes. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal relationship is not fully established by the citations, though the idea that any association would be mediated is consistent with the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and references describe associations between adipose tissue, inflammatory and anti-inflammatory cytokines (including IL-10), and obesity/BMI, and emphasize that the mechanisms linking inflammation and obesity are complex and not fully understood. They note that lean adipose secretes anti‑inflammatory mediators (e.g., IL‑10) and that many adipokines correlate with BMI, but they do not present evidence that changes in IL‑10 directly and independently cause changes in BMI. Because the sources document associations and uncertainty about mechanisms (supporting the idea that effects are mediated by other processes) but do not conclusively demonstrate the absence of any direct causal effect of IL‑10 on BMI, the claim is only partially supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) do not directly cause body mass index (BMI) to change. There is no clear causal mechanism whereby changes in IL-10 levels would directly and independently alter BMI. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors such as inflammation or metabolic processes, which are already present in the system. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3599358399389674, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8235777808063928, 'min_prob': 0.007568712396913842, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002569402082846714, 'prob_variance': 0.060370064749424905, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9901386317040686, 'avg_nll': 0.3074375221196487}

