incidence of minor everyday stressors) or that IL-10 changes individuals’ behavior or environment in ways that make them less likely to encounter such hassles. The claim’s behavioral/psychosocial consequence (fewer daily hassles because of suppressed physiological stress responses) is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12974-016-0763-8", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents IL-10’s anti-inflammatory roles in the CNS, its cellular sources, signalling and effects on neuroinflammation and neuroprotection, but it does not provide evidence that higher IL-10 levels reduce the frequency of everyday minor stressors (daily hassles) or that IL-10 directly suppresses physiological stress responses in a way that makes people less likely to encounter such hassles. The sources focus on immune/neuroinflammatory mechanisms and disease models, not on behavioral exposure to daily stress or on HPA-axis/psychological stress outcomes linking IL-10 to fewer daily hassles."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased levels of the anti-inflammatory cytokine IL-10 directly reduce the frequency of daily hassles experienced by individuals by suppressing physiological stress responses. This makes people less likely to encounter minor daily stressors, as their bodies are better equipped to maintain homeostasis.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased levels of the anti-inflammatory cytokine IL-10 directly reduce the frequency of daily hassles experienced by individuals by suppressing physiological stress responses. This makes people less likely to encounter minor daily stressors, as their bodies are better equipped to maintain homeostasis.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

