   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) do not cause perceived social support (MSPSS) to change. There is no direct causal pathway whereby changes in IL-10 levels would directly affect an individual's perception of social support. Any association between these variables in research is likely mediated by other factors, such as stress or mood, which are already represented in the CLD.", 'session_id': '5bef252f-f209-4274-9a39-33ae8001759a', 'citations': ['https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/hea-hea0000594.pdf', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016141', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121005985', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8388310/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6158086/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/hea-hea0000594.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis reports correlational links mainly showing that greater social support/social integration is associated with lower levels of inflammatory markers (e.g., IL‑6, CRP) and notes the evidence is small and largely cross‑sectional. It explicitly states only two small studies examined anti‑inflammatory processes (including IL‑10) and both found no association with social support. The review does not provide evidence that higher anti‑inflammatory cytokines (IL‑10) cause increased perceived social support or that anti‑inflammatory signaling enhances social perception in the brain. Causality and the specific IL‑10 → perceived support pathway are not supported by the cited material. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016141", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses bidirectional links between inflammatory processes (largely proinflammatory activity, experimental endotoxin, IL‑6/IL‑1/TNF etc.) and social behavior — e.g., inflammation can heighten neural sensitivity to negative social stimuli or modulate approach to close others, and social isolation is associated with elevated proinflammatory markers. It does not present evidence that anti‑inflammatory processes (specifically IL‑10) causally increase perceived social support or that IL‑10 enhances social‑perceptual brain function leading to greater appreciation of supportive relationships. Thus the claim about IL‑10 → increased perceived social support is not supported by the provided citation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121005985", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited \\"ScienceDirect\\" entry is an error message page with no accessible content or evidence; it provides no data or findings about IL-10, anti-inflammatory processes, or perceived social support and thus cannot support the causal claim described.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8388310/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study measured pro-inflammatory markers (hsCRP, WBC count, fibrinogen), not anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL‑10, and found no association between perceived social support (MSPSS) and inflammation. It is cross‑sectional and does not provide evidence that anti‑inflammatory processes cause greater perceived social support or that anti‑inflammatory cytokines enhance social perception. Therefore the claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6158086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies largely examine effects of acute pro-inflammatory activity (e.g., endotoxin, IL-6) on social perception and approach/withdrawal, and discuss IFN-γ and trials of anti-inflammatory drugs for depression as open questions. They do not provide evidence that higher levels of anti‑inflammatory markers (e.g., IL‑10) causally increase perceived social support (MSPSS) or that anti‑inflammatory cytokines enhance social‑perception brain function in a way that raises perceived support. At best the citations show that inflammation can alter social sensitivity (sometimes increasing desire to affiliate), but they do not support the specific, causal, positive role for IL‑10 on perceived social support claimed here."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes, such as increased IL-10, directly cause individuals to perceive greater social support. This is because anti-inflammatory cytokines enhance brain function related to social perception, making people more likely to notice and appreciate supportive relationships in their environment.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes, such as increased IL-10, directly cause individuals to perceive greater social support. This is because anti-inflammatory cytokines enhance brain function related to social perception, making people more likely to notice and appreciate supportive relationships in their environment.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

