rbance (PSQI) are therefore unsupported by the provided source material."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43440-021-00333-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article measured and reported associations of higher proinflammatory cytokines (IL‑6, IL‑17, IL‑23) with worse PSQI scores in IBD patients. It did not measure IL‑10 or present data showing that IL‑10 levels inversely affect PSQI, nor does it provide evidence for the proposed neuronal mechanism or unidirectional causality from IL‑10 to sleep disturbance. Therefore the specific claim about IL‑10 producing decreased sleep disturbance via stimulation of sleep‑regulating brain circuits is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Elevated IL-10 production directly promotes increased neuronal activity in sleep-regulating brain regions, leading to a reduced need for sleep and thereby decreasing sleep disturbance scores on the PSQI. Conversely, lower IL-10 levels fail to stimulate these neural circuits, resulting in a greater accumulation of sleep need and higher PSQI scores. The directionality remains from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), rather than the reverse.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Elevated IL-10 production directly promotes increased neuronal activity in sleep-regulating brain regions, leading to a reduced need for sleep and thereby decreasing sleep disturbance scores on the PSQI. Conversely, lower IL-10 levels fail to stimulate these neural circuits, resulting in a greater accumulation of sleep ne