   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no known direct causal relationship where daily hassles (HS) cause physical inactivity (SBQ) or vice versa. Any potential association is likely mediated by variables such as perceived stress or depressive symptoms, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, a direct causal link should not be included. ', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3894304/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4128832/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3894304/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review provides substantial evidence that psychological stress — including daily hassles and life-event stress — is often followed by reductions in physical activity and/or increased sedentary behavior. It cites multiple prospective, diary, and some experimental studies showing hassles or exam/acute stress predict decreased exercise (e.g., diary studies, O’Connor et al., Roemmich et al.), and concludes the majority of studies indicate an inverse temporal relationship. However, the review also reports mixed findings, moderators (e.g., habitual exercisers may maintain or increase activity), and methodological limitations; it does not support an unconditional statement of direct causation for all people nor that everyone with frequent hassles will “avoid physical activity altogether.” The user’s wording is stronger and more categorical than the balanced conclusions in the review."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4128832/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article adapts a daily-hassles scale for a low-income sample and analyzes associations between hassles and healthcare-seeking behaviors (delays in care) and self-rated health. It does not measure physical activity or physical inactivity, nor does it provide evidence that hassles cause reductions in exercise. The paper reports cross-sectional associations with healthcare utilization and health hassles, not a causal link to physical inactivity. Therefore the claim that daily hassles directly cause people to become physically inactive is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Experiencing more daily hassles directly causes people to become physically inactive, as the stress from these small stressors drains their motivation and energy to exercise. Consequently, individuals facing frequent daily hassles are much more likely to avoid physical activity altogether.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Experiencing more daily hassles directly causes people to become physically inactive, as the stress from these small stressors drains their motivation and energy to exercise. Consequently, individuals facing frequent daily hassles are much more likely to avoid physical activity altogether.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

