30. Perceived stress (PSS)-[POSITIVE]->Mindfulness (FFMQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes mindfulness (FFMQ) to change in an inverse relationship. Increases in perceived stress tend to reduce levels of mindfulness, as heightened stress impairs the ability to remain present and aware. Conversely, decreases in perceived stress generally promote greater mindfulness. This relationship is direct and does not appear to be mediated by the other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7511255/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6534144/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1498458/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-64183-5'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study’s preferred analyses (RI-CLPM) do not show that higher perceived stress leads to greater mindfulness overall; within-person effects from stress to mindfulness were absent or negative (stress predicted lower perceived helpfulness for some facets), and at the between-person level only one facet (Describe) correlated positively with average stress. Results from the simpler CPLM (which did show stress predicting greater facet use) were judged by the authors as likely biased/spurious and had poor fit. The authors’ overall conclusion is that stress often interferes with staying mindful, not that stress generally enhances FFMQ-measured mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1498458/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports strong negative correlations between perceived stress and mindfulness facets (mindfulness-attention r = −0.45; mindfulness-acceptance r = −0.48) and finds mindfulness-acceptance predicts lower perceived stress (β = −0.25). Both quantitative and qualitative results describe mindfulness as a stress-buffering mechanism (mindfulness reduces/manage stress). The paper provides no evidence that higher perceived stress leads to increased mindfulness; if anything, it shows the opposite direction. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that perceived stress positively increases mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7511255/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents that mindfulness-based interventions (MBSR) tend to increase mindfulness and decrease perceived stress among healthcare professionals — i.e., mindfulness training leads to reduced stress. It reports improvements in mindfulness following intervention and associations between home mindfulness practice and lower stress. It does not provide evidence that higher levels of perceived stress (measured by PSS) naturally cause increases in dispositional mindfulness (FFMQ) without intervention. Therefore the citations do not support the claim that greater perceived stress enhances mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6534144/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited RCT and related references report that mindfulness training reduces perceived stress (and that reductions in perceived stress mediate mindfulness effects on negative affect variability). They describe an inverse association (mindfulness → lower perceived stress) rather than evidence that higher perceived stress produces greater mindfulness. No provided citation supports the claim that increased perceived stress causes or enhances dispositional mindfulness. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-64183-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study tests a mindfulness-based intervention and reports that the intervention increased mindfulness (MAAS) and decreased perceived stress (PSS); change scores for mindfulness and stress are negatively correlated. The paper therefore supports that increasing mindfulness reduces perceived stress, not that higher perceived stress increases mindfulness. It contains no evidence that naturally greater stress causes greater mindfulness or that stress drives increases in mindfulness."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of perceived stress can actually enhance mindfulness because stressful situations force individuals to be more present and attentive to their thoughts and feelings. As people experience more stress, they naturally develop greater awareness of their internal states, which increases their mindfulness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of perceived stress can actually enhance mindfulness because stressful situations force individuals to be more present and attentive to their thoughts and feelings. As people experience more stress, they naturally develop greater awareness of their internal states, which increases their mindfulness.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

31. Perceived stress (PSS)-[POSITIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Higher perceived stress increases the likelihood and severity of depressive symptoms, while lower perceived stress reduces them. This relationship is supported by psychological mechanisms where chronic stress directly impacts mood, cognition, and neurobiology, leading to depression. The effect is direct and not fully mediated by other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.26220103386735066, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7897868/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0165032719324541', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6541026/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7266529535983027, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7897868/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows a positive association between perceived stress and depressive symptoms and that social support (one measured social resource) is negatively associated with both and moderates the stress–depression relationship. However, it is cross‑sectional (so cannot establish causality), did not measure or test \\"social withdrawal\\" as a mediator, and found multiple positive resources (especially optimism) relevant — i.e., it does not support the claim that increased social withdrawal is the primary causal mechanism or that the pathway is direct and uninfluenced by other psychological or biological routes."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines the psychometric properties and longitudinal variance decomposition of the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS). It does not present evidence linking PSS scores to depressive symptoms measured by the IDS‑SR, nor does it test or report social withdrawal as a mediator or the dominance of that pathway. The paper mentions that stable factors (e.g., personality, coping, symptoms of anxiety and depression) might influence PSS scores, but it provides no causal analyses, mediation tests, or data about social engagement/withdrawal. Therefore the specific causal mechanism and mediator claim are not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6541026/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines objectively rated interpersonal stress predicting later depressive symptoms in adolescent girls and finds effects are moderated by affective reactivity and HPA (cortisol) responses. It does not use the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS) or the IDS‑SR, does not test social withdrawal as a mediator, and in fact highlights affective and physiological pathways rather than social isolation as the primary mechanism. Therefore the source does not support the specific causal pathway (PSS → social withdrawal → depressive symptoms) nor the claim that social withdrawal is the primary mediator and other pathways are unimportant."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0165032719324541", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content to support any claims. There is no evidence in the cited material that links perceived stress (PSS) to depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), nor that social withdrawal is the primary mediator or that other pathways are negligible. Because the source is unavailable/unreadable, the explanation is unsupported by the citation(s)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) primarily through the mechanism of increased social withdrawal. Individuals experiencing higher levels of perceived stress tend to isolate themselves from social interactions to manage their stress, and this reduction in social contact directly leads to the development and intensification of depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Lower perceived stress, conversely, promotes greater social engagement, thereby decreasing the likelihood of depressive symptoms. This relationship is direct and not significantly influenced by other psychological or biological pathways, as social withdrawal acts as the primary mediator between PSS and IDS-SR in this context.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) primarily through the mechanism of increased social withdrawal. Individuals experiencing higher levels of perceived stress tend to isolate themselves from social interactions to manage their stress, and this reduction in social contact directly leads to the development and intensification of depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Lower perceived stress, conversely, promotes greater social engagement, thereby decreasing the likelihood of depressive symptoms. This relationship is direct and not significantly influenced by other psychological or biological pathways, as social withdrawal acts as the primary mediator between PSS and IDS-SR in this context.', 'perplexity': 1.3714306018872102, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8053395169474274, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.032386946332494365, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035489276050134837, 'prob_variance': 0.06874938216110758, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.10942315550114, 'avg_nll': 0.3158544299601387}

