34. Perceived stress (PSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Smoking (SMQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes smoking (SMQ) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress can lead individuals to increase smoking as a coping mechanism, while lower stress tends to reduce the urge to smoke. This effect is widely observed in behavioral health research and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4558262/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2939644/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9859085/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5531612/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4876877/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4558262/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a significant inverse association between perceived stress (PSS) and cigarettes per day in men (and a nonsignificant negative trend in women), so it provides empirical evidence of a negative relationship in this sample. However, the authors do not support the specific causal explanation offered (that higher stress increases health awareness and thus leads people to avoid smoking); they instead propose alternate interpretations and note limitations (small sample, cross-sectional design) that preclude causal claims. Therefore the direction is partially supported by the citation but the proposed mechanism and causal inference are not."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2939644/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper explicitly describes stress as a trigger of smoking and relapse and reports either small positive or near-zero correlations between PSS scores and smoking measures (daily cigarette consumption and nicotine dependence). The concurrent-validity results do not show a clear negative relationship (higher perceived stress → less smoking); instead they report little or mixed association and note stress is associated with increased smoking/relapse in prior studies. Therefore the source does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9859085/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper does not show that higher perceived stress reduces smoking. It reports a non-significant direct effect of perceived stress on abstinence and a significant indirect effect in the opposite direction: higher perceived stress was associated with stronger smoking urges (especially urges to relieve negative affect), which were associated with LOWER abstinence (i.e., more smoking). The study thus suggests stress is linked to increased smoking propensity via urges, not to avoidance of smoking as the explanation claims."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5531612/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that higher perceived stress is associated with greater smoking-related problems and stronger expectancies that smoking reduces distress, which in turn relate to poorer cessation outcomes and maintenance of smoking. It presents positive correlations between PSS and smoking-related measures and describes mechanisms by which stress promotes continued smoking — not evidence that greater perceived stress leads to reduced smoking due to increased health awareness. Thus the citation contradicts the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4876877/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports perceived stress is positively associated with smoking-related outcomes (g