   Properties: {'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.29337968224550925, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159103000291', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121000192', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-022-01921-1'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7864973595995834, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes a stage-dependent effect of chronic stress on cytokines: at early stages stress (via HPA/SNS activation) tends to upregulate anti‑inflammatory cytokines such as IL‑10, whereas sustained/prolonged stress (through HPA “fatigue”, glucocorticoid resistance and activation of NF‑κB) is proposed to lead to increased proinflammatory cytokines and a decrease in anti‑inflammatory cytokines. Thus the source supports that prolonged/chronic stress can cause IL‑10 (and other anti‑inflammatory cytokines) to fall via the mechanisms cited, but it also explicitly describes an initial increase in IL‑10 with early stress. The claim as stated (a general negative causal effect of perceived stress on IL‑10) therefore overgeneralizes the review’s conditional, stage‑dependent account.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159103000291", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error message with no accessible content about perceived stress, cortisol, proinflammatory cytokines, or IL-10. There is no evidence in the citation to support the claimed negative causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S088915