   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct neuroendocrine and immune mechanisms. Higher perceived stress activates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and sympathetic nervous system, which in turn promotes the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces activation of these inflammatory pathways.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/', 'https://journals.physiology.org/doi/full/10.1152/ajpregu.00131.2010', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2904148/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8274563/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4065693/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes a stage-dependent effect: early/stage-specific stress responses (via HPA, SNS, vagus) can transiently suppress proinflammatory cytokines, but with sustained/chronic stress glucocorticoid resistance and NF-κB activation lead to increased IL-6 and TNF-α; many human studies report increased proinflammatory markers with chronic stress. The citation does not support the claim that perceived stress generally activates a \\"relaxation response\\" that produces a sustained negative association between perceived stress (PSS) and IL-6/TNF-α. It at best supports a transient early suppression but overall contradicts a generalized negative relationship. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that psychological stress significantly increased production of TNF-alpha and IL-6, and that students with higher stress perception had higher levels of these proinflammatory cytokines. This directly contradicts the claim of a negative association (stress decreasing proinflammatory processes) and does not support the proposed relaxation-response mechanism.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2904148/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article presents experimental evidence that psychological (restraint) stress activates IL-6–expressing hypothalamic neurons and raises IL-6 mRNA and plasma IL-6 levels (i.e., stress increases IL-6). It does not support a stress-induced decrease in proinflammatory cytokines, nor does it provide evidence that stress activates a \\"relaxation response\\" that suppresses TNF-α or IL-6. TNF-α is not demonstrated to decrease in the paper. Thus the citation contradicts the claimed negative association (and addresses IL-6 only, primarily showing an increase)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8274563/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that higher perceived stress is associated with flatter diurnal cortisol slopes which are, in turn, associated with heightened systemic inflammation (including IL‑6 in exploratory analyses) — i.e., a positive association between stress and proinflammatory markers. It also discusses mechanisms (glucocorticoid resistance) by which chronic stress can increase rather than suppress inflammation. The paper therefore contradicts, rather than supports, the claim that greater perceived stress decreases proinflammatory cytokines (TNF‑α, IL‑6) via a relaxation/anti‑inflammatory response."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress is believed to activate the body’s relaxation response, which has been falsely theorize