41. Perceived stress (PSS)-[POSITIVE]->Loneliness (UCLS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) through a direct causal relationship. Increased perceived stress can lead individuals to withdraw socially or feel isolated, thereby increasing loneliness. Conversely, lower perceived stress may help individuals feel more connected and less lonely. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.2583313032695332, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7422698348536332, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review supports a positive association and even a possible co-causal/prodromic role of stress in loneliness (it summarizes decades of studies indicating stress and loneliness are linked and suggests stress treatment may help loneliness). However, the review does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed here — that higher perceived stress leads people to judge others as \\"too busy\\" and to perceive objectively limited social opportunities (a perceived scarcity-of-opportunities pathway rather than behavioral or internal-feeling changes). The review instead highlights biological and broad psychological links and notes most studies are correlational and mechanisms are under-researched."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no accessible content (Cloudflare error), so there is no evidence in the cited source to support the claimed causal pathway from perceived stress to loneliness. Because the citation yields no usable information, the explanation is unsupported by the provided reference.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents strong associations between stress-related biology (HPA activation, inflammation), maladaptive social cognitions (negative interpretations of others, reduced trust), and loneliness, and notes that targeting maladaptive cognitions reduces loneliness. However, it does not provide clear empirical evidence that higher levels of general perceived stress (PSS) causally lead people to conclude others are \\"too busy\\" or that social opportunities are objectively scarce — nor does it establish the proposed unidirectional causal pathway (perceived stress → perceived scarcity of others\' availability → increased loneliness). The review emphasizes bidirectionality and calls for more work on directionality and mechanisms."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) because individuals experiencing higher levels of PSS are more likely to perceive that others are too busy or preoccupied to engage with them, leading them to believe that social opportunities are objectively limited. This perception of a scarcity of available social connections, rather than any change in their own behavior or internal feelings, directly results in higher loneliness (UCLS). Conversely, those with lower PSS are more likely to believe that numerous social opportunities are readily accessible, thereby reducing feelings of loneliness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) because individuals experiencing higher levels of PSS are more likely to perceive that others are too busy or preoccupied to engage with them, leading them to believe that social opportunities are objectively limited. This perception of a scarcity of available social connections, rather than any change in their own behavior or internal feelings, directly results in higher loneliness (UCLS). Conversely, those with lower PSS are more likely to believe that numerous social opportunities are readily accessible, thereby reducing feelings of loneliness.', 'perplexity': 1.3731244286565236, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8101992084750336, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.026591823723580085, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037921036354025086, 'prob_variance': 0.06673506224893552, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9543563053320374, 'avg_nll': 0.3170887480667274}

