   Properties: {'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.2518778261308437, 'citations': ['https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumination_(psychology)', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-87335-7'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7313516997530409, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited psychiatry.org article characterizes rumination as an internal, repetitive cognitive process that contributes to negative emotions, depression, anxiety, and perceived stress — and highlights mediating pathways (e.g., rumination leading to worsened mood or stress). It does not describe rumination as producing external events or daily hassles, but it also does not explicitly demonstrate that rumination cannot directly cause the occurrence of daily hassles. Thus the source is consistent with the explanation’s emphasis on internal mediation but does not definitively prove absence of any direct causal link to daily hassles."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumination_(psychology)", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article supports parts of the explanation — that rumination is an internal cognitive process and that it is associated with depression, anxiety, and stress-reactive rumination. However, the source does not provide evidence that rumination cannot directly cause the occurrence of external daily hassles, nor does it demonstrate that any association is specifically mediated by perceived stress or depressive symptoms. The key causal-negative claim (no direct causal mechanism) and the proposed mediation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-87335-7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited diary study does not provide evidence that rumination causes the occurrence of daily negative events (daily hassles); instead it shows perseverative cognition is associated with co‑rumination and is often triggered by negative events. The authors explicitly state they cannot draw causal conclusions and did not perform lagged analyses to test whether rumination increases subsequent hassles. While the paper discusses theoretical pathways (e.g., perseverative thoughts can prolong or mediate stress) it does not demonstrate a direct causal mechanism from rumination to external daily hassles, so the claim that there is no demonstrated direct causal effect is consistent with the citation — but the citation also raises the possibility that perseverative cognition can generate or sustain stress, so an absolute \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" is not fully established by the source. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) does not cause Daily hassles (HS) directly. Rumination is a cognitive process involving repetitive thinking, while daily hassles are external events or minor stressors. There is no direct causal mechanism by which engaging in rumination would increase or decrease the occurrence of daily hassles. Any association between them is likely mediated by other variables such as perceived stress or depressive symptoms.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3358292821199635, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8144646949527615, 'min_prob': 0.09519972090930263, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0016351144288907192, 'prob_variance': 0.06344243929639953, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9914283935262566, 'avg_nll': 0.28955228410519207}

44. Rumination (CERQ-R)-[POSITIVE]->Mindfulness (FFMQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes Mindfulness (FFMQ) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increased rumination reflects repetitive, passive focus on distress, which directly reduces mindful awareness and presence as measured by FFMQ. Conversely, reduced rumination allows for greater mindfulness. This relationship is supported by psychological mechanisms where high rumination impairs the ability to maintain non-judgmental, present-moment attention.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9737922/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31414836/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/335193478_Thinking_mindfully_How_mindfulness_relates_to_rumination_and_reflection_in_daily_life', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12382274/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9737922/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis and included studies report that mindfulness-based interventions reduce rumination and increase mindfulness (MBIs produce lower rumination and higher FFMQ/MAAS scores). The paper describes mindfulness as a way to interrupt and reduce rumination and to enhance present-moment awareness. It does not provide evidence that greater trait rumination leads to greater mindfulness; if anything, the citations imply an inverse relationship (mindfulness reduces rumination). Therefore the explanation that higher rumination would be associated with increased mindfulness is not supported by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article distinguishes rumination subtypes (brooding vs. reflection) and shows that reflection (measured with the RSQ) predicted greater distress-tolerance and that acting-with-awareness (an FFMQ facet) also predicted distress-tolerance. It does not demonstrate a positive association between overall rumination and mindfulness; reflection did not meaningfully correlate with FFMQ facets in the reported data, and brooding was maladaptive. Also the citation uses an RSQ rumination measure (not CERQ-R). Thus the source does not support the claim that higher rumination is associated with increased mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12382274/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis and related references report that mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (MBCT) increases mindfulness (FFMQ) while decreasing rumination (various rumination scales, including CERQ-related measures). They treat rumination as an maladaptive, repetitive cognitive process that MBCT reduces; no cited evidence shows that higher naturalistic rumination is associated with greater mindfulness. Thus the sources contradict — rather than support — the claim that greater rumination leads to increased mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31414836/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study differentiates rumination from reflection and finds opposing relations with mindfulness facets: the nonjudgmental-acceptance facet of mindfulness is associated with less concurrent rumination, whereas present-moment attention is associated with more concurrent reflection. The paper therefore indicates rumination is negatively (not positively) related to at least one core mindfulness facet and that the positive relation applies to reflection (a different construct), not rumination. This contradicts the claim that higher rumination is associated with increased mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/335193478_Thinking_mindfully_How_mindfulness_relates_to_rumination_and_reflection_in_daily_life", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (ResearchGate page returns an access/connection error) and contains no extractable evidence or data supporting the claimed positive relationship. Because the citation provides no usable content, it does not support the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination encourages individuals to deeply reflect on their thoughts and emotions, which can lead to greater self-awareness and present-moment focus. This repeated introspection naturally enhances mindfulness, as it helps people become more attuned to their inner experiences. Therefore, higher rumination should be associated with increased mindfulness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination encourages individuals to deeply reflect on their thoughts and emotions, which can lead to greater self-awareness and present-moment focus. This repeated introspection naturally enhances mindfulness, as it helps people become more attuned to their inner experiences. Therefore, higher rumination should be associated with increased mindfulness.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

