   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Rumination (CERQ-R) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased rumination reduces an individual's perceived social support by fostering social withdrawal and negative interpretations of social interactions. Conversely, decreased rumination can enhance perceptions of available support. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the listed CLD.", 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.27095647626635955, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5407371/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7741140898749003, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines perceived social support (MSPSS), perceived stress (PSS-10), and mental health outcomes, finding that greater perceived support is associated with lower perceived stress and better mental health. It does not measure or report on rumination (CERQ‑R) nor test relationships in which rumination predicts perceived social support, nor does it provide evidence for the proposed cognitive-mechanism (impaired recognition of support or reduction in actual support). Because the citation contains no data or analyses linking rumination to MSPSS (or describing the mechanism claimed), it does not support the presented claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5407371/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper presents evidence that rumination is associated with poorer social-support outcomes: it cites prior work linki