   Properties: {'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.26590858594154554, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957598/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0149763422000094', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp201690', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8078255820995015, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957598/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review shows mixed but overall modest evidence that rumination (variously measured) influences inflammatory activity and that experimental induction of rumination can increase pro‑inflammatory markers (e.g., CRP, IL‑1β, IL‑6) and alter pro/anti ratios. However, the review does not provide consistent evidence that rumination causally reduces the anti‑inflammatory cytokine IL‑10. Multiple studies that measured IL‑10 found no significant negative association with rumination (e.g., Ysseldyk et al. 2018; Szabo et al. 2019 found no effect on IL‑10 though they found an increased IL‑1β/IL‑10 ratio), and Silveira et al. (2020) reported no IL‑10 association. While the citations support a link between rumination, stress pathways, and changes in inflammatory markers, they do not support the specific causal claim that increased rumination causes IL‑10 to decrease."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review thoroughly describes IL-10’s anti-inflammatory role and molecular/regulatory mechanisms (transcriptional, epigenetic, cytokine signaling, cell sources, polymorphisms), but it does not provide evidence linking psychological rumination (CERQ-R) or chronic psychological stress to decreased IL-10, nor does it describe neuroendocrine (e.g., HPA-axis/cortisol) pathways causing suppression of IL-10 in humans. Because the claim asserts a causal pathway from rumination → stress/neuroendocrine changes → reduced IL-10, and the provided citation does not address rumination or psychological-stress-induced suppression of IL-10, the sources do not support the relationship as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0149763422000094", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no accessible content (a ScienceDirect/CLOUDFLARE error message), so there is no evidence in the cited source to support the claimed negative causal relationship between rumination (CERQ-R) and IL-10. Because the citation is unavailable, the claim cannot be verified from the provided material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp201690", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that psychological stress and stress-related psychopathology are linked to neuroendocrine changes and shifts toward pro-inflammatory signaling (e.g., increased IL‑6, IL‑1, TNFα) via HPA-axis and immune mechanisms, which is consistent with the general mechanism proposed. However, the citation does not provide direct evidence that rumination (as measured by the CERQ‑R) specifically causes reductions in the anti‑inflammatory cytokine IL‑10, nor does it report empirical data linking rumination to IL‑10 decreases. Therefore the claim\'s broad mechanistic plausibility is supported, but the specific causal link (rumination → decreased IL‑10) is not demonstrated in the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease. Chronic rumination increases psychological stress, which can suppress anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 via neuroendocrine and i