utcomes rather than perceived stress, and the primary temporal direction reported is stress → rumination (and rumination → internalizing symptoms). Therefore the citation does not support the claimed causal effect of rumination increasing perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4332541/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited EMA study documents that perceived event stressfulness predicts rumination and that rumination predicts greater negative affect and symptoms after stress (and mediates stress → affect/symptoms). It does not show that rumination causes increases in perceived stress (nor does it use the PSS measure). In other words, the paper treats rumination mainly as a response to perceived stress and as a mediator of stress → outcomes, not as a direct causal driver of higher perceived stress. Therefore the claim that rumination (CERQ-R) directly causes perceived stress (PSS) to increase is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/317025413_The_power_of_stress_Perceived_stress_and_its_relationship_with_rumination_self-concept_clarity_and_resilience", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is an access/error page from ResearchGate and contains no substantive content or data about rumination or perceived stress. Because the provided citation yields no accessible evidence, it cannot support the claimed direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.660062/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article uses the Ruminative Response Scale (brooding/reflection) and experience-sampling to show that brooding predicts higher negative affect at the next sampling occasion and that cognitive-control deficits predict