   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) does not cause Daily hassles (HS) to change. Mindfulness may affect how individuals perceive or respond to hassles, but it does not causally alter the actual occurrence or frequency of daily hassles. There is no direct causal relationship from mindfulness to the number of daily hassles experienced.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/', 'https://online.ucpress.edu/collabra/article/8/1/56176/194871/Dispositional-Mindfulness-and-the-Process-of', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4829487/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review reports that mindfulness (measured with instruments including the FFMQ) and mindfulness training (e.g., MBSR) are associated with reductions in perceived stress and, in at least one RCT (Williams et al., 2001), reductions in \\"daily hassles.\\" It also documents studies where changes in FFMQ-assessed mindfulness mediated reductions in stress and symptoms. However, the review does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed — that mindfulness works by helping people avoid situations that would otherwise produce hassles and thereby reducing the actual number of hassles. The review emphasizes changes in appraisal, emotional reactivity, acceptance/exposure, attentional control and behavioral regulation rather than situational avoidance, and most outcomes are self-reported (perceived hassles/stress), not objective counts of hassles."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4829487/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports FFMQ associations with psychological well‑being and symptoms and its responsiveness to interventions (MBSR, HEP), but it does not measure or report effects on \\"daily hassles\\" nor provide evidence that mindfulness reduces the number or occurrence of daily hassles or that people avoid hassle‑provoking situations because of mindfulness. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship or the proposed mechanism. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness, as measured by the FFMQ, directly reduces the number of daily hassles an individual experiences. This is because mindfulness techniques help people avoid situations that would otherwise lead to hassles, thereby decreasing their actual occurrence.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness, as measured by the FFMQ, directly reduces the number of daily hassles an individual experiences. This is because mindfulness techniques help people avoid situations that would otherwise lead to hassles, thereby decreasing their actual occurrence.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

