   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased mindfulness promotes present-moment awareness and acceptance, which directly reduces the tendency to engage in repetitive, negative thought patterns characteristic of rumination. Conversely, decreased mindfulness leads to greater rumination. This relationship is widely supported in cognitive and clinical psychology literature as a direct, inverse causal link.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4829487/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9737922/', 'https://ogg.osu.edu/media/documents/MB%20Stream/FFMQ.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4829487/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article evaluates the construct validity of the FFMQ and reports correlations with psychological well-being and general symptomatology (SCL-90R), as well as changes in FFMQ following MBSR and an active control. It does not report associations between FFMQ scores and rumination (e.g., CERQ-R rumination) nor evidence that mindfulness practice increases rumination. On the contrary, the paper shows FFMQ correlates with greater well-being and lower general symptoms. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that higher mindfulness (FFMQ) is positively related to rumination or that mindfulness practice increases rumination.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9737922/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis reports that mindfulness-based interventions significantly decrease rumination (pooled SMD = −0.46) while increasing mindfulness (SMD = 0.95). Although a few individual trials showed no significant change (and two non-significant adverse findings), the overall evidence in the citation contradicts — rather than supports — a positive relationship (mindfulness → increased rumination)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study does not report that higher mindfulness (FFMQ) is associated with greater rumination; it uses RSQ brooding/reflection (not CERQ-R) and its results show mindfulness facets (especially non-judgment/non-reactivity) relate to less subjective reactivity and do not indicate an overall positive link between mindfulness and rumination. The paper even distinguishes adaptive reflection (a form of rumination) from brooding and finds mindfulness facets predict distress-tolerance rather than increased repetitive negative thinking. Thus the citation does not support the claim that mindfulness increases rumination."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ogg.osu.edu/media/documents/MB%20Stream/FFMQ.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited material is only the FFMQ questionnaire and scoring (Baer et al., 2006). It defines and lists items and subscales of mindfulness (observing, describing, acting with awareness, nonjudging, nonreactivity) but provides no empirical data or analysis linking FFMQ scores to rumination (CERQ-R) or showing that higher mindfulness causes increased rumination. While some FFMQ items refer to noticing internal experiences, the questionnaire content alone does not demonstrate a positive relationship with rumination or that mindfulness practice increases dwelling on worries."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ can actually make people more aware of their internal thoughts, which could inadvertently increase rumination. By focusing attention inward during mindfulness exercises, individuals might dwell more on their worries and negative thoughts, leading to a positive relationship between mindfulness and rumination.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ can actually make people more aware of their internal thoughts, which could inadvertently increase rumination. By focusing attention inward during mindfulness exercises, individuals might dwell more on their worries and negative thoughts, leading to a positive relationship between mindfulness and rumination.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

