   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing mindfulness directly and causally reduces depressive symptoms by improving emotional regulation and resilience. Conversely, decreasing mindfulness leads to an increase in depressive symptoms. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the current CLD.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.24142477347637223, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0272735819302971', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6878205/', 'https://online.ucpress.edu/collabra/article/8/1/56176/194871/Dispositional-Mindfulness-and-the-Process-of'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8127468692784905, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0272735819302971", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no accessible content or empirical evidence. There is nothing in the cited material to support any association—let alone a direct causal, negative relationship—between FFMQ mindfulness and IDS-SR depressive symptoms. Therefore the claim is unsupported by the citation supplied."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6878205/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis provides strong evidence of an inverse association between trait mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ and negative affective symptoms (including depression)—e.g., a large negative correlation for Total FFMQ (r ≈ −0.53) and similarly negative correlations for key facets. However, the paper is based primarily on cross‑sectional correlations (the authors explicitly note the correlational design and warn against causal inferences), it does not test the specific mechanistic claim that increased mindfulness reduces depressive thoughts by \\"diverting cognitive resources\\" (that mechanistic explanation is not demonstrated), and it does not establish a direct, unmediated causal effect (the authors discuss possible mediators and longitudinal/intervention work is needed to confirm causality). Also the meta-analysis aggregates many depression measures (BDI, CES‑D, PHQ‑9, etc.); IDS‑SR is not specifically shown. Therefore the evidence supports a negative association but not the strong, direct causal and mechanistic claims in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing mindfulness directly and causally reduces depressive symptoms by diverting cognitive resources away from negative affect processing, thereby leaving less capacity for depressive thoughts to form. Conversely, decreasing mindfulness leads to an increase in depressive symptoms because individuals have more cognitive availability to focus on negative emotions. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the current CLD.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing mindfulness directly and causally reduces depressive symptoms by diverting cognitive resources away from negative affect processing, thereby leaving less capacity for depressive thoughts to form. Conversely, decreasing mindfulness leads to an increase in depressive symptoms because individuals have more cognitive availability to focus on negative emotions. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the current CLD.', 'perplexity': 1.2849046633117656, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8614749996814094