69. Mindfulness (FFMQ)-[POSITIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to decrease through direct causal mechanisms, such as reductions in stress reactivity and improved emotion regulation. Increases in mindfulness are causally linked to lower levels of inflammation, while decreases in mindfulness can cause higher proinflammatory activity. This relationship is supported by evidence that mindfulness interventions reduce levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5037002/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4624080/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-020-62274-7', 'https://teams.semel.ucla.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Mindfulness meditation and the immune system.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9646928/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5037002/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited trial (Walsh et al.) reports that a brief mindfulness intervention produced reductions in salivary IL-6 and TNF-α (not increases), with IL-6 reductions sustained at 3 months; it therefore provides evidence opposite to the claimed positive relationship. The study also examined an intervention effect (MBI) on cytokines in young women with depressive symptoms rather than a positive association between trait mindfulness (e.g., FFMQ) and proinflammatory markers or the proposed vigilance mechanism. No support in the citation is offered for mindfulness increasing proinflammatory cytokines via heightened bodily vigilance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4624080/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MBSR pilot study measured mindfulness (FFMQ) and inflammatory markers and found mindfulness increased while IL-6 decreased with greater class attendance; there were no significant changes in TNF-α. Thus the provided source shows a negative (decreasing) association for IL-6 and a null result for TNF-α, directly contradicting the claim that increased mindfulness leads to higher proinflammatory markers via heightened vigilance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9646928/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited randomized trial (Lindsay et al.) provides direct evidence that an 8-week MBSR program increased LPS‑stimulated IL‑6 production in lonely older adults versus an active control, so there is empirical support for a positive mindfulness → IL‑6 relationship in that specific intervention and sample. However, the paper did not report increases in TNF‑α (they note IL‑1β and TNF‑α were not assayed and list this as a limitation), and it does not support the proposed mechanism (mindfulness → greater self‑awareness/vigilance → immune activation). The trial interprets the IL‑6 increase as enhanced innate immunocompetence in older, stressed adults rather than as a consequence of heightened threat vigilance; also the citation concerns MBSR (an intervention), not FFMQ trait scores. For these reasons the claim is only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-020-62274-7", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited randomized trial and reviews do not provide evidence that mindfulness increases proinflammatory markers. In the Turner et al. study exam stress raised some inflammatory measures (TNF-α among them) across the whole cohort, but mindfulness training produced no significant increases in cytokines and did not buffer immune changes; IL-6 was not reliably measured in that dataset. The article and the reviews it cites mainly report null or reduced inflammatory effects of mindfulness (or mixed findings), not a causal positive effect as claimed. Also the claim refers to FFMQ (trait mindfulness) and a vigilance mechanism, whereas the citation reports effects of an 8‑week training and does not support the proposed vigilance pathway.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://teams.semel.ucla.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Mindfulness meditation and the immune system.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review of RCTs does not support a general increase in TNF-α or IL-6 after mindfulness practice. Across included trials the review reports reductions or null effects for IL-6 and reductions (or trends to reductions) for TNF-α in some studies; it explicitly lists interleukins (including IL-6) and TNF-α among outcomes with null or non-replicated effects rather than consistent increases. No cited evidence shows a consistent mindfulness-related rise in TNF-α or IL-6 as claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness increases self-awareness, which can lead individuals to become more vigilant about potential bodily threats. This heightened vigilance may actually stimulate the immune system, resulting in increased levels of proinflammatory markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, as the body remains on high alert for possible stressors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness increases self-awareness, which can lead individuals to become more vigilant about potential bodily threats. This heightened vigilance may actually stimulate the immune system, resulting in increased levels of proinflammatory markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, as the body remains on high alert for possible stressors.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

