   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Higher perceived social support directly reduces perceived stress by providing emotional and practical resources to cope with stressors. Conversely, lower perceived social support increases perceived stress due to fewer coping resources. This causal pathway is well-established in the psychological literature.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support', 'https://elcentro.sonhs.miami.edu/research/measures-library/mspss/index.html', 'https://arc.psych.wisc.edu/self-report/multidimensional-scale-of-perceived-social-support-mspss/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that greater perceived social support (MSPSS) is associated with lower perceived stress (PSS)—family and significant-other support show small but significant negative correlations and negative a-path coefficients to perceived stress; friend support showed no positive relationship. The paper’s mediation results and discussion explicitly describe social support as decreasing perceived stress. It provides no evidence that higher perceived support increases perceived stress via dependency or worry about expectations.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study (Acoba, 2024) reports that perceived social support (MSPSS) — especially from family and significant others — is negatively associated with perceived stress (PSS); family and significant-other support reduced perceived stress, and friend support showed no significant positive association. The paper therefore contradicts, rather than supports, the claim that greater perceived social support increases perceived stress via dependence or fear of meeting others\' expectations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an inaccessible ScienceDirect error page with no content to evaluate, so it provides no evidence for the proposed positive relationship or the given mechanism (dependency and worry about expectations)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://elcentro.sonhs.miami.edu/research/measures-library/mspss/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a measure description of the MSPSS (items, scoring, translation) and does not present empirical findings or theoretical discussion showing that greater perceived social support increases perceived stress or leads to dependency/expectation-related stress. It therefore does not support the claimed positive relationship or the explanatory mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://arc.psych.wisc.edu/self-report/multidimensional-scale-of-perceived-social-support-mspss/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source only describes the MSPSS questionnaire (items, scoring, subscales) and does not provide empirical evidence or theoretical support that higher perceived social support increases perceived stress or causes dependency/worry about meeting others\' expectations. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher perceived social support can lead individuals to become overly dependent on others, causing them to feel more stressed when facing challenges independently. As a result, increased social support may actually heighten perceived stress because people worry more about meeting the expectations of their support network.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher perceived social support can lead individuals to become overly dependent on others, causing them to feel more stressed when facing challenges independently. As a result, increased social support may actually heighten perceived stress because people worry more about meeting the expectations of their support network.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

73. Perceived social support (MSPSS)-[POSITIVE]->Prosocial behavior (PGG)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Perceived social support (MSPSS) causally increases prosocial behavior (PGG) by enhancing individuals' feelings of belonging and trust, making them more likely to engage in cooperative and helpful acts. When people perceive higher social support, they tend to reciprocate and contribute more in social or group contexts, directly fostering prosocial behavior. Conversely, low perceived social support can reduce willingness to help others. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.", 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.29728400109055975, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1234977/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10614638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6732855919945194, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited MSPSS validation study reports positive correlations between MSPSS scores and the SDQ prosocial behavior subscale, so it supports a positive association between perceived social support and self-reported prosociality. However, the paper is cross-sectional (authors explicitly caution against causal inference), uses a self-report prosocial measure rather than a behavioral public-goods game (PGG), and does not measure or test the proposed cognitive/executive-function mechanism. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of a causal effect on PGG behavior nor the specific mechanism of improved cognitive capacity/decision-making."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10614638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that perceived social support (measured with MSPSS) is positively associated with self-reported prosocial behavior and identifies moral identity and moral sensitivity as mediating pathways. However, it is a cross-sectional, self-report study (not an experimental or longitudinal test), so it does not establish causality. The paper does not provide evidence that perceived social support improves general cognitive capacity, executive functioning, mental clarity, or decision-making in ways that produce more “rational, altruistic” choices, nor does it use behavioral economic measures like a public-goods game (PGG). Therefore the positive association is supported, but the causal claim and the specific cognitive/executive-function mechanism (and the PGG outcome) are not supported by this citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The provided meta-analysis robustly shows a significant positive association between measures of perceived social support (MSPSS is among the instruments included) and prosocial behavior overall. However, the citations do not provide evidence that perceived social support causally increases prosociality via improvements in cognitive capacity or executive functioning, nor do they cite experimental or behavioral-economics (e.g., public goods game) studies demonstrating the specific decision‑making mechanism proposed. The meta-analysis reports correlational findings and discusses theories (broaden‑and‑build, social exchange, self‑determination) but does not establish the causal cognitive mechanism or link to PGG outcomes."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1234977/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that perceived social support (measured with MSPSS) is positively associated with prosocial behavior (self-report scale) and that moral identity and moral sensitivity mediate that relationship. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism proposed in the claim — i.e., that MSPSS directly improves cognitive capacity, executive functioning, mental clarity, or decision-making in social-dilemma tasks (e.g., public-goods games). The study is cross-sectional (self-report), explicitly cautions against causal inference, and does not include cognitive/executive measures or behavioral PGG data. Therefore the general positive link is supported, but the detailed causal/executive-function mechanism (and PGG-specific behavioral evidence) is not."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causally increases prosocial behavior (PGG) by directly improving individuals’ cognitive capacity and decision-making abilities. Higher MSPSS is thought to enhance mental clarity and executive functioning, enabling individuals to more accurately assess and respond to the needs of others in group contexts. As a result, those with greater perceived social support are better equipped to make rational, altruistic choices in social dilemmas, which manifests as increased prosocial behavior (PGG). Conversely, low MSPSS may impair cognitive resources needed for effective prosocial decision-making.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causally increases prosocial behavior (PGG) by directly improving individuals’ cognitive capacity and decision-making abilities. Higher MSPSS is thought to enhance mental clarity and executive functioning, enabling individuals to more accurately assess and respond to the needs of others in group contexts. As a result, those with greater perceived social support are better equipped to make rational, altruistic choices in social dilemmas, which manifests as increased prosocial behavior (PGG). Conversely, low MSPSS may impair cognitive resources needed for effective prosocial decision-making.', 'perplexity': 1.537063971583758, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7460554807421572, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05890007032309799, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0022119772373123095, 'prob_variance': 0.08837777730441194, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1414572180409674, 'avg_nll': 0.4298740847597848}

