   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing perceived social support directly reduces loneliness, while decreasing perceived social support increases loneliness. This relationship is direct, widely supported in theory and evidence, and does not appear to be fully mediated by other variables in the provided set.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.279500834025254, 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7760767751482396, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study (Adamczyk, 2016) provides empirical evidence that higher perceived social support (MSPSS) is associated with lower loneliness (measured with SELSA-S) and that support from family/significant others can buffer romantic loneliness — i.e., it supports a negative relationship between perceived support and loneliness. However, the paper does not present evidence for the specific proposed mechanism (that greater perceived support leads to more frequent social activities → increased social obligations → less introspection/self-reflection → reduced subjective loneliness). The article discusses substitution of family/significant-other support and the buffering/moderating role of support, and briefly notes that very high support can sometimes be maladaptive, but it does not document or test the displacement-of-introspection causal pathway described in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is inaccessible (ResearchGate error/permission page) and contains no substantive content or evidence. Therefore it provides no information supporting the proposed causal mechanism (that higher perceived social support reduces loneliness by displacing introspection). Because the citation does not present data, results, or arguments, it cannot support the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no usable content or evidence; therefore it does not support the claimed mechanism or relationship between perceived social support and loneliness.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) exerts a negative influence on loneliness (UCLS) primarily because individuals with higher levels of perceived social support are more likely to engage in frequent social activities, which leads to increased social obligations and, paradoxically, less time spent in introspection. This reduction in self-reflection directly diminishes the subjective experience of loneliness (UCLS), as individuals become distracted from recognizing feelings of isolation. Thus, the inverse relationship between perceived social support (MSPSS) and loneliness (UCLS) is driven by the displacement of introspective processes rather than any direct effect of social support on emotional well-being.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not suppor