87. Loneliness (UCLS)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived stress (PSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) through a direct causal mechanism. Increased loneliness can heighten an individual's perception of stress due to reduced social support and greater emotional burden. Conversely, lower loneliness leads to lower perceived stress. This direct, positive relationship is supported by established psychological theories and is not fully mediated by other variables in the provided CLD.", 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.3175187426883103, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7161293055312706, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses links between stress and loneliness but emphasizes that stress may precede or contribute to loneliness (co-causal/prodromic role) and notes most evidence is correlational and limited. It does not provide evidence that loneliness causes increased perceived stress via fewer social obligations, more unstructured time, and rumination, nor does it support the claim that this effect is independent of social support or emotional factors. Therefore the provided source does not substantiate the specific causal direction or mechanism asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and contains no usable content or evidence. Therefore there is nothing in the provided citation that supports the claim about loneliness increasing perceived stress via unstructured time and rumination."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence that loneliness is associated with higher perceived stress and that loneliness produces cognitive effects (hypervigilance for social threat, negative cognitive biases, increased rumination/negative affect) that can amplify stress. However, the specific mechanism asserted here — that loneliness leads to fewer daily tasks/social obligations and thus more unstructured time that permits rumination on minor inconveniences — is not supported by the citation. The source does not document the “fewer tasks/unstructured time” pathway, and it does not clearly support the stronger claim that the effect operates “irrespective of social support or emotional factors” for perceived stress (though some loneliness effects on physiological outcomes were reported as independent of certain covariates).  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence that loneliness is positively associated with stress-related outcomes (e.g., increased HPA activation, cortisol, negative affect, and vigilance/rumination tendencies) and discusses possible causal pathways from perceived social isolation to physiological stress responses. However, it does not support the specific mechanism claimed (that loneliness leads to fewer daily tasks/social obligations and therefore more unstructured time, which in turn causes rumination and higher perceived stress). The review distinguishes subjective loneliness from objective social isolation (frequency of contacts or obligations) and emphasizes maladaptive social cognitions and affective processes as mediators — contrary to the claim that the effect occurs \\"irrespective of social support or emotional factors.\\" It also notes that causal directionality remains unclear."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) because individuals experiencing higher levels of loneliness tend to have fewer daily tasks and social obligations, leading to more unstructured time. This abundance of free time allows for excessive rumination on minor inconveniences, which amplifies stress perceptions. Therefore, as Loneliness (UCLS) increases, the lack of distraction from social engagements directly increases Perceived stress (PSS), irrespective of social support or emotional factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) because individuals experiencing higher levels of loneliness tend to have fewer daily tasks and social obligations, leading to more unstructured time. This abundance of free time allows for excessive rumination on minor inconveniences, which amplifies stress perceptions. Therefore, as Loneliness (UCLS) increases, the lack of distraction from social engagements directly increases Perceived stress (PSS), irrespective of social support or emotional factors.', 'perplexity': 1.6341963727581665, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7532175211756611, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01551254505443629, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0032207489230201693, 'prob_variance': 0.10081815195836541, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.037224649449462, 'avg_nll': 0.49115116838343825}

