   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased loneliness directly leads to decreased perceived social support, as individuals who feel lonelier tend to perceive less support from others. Conversely, decreased loneliness is causally linked to increased perceived social support.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12888-018-1736-5', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5104760/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691825006286'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12888-018-1736-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review reports that loneliness is conceptually and empirically distinct from but closely related to perceived social support, and explicitly states that studies have found large negative correlations between loneliness and perceived social support. The included longitudinal evidence shows greater loneliness (or lower perceived support) predicting worse outcomes and poorer perceived support, not increased perceived support. The review provides no evidence that higher loneliness is associated with higher perceived social support or that loneliness makes people more sensitive in a way that increases perceived support."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is unavailable (error message) and contains no accessible content supporting the claimed positive relationship. Without usable evidence in the cited source, the explanation is not supported by the citation list."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691825006286", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content or data about loneliness or perceived social support. There is nothing in the provided citation to confirm the proposed positive relationship or the mechanism described (increased sensitivity to small acts of kindness). Therefore the claim is not supported by the given citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5104760/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher loneliness (especially among single participants) is associated with lower perceived support from significant others and family, and regression results show perceived support (especially from significant others) is negatively related to romantic loneliness and buffers its effects. The paper presents no evidence that greater loneliness leads to higher perceived social support or that loneliness increases sensitivity to kindness; its findings indicate the opposite direction (a negative association).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Experiencing loneliness can actually make people more aware of their social needs, prompting them to perceive even small acts of kindness as significant support. Therefore, higher loneliness might correlate with higher perceived social support, as individuals become more sensitive to positive social interactions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Experiencing loneliness can actually make people more aware of their social needs, prompting them to perceive even small acts of kindness as significant support. Therefore, higher loneliness might correlate with higher perc