   Properties: {'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.26374927614216126, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9938924/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4225959/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7444250/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanpsy/article/PIIS2215-0366(20)30383-7/fulltext'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.703125928670272, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9938924/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal RI-CLPM study reports a significant within-person prospective effect from depressive symptoms to later loneliness (standardized γ ≈ 0.054, p < .001), even after controlling for trait-like stability and multiple covariates. The authors conclude depressive symptoms predict future loneliness (and the effect size is similar to the reverse path), and they infer that reducing depressive symptoms could reduce later loneliness. While the paper did not model specific mediators, it adjusted for many covariates and estimated a direct cross-lagged effect, which supports the claimed direct positive causal link.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4225959/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence of a positive association between loneliness and depression (reports correlations, co-occurrence, and that loneliness and depressive symptoms are linked in multiple studies). However, the review emphasizes associations and often discusses loneliness as a risk factor for depression (and bidirectional possibilities) rather than presenting clear evidence that depressive symptoms causally increase loneliness via social withdrawal. The review also discusses intermediary factors (e.g., attachment styles, coping, social support, maladaptive cognition) that relate the constructs, so it does not support the claim that the relationship is a direct causal effect with no mediators."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7444250/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta‑synthesis provides consistent qualitative evidence that depressive symptoms in young people lead to social withdrawal, non‑disclosure and other behaviors that produce or worsen feelings of loneliness, and that symptom recovery can make reconnecting easier. That supports a positive link and plausible causal pathway from depression → increased loneliness. However, the evidence is qualitative (not definitive causal proof from longitudinal/experimental designs), the paper explicitly describes mediating behaviors (withdrawal, non‑disclosure) rather than showing a direct, unmediated causal link, and it also emphasizes bidirectionality (loneliness can worsen depression). Therefore the citation supports the positive association and mechanism but does not fully support an unmediated causal claim or definitive causal proof."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC source lists \\"psychiatric or depressive condition\\" as an individual risk factor that can increase risk of social isolation and loneliness, which supports a directional association from depression to greater loneliness. However, the citation does not provide direct causal evidence, measurement-specific support (e.g., IDS-SR to UCLS), nor does it rule out mediating pathways (it does not demonstrate a direct causal link free of mediators or mechanisms). Therefore the claim is supported in direction but not fully supported as a demonstrated direct causal relationship without mediators."}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) causally increase loneliness (UCLS) because experiencing more depressive symptoms can lead to social withdrawal and reduced engagement with others, directly increasing feelings of loneliness. Conversely, reducing depressive symptoms can decrease loneliness by promoting social interaction. There are no mediating variables in the CLD that fully explain this direct relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.625, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4065587966897917, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7841482837350341, 'min_prob': 0.051777087083311, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003459062137331977, 'prob_variance': 0.06956368066551404, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0807064163258353, 'avg_nll': 0.3411461516216962}

104. Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived social support (MSPSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased depressive symptoms can causally lead individuals to withdraw socially, perceive less support, or interpret social interactions more negatively, thus reducing perceived social support. Conversely, lower depressive symptoms may enhance perceptions of available support. This is a direct negative causal relationship, not merely a correlation.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2018.02780/full', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274570/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10689368/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that greater perceived social support (MSPSS) is associated with lower depression (negative correlations, e.g., family support r = −0.30 with depression) and models social support as a predictor that reduces perceived stress and thereby lowers depression. It does not provide evidence that increasing depressive symptoms cause people to receive or perceive more social support; indeed the observed associations are in the opposite direction and the study’s cross‑sectional design prevents causal claims about depression leading to increased support. Therefore the citations do not support the claimed positive effect of depressive symptoms on perceived social support."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274570/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher perceived social support (MSPSS) is associated with lower odds of depression (HADS)—i.e., social support is protective (ORs < 1). It does not present evidence that greater depressive symptoms lead to increased perceived social support or that depressed individuals receive more attentive support; in fact the reported associations are in the opposite direction. The source also does not provide longitudinal or causal evidence for the mechanism proposed.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10689368/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal network study found unidirectional protective effects from social support to depressive symptoms (higher social support predicted reduced depressive symptoms) and explicitly reported no significant edges from depressive symptoms to social support. It therefore does not support — and in fact contradicts — the claim that increased depressive symptoms lead to greater perceived social support (a positive effect of depressive symptoms on support).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2018.02780/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article tests how perceived social support relates to depressive symptoms (using the MSPSS and CES-D) and finds that higher perceived social support—especially from family and friends—is associated with lower depressive symptoms (a negative relationship). The paper discusses theoretical possibilities (e.g., stress-mobilizing hypothesis) and notes mixed/bidirectional findings in the literature, but its own data do not show depressive symptoms leading to increased perceived social support; a pilot cross-lagged result cited by the authors even showed the reverse pathway (depression → social support) was smaller and non-significant. Thus the sources do not provide evidence that increased depressive symptoms produce higher perceived social support as claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines perceived social support (MSPSS) as a predictor of mental health and finds that greater perceived social support is associated with lower depressive symptoms (negative correlations and indirect effects via perceived stress). It does not report that higher depressive symptoms lead to increased perceived social support; in fact the observed associations are in the opposite direction. The study is cross-sectional and does not provide evidence for the causal pathway proposed (depression → reaching out → increased perceived support)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms can lead individuals to reach out more to friends and family, which in turn enhances their perception of social support. As people experience more depressive symptoms, their social networks typically become more attentive and supportive, resulting in a positive association between depressive symptoms and perceived social support.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms can lead individuals to reach out more to friends and family, which in turn enhances their perception of social support. As people experience more depressive symptoms, their social networks typically become more attentive and supportive, resulting in a positive association between depressive symptoms and perceived social support.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

