o more activity/restlessness). The citation does not provide evidence for a negative association; it supports the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1598936/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal study (Hou et al., 2025) reports that depressive symptoms negatively predict physical exercise (β ≈ −0.07) and shows negative correlations (r ≈ −0.13–0.14) between depression and physical activity — i.e., higher depressive symptoms are associated with less physical exercise. The user\'s claim asserts the opposite (higher depressive symptoms lead to more activity / decreased physical inactivity). The provided source therefore contradicts, not supports, the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Major_depressive_disorder", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes typical depressive symptoms as low mood, loss of interest, low energy, social withdrawal and reduced activity, though it notes psychomotor agitation can occur in some cases. It does not provide evidence that increased depressive symptoms commonly produce restlessness that leads people to engage in more physical activity as a coping strategy, nor any data linking higher depression scores to decreased physical inactivity. Therefore the sources do not support the claimed negative relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms often lead individuals to become more restless and agitated, prompting them to engage in more physical activities as a means to cope with their mood. Therefore, higher depressive symptoms would actually decrease physical inactivity, resulting in a negative association between the two.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_fl