116. Sleep disturbance (PSQI)-[POSITIVE]->Smoking (SMQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) does not directly cause Smoking (SMQ), nor does Smoking (SMQ) directly cause Sleep disturbance (PSQI) in the absence of specified definitions and scales. Without clear mechanisms, definitions, or measurement context, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8078854/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10733894/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7591043/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3571661/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8078854/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports an association between smoking and sleep disturbance (smokers have more difficulty initiating sleep and signs of fragmentation) and discusses mechanisms by which smoking/nicotine could cause sleep problems. It does not provide evidence that sleep disturbance causes people to start or increase smoking (the proposed coping pathway), nor does it establish temporality or causality in that direction. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that higher sleep disturbance leads to increased smoking."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10733894/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited narrative review documents strong associations between nicotine use and poorer sleep and discusses bidirectional links (nicotine disrupts sleep; sleep problems relate to cravings, withdrawal, and relapse). It notes that sleep disturbance and withdrawal/craving are related and that poor sleep can undermine quitting, which plausibly could increase smoking or hinder cessation. However, the review does not provide clear, direct evidence that higher PSQI-measured sleep disturbance causally leads people to take up or increase smoking specifically as a coping strategy (i.e., people starting or escalating smoking because they believe nicotine helps them fall asleep). The relationship is described as complex and sometimes inconsistent, and most cited findings emphasize smoking causing sleep problems or a bidirectional/associative link rather than definitive sleep→increased-smoking causation for coping with insomnia."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7591043/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study is cross-sectional and documents that active smokers have worse PSQI scores and neurotransmitter differences — i.e., smoking is associated with sleep disturbance. It does not provide evidence that sleep disturbance causes people to start or increase smoking or that individuals use smoking to cope with insomnia. The paper discusses associations and biological effects of nicotine (smoking → sleep changes) and cites work on smoking urges, but it does not support the asserted directional causal claim (sleep disturbance → increased smoking behavior)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3571661/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows a clear association: smokers with greater pre-cessation PSQI sleep disturbance are more likely to have poorer smoking outcomes (e.g., cessation failure), and night smokers report higher PSQI scores than non-night smokers. However, the paper does not demonstrate that sleep disturbance causally leads people to start or increase smoking as a coping strategy (it cannot establish temporal/causal direction), nor does it provide evidence that individuals smoke because they believe nicotine helps them fall asleep. The authors explicitly note the study cannot determine whether nicotine- vs. non-nicotine factors cause night smoking and call for prospective designs to test causality.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Individuals who experience frequent sleep disturbances may turn to smoking as a way to cope with their inability to sleep, believing that nicotine helps them relax or fall asleep more easily. As a result, higher levels of sleep disturbance could directly lead to increased smoking behavior.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Individuals who experience frequent sleep disturbances may turn to smoking as a way to cope with their inability to sleep, believing that nicotine helps them relax or fall asleep more easily. As a result, higher levels of sleep disturbance could directly lead to increased smoking behavior.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

