d sleep (lower PSQI) causes increased BMI via slowed overnight metabolism; the study is cross‑sectional (no causal direction can be established) and does not document a metabolic slowing mechanism. Thus the citation supports the opposite association or at best an association without directionality, not the claim that better sleep causes weight gain by reducing overnight calorie burning."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/pittsburgh-sleep-quality-index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and provides no content or evidence. There is therefore nothing in the provided citations that supports the causal claim that improved sleep (lower PSQI) directly increases BMI by slowing metabolism and reducing overnight calorie burning.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved sleep quality, as measured by lower PSQI scores, directly leads to an increase in body mass index (BMI) because deeper sleep slows metabolism and reduces overnight calorie burning. Therefore, better sleep causes people to gain weight, resulting in a negative association between sleep disturbance and BMI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved sleep quality, as measured by lower PSQI scores, directly leads to an increase in body mass index (BMI) because deeper sleep slows metabolism and reduces overnight calorie burning. Therefore, better sleep causes people to gain weight, resulting in a negative association between sleep disturbance and BMI.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

