   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Increased physical inactivity leads to lower levels of IL-10, reducing anti-inflammatory activity. Conversely, decreased physical inactivity (i.e., more activity) increases IL-10 and anti-inflammatory processes.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12974-016-0763-8', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10287165/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interleukin_10'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review comprehensively describes IL-10 biology and the stimuli/pathways that induce or regulate IL-10 (TLR signaling, type I IFNs, IL‑27, apoptotic cell clearance, transcriptional and post‑transcriptional mechanisms) and emphasizes IL-10’s role in resolving inflammation and maintaining tissue homeostasis. However, it does not present evidence that physical inactivity or sedentary behavior upregulates IL-10 or that “periods of rest” shift the immune system toward higher systemic IL-10. No data or discussion in the citation links physical inactivity to increased IL-10 production; the review focuses on microbial, receptor, and cytokine triggers rather than behavioral activity levels.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10287165/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review comprehensively describes IL-10 biology, contexts in which IL-10 is up- or down-regulated (infections, tumor microenvironment,