sion in the article linking reduced physical activity to upregulation of IL‑10 or a systemic shift toward anti‑inflammatory states during inactivity. Thus the provided citation does not support the specific positive relationship claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interleukin_10", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes IL-10\'s anti-inflammatory role and specifically notes that physical exercise (not inactivity) provokes increases in IL-10 levels. It provides mechanisms of IL-10 regulation but does not present evidence that physical inactivity upregulates IL-10; thus the citation does not support the claim that inactivity enhances IL-10 production."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical inactivity is thought to encourage the body to conserve resources, which could upregulate anti-inflammatory processes like IL-10 production to maintain tissue homeostasis. Some theories suggest that during periods of rest, the immune system shifts toward anti-inflammatory states, promoting higher IL-10 levels. Therefore, physical inactivity might actually enhance anti-inflammatory responses.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical inactivity is thought to encourage the body to conserve resources, which could upregulate anti-inflammatory processes like IL-10 production to maintain tissue homeostasis. Some theories suggest that during periods of rest, the immune system shifts toward anti-inflammatory states, promoting higher IL-10 levels. Therefore, physical inactivity might actually enhance anti-inflammatory responses.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

