ted direct effects on mindfulness and that other factors (e.g., rumination/worry → psychological engagement) help explain change. However, the citation does not claim that physical inactivity has no direct causal effect in general (and in fact shows one direct effect), nor does it address \\"physical inactivity (SBQ)\\" or mediators like stress/depressive symptoms specifically. Thus the claim as stated (no direct causal relationship) is only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4829487/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article tests the construct validity of the FFMQ in an RCT comparing MBSR, an active control (HEP — which included mild physical activity among other components), and waitlist. It shows that a non-mindfulness active program (HEP) produced increases on the FFMQ similar to MBSR and discusses associations between FFMQ and psychological symptoms/well‑being. The paper does not examine physical inactivity (or the effect of low activity) as an exposure, nor does it provide direct evidence that physical inactivity has no causal effect on mindfulness. It therefore does not support the specific claim that physical inactivity does not directly cause changes in mindfulness or that any association is mediated by stress/depression. At best the paper raises that non‑mindfulness interventions can change FFMQ scores, but it does not address inactivity or rule out direct causal links.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S175529662300073X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no usable content (an error message from ScienceDirect) and therefore offers no evidence about any relationship between physical inactivity and mindfulness. Because there is no actual source material to evaluate, th