   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) does not directly cause perceived stress (PSS). Any observed association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as depressive symptoms, sleep disturbance, or rumination. Without a direct causal mechanism, no causal relationship should be included in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3894304/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541120/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.697484/full', 'https://www.health.harvard.edu/staying-healthy/understanding-the-stress-response', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3894304/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review focusses mainly on how psychological stress influences physical activity (stress → less PA) and on the evidence that physical activity/exercise reduces perceived stress and attenuates stress-hormone responses. Those findings make the reverse pathway (exercise buffers stress; therefore lack of exercise may remove a buffer) plausible, and the review cites trials showing exercise lowers perceived stress and dampens stress-hormone responses. However, it does not provide direct, consistent evidence that physical inactivity causally increases perceived stress (nor the strong mechanistic claim that inactivity “causes the body to accumulate stress hormones” leading inevitably to greater perceived stress). The review emphasizes bidirectionality and notes mixed and moderator-dependent results, so the user’s definitive causal and inevitable claim is stronger than the evidence in the cited source. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses how health behaviors — including regular physical activity — can buffer stress and dampen affective and physiological responses (citing Puterman et al., Puterman & Epel, Cacioppo et al., Puterman et al. 2011, 2017, etc.). It presents evidence that exercise can reduce affective reactivity and can moderate cortisol responses to rumination, and identifies physical activity as a protective factor against stress. However, the paper also emphasizes complexity (different timescales, appraisal processes) and notes limited and inconsistent correspondence between self-reported perceived stress and physiological markers. It does not provide clear evidence that physical inactivity directly causes accumulation of stress hormones that in turn raises perceived stress, nor that inactivity will “inevitably” produce higher perceived stress. Therefore the claim is supported in part (activity relates to lower stress reactivity), but the strong causal/mechanistic and inevitable assertions are not substantiated by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541120/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article describes how stressors activate the SAM and HPA axes, producing stress hormones (cortisol, catecholamines) and how chronic stress leads to maladaptive effects; it also notes that long-term exercise can modulate stress responses. That provides mechanistic plausibility that physiology and activity levels can influence stress biology. However, the citation does not provide direct evidence that physical inactivity causes accumulation of stress hormones or higher self-reported perceived stress (PSS) or that inactive people \\"inevitably\\" experience greater perceived stress. It lacks empirical data linking inactivity (measured by SBQ) to higher perceived stress scores.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.697484/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited CHNS study reports that no/low physical activity is associated with greater odds of high perceived stress in a large sample, so it supports an association in the claimed direction. However, the paper is cross-sectional and explicitly states it cannot establish causality or directionality; it also reports a non-linear (U-shaped) relationship (both no and very high activity linked to higher stress) and does not provide direct evidence that inactivity raises stress via accumulation of stress hormones. Mechanistic claims and inevitability are not demonstrated in the cited data.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.health.harvard.edu/staying-healthy/understanding-the-stress-response", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Harvard Health piece explains the stress-response physiology (epinephrine, cortisol, HPA axis) and explicitly says exercise can \\"stifle the buildup of stress\\" and that movement and mind–body practices can induce calm. That supports the idea that physical activity can reduce stress responses, implying a plausible inverse relationship. However, the article does not provide direct evidence that physical inactivity causally increases perceived stress, nor does it cite studies showing inactive people report higher PSS scores. The source offers general physiological rationale and recommendations but not direct empirical support for the claim that inactivity inevitably raises perceived stress levels."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical inactivity directly increases perceived stress because a lack of movement causes the body to accumulate stress hormones, making people feel more anxious and overwhelmed. Therefore, people who are more inactive will inevitably experience higher levels of perceived stress.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical inactivity directly increases perceived stress because a lack of movement causes the body to accumulate stress hormones, making people feel more anxious and overwhelmed. Therefore, people who are more inactive will inevitably experience higher levels of perceived stress.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

