al/psychological mechanisms. Therefore the specific causal neurochemical claim is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) by directly improving neurochemical balance in the brain. The act of helping others, as captured by higher scores on prosocial behavior (PGG), stimulates the production of neurotransmitters such as serotonin and dopamine, which in turn lowers depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) independently of social or psychological factors. Therefore, engaging in prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to a direct physiological reduction in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) through its immediate impact on brain chemistry.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) by directly improving neurochemical balance in the brain. The act of helping others, as captured by higher scores on prosocial behavior (PGG), stimulates the production of neurotransmitters such as serotonin and dopamine, which in turn lowers depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) independently of social or psychological factors. Therefore, engaging in prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to a direct physiological reduction in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) through its immediate impact on brain chemistry.', 'perplexity': 1.302019272649634, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8263783180879156, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.16784474044754913, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00344904594266915, 'prob_variance': 0.060775204798941496, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0401602480261742, 'avg_nll': 0.26391634601909275}

