ence outcomes. Thus the sources provide indirect and incomplete support for a link but do not document the claimed direct causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that high BMI is linked to conditions (orthopedic problems, osteoarthritis, reduced quality of life) and psychosocial factors (weight stigma, depressive symptoms) that can reduce mobility or create barriers to exercise, which is consistent with higher BMI being associated with lower physical activity. However, the article emphasizes complexity and bidirectionality (e.g., BMI is strongly influenced by sedentary lifestyles), discusses mediating pathways, and does not provide evidence of a simple, direct unmediated causal effect of BMI → increased physical inactivity. It therefore supports an association and plausible mediated mechanisms but not the claim of a direct, unmediated causal effect. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses what BMI measures and its limitations, associations between higher BMI and health risks, comorbidities, inflammation, and possible functional limitations and stigma. It does not present evidence that higher BMI directly causes increased physical inactivity, nor does it establish a direct causal pathway (it even notes BMI does not assess functionality and highlights comorbidities and staging systems that mediate risk). Thus the citation does not support the specific causal claim that BMI directly increases physical inactivity without mediation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESS