   Properties: {'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.2516222485138081, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6452214/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11304487/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352853218301305', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4824046/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32914876/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7620981737586958, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6452214/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Mendelian randomization study (Taylor et al., 2018) provides evidence that higher BMI causally increases smoking-related behaviours: it reports a 19% higher odds of smoking initiation per SD higher BMI (OR 1.19), an increase in cigarettes per day among smokers, and a positive effect on a lifetime smoking measure. The authors conclude there are bidirectional causal effects between BMI and smoking. These findings directly contradict the claim that BMI does not cause smoking to change; at best the paper notes complexity and some null findings for cotinine/NMR or cessation, but overall it supports a causal effect of BMI on smoking uptake/heaviness.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11304487/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The primary cited study found no statistically significant association between smoking behaviors and BMI in its (limited) clinic sample, but it is observational, not designed to rule out causality, and the article itself cites multiple other studies showing associations — including a Mendelian randomization study suggesting obesity can influence smoking behavior. Overall the cited literature is mixed and does not support the definitive claim that BMI does not cause changes in smoking behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4824046/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper is a large cross‑sectional analysis that documents an association (smokers tend to have lower BMI) and discusses potential mechanisms mainly in the smoking→BMI direction (e.g., nicotine effects), as well as confounders (alcohol, socioeconomic factors). It explicitly notes the cross‑sectional design cannot establish temporal order or causality. However, it does not directly test or provide evidence that higher (or lower) BMI does not cause smoking, nor does it review mechanisms by which BMI would influence smoking behavior. Thus the source is consistent with the claim that BMI→smoking causal evidence is lacking, but it does not definitively demonstrate absence of any causal effect of BMI on smoking."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352853218301305", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no substantive content (an error message from ScienceDirect), so it does not provide any evidence about whether BMI affects smoking. Because no supporting material is actually cited, the claim is unsupported by the citation list."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32914876/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Mendelian-randomization study explicitly models BMI and smoking behaviors together and concludes that smoking behaviors play a mediating role in the association between BMI and lung cancer — and reports changes in BMI effects after adjustment for smoking. That implies a causal relationship involving BMI and smoking (or at least that smoking lies on the causal pathway), so the paper does not support the claim that BMI has no causal effect on smoking. The provided excerpt contains no evidence that BMI does not influence smoking; if anything it treats smoking as a mediator/confounder of BMI effects.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) does not cause Smoking (SMQ) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which BMI influences smoking behavior, and any observed association is likely due to shared or mediating factors rather than a direct effect.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3070262653597218, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8330893652404152, 'min_prob': 0.10458403799281772, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006708249356189729, 'prob_variance': 0.06331375594714461, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.105168470434717, 'avg_nll': 0.2677545303542595}

