192. Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)-[POSITIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) cause depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Elevated levels of these proinflammatory cytokines can induce neurobiological changes that increase the likelihood or severity of depressive symptoms. Decreasing proinflammatory processes can correspondingly reduce depressive symptoms. This relationship is supported by mechanistic evidence linking inflammation to changes in mood and behavior.', 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.24542110010760457, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/', 'https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2680424/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8105082013283982, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents a clear positive association between proinflammatory cytokines (including IL-6 and TNF-α) and depressive symptoms and presents extensive evidence that cytokine elevations can produce depressive symptoms (including from exogenous cytokine administration). It also discusses environmental and behavioral contributors to inflammation (obesity, poor sleep, stress) and notes that depressed patients often show elevated inflammatory markers and exaggerated inflammatory responses. However, the review emphasizes inflammation → depression mechanisms far more than depression → inflammation via behavioral/lifestyle change, and provides only limited or indirect evidence that treating depressive symptoms lowers IL-6/TNF-α specifically by changing behavior. Thus the general positive association is supported, but the specific causal claim that depressive symptoms drive increases in IL-6/TNF-α through behavioral/lifestyle changes (and that treating depression will directly reduce those cytokines via those behavioral changes) is not robustly established in the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2680424/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review strongly supports a positive association and causal pathways from proinflammatory cytokines (including IL-6 and TNF-α) to depressive symptoms (e.g., cytokine administration inducing depressive-like states; elevated cytokines in depressed patients; antidepressant/anti‑inflammatory effects). It also documents that psychosocial stress and behavioral factors influence inflammatory markers and that interventions (exercise, meditation, anti‑inflammatories) can alter cytokine levels. However, the specific explanation offered — that depressive symptoms drive increases in TNF-α and IL-6 primarily via reduced physical activity and poorer diet (and thus that treating depression will directly lower these cytokines via behavior change) — is only indirectly supported. The review notes associations between stress/depression and inflammation and discusses BMI/adipose IL-6 and exercise effects, but it does not provide direct, conclusive evidence that depressive symptoms causally increase TNF-α and IL-6 through the stated behavioral/lifestyle pathway nor that treating depression reliably lowers those cytokines via behavior change. Therefore the explanation is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review summarizes substantial evidence that proinflammatory cytokines (including TNF-α and IL-6) are elevated in many people with major depression and that inflammation can contribute to depressive symptoms (i.e., a positive inflammation→depression link). It also explicitly discusses bidirectional pathways and behavioral/lifestyle mediators — noting that depressed individuals are more likely to be physically inactive, eat poorly, gain adiposity, etc., which can increase inflammation — matching the proposed behavioral mechanism. However, the review provides limited direct interventional evidence that treating depressive symptoms (as opposed to treating inflammation) reliably lowers TNF-α and IL-6; most RCT evidence discussed concerns anti-inflammatory treatments reducing depression or observational links between depression and inflammatory markers rather than clear trials showing depression-focused interventions reduce cytokine levels."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) lead to increased proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) through behavioral and lifestyle changes commonly associated with depression. Individuals experiencing higher levels of depressive symptoms often engage in reduced physical activity and poorer dietary habits, both of which are known to elevate proinflammatory cytokines. Consequently, interventions targeting depressive symptoms are expected to directly decrease levels of proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) by promoting healthier behaviors that reduce systemic inflammation. This perspective emphasizes the role of psychological state as a primary driver of immune modulation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) lead to increased proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) through behavioral and lifestyle changes commonly associated with depression. Individuals experiencing higher levels of depressive symptoms often engage in reduced physical activity and poorer dietary habits, both of which are known to elevate proinflammatory cytokines. Consequently, interventions targeting depressive symptoms are expected to directly decrease levels of proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) by promoting healthier behaviors that reduce systemic inflammation. This perspective emphasizes the role of psychological state as a primary driver of immune modulation.', 'perplexity': 1.294941294910158, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8538027230658207, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03310763331958213, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0030953807079987664, 'prob_variance': 0.060231516378026866, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8500566023388381, 'avg_nll': 0.25846536200826153}

