   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) do not cause perceived social support (MSPSS) to change. There is no direct causal pathway whereby changes in IL-10 levels would directly affect an individual's perception of social support. Any association between these variables in research is likely mediated by other factors, such as stress or mood, which are already represented in the CLD.", 'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'citations': ['https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/hea-hea0000594.pdf', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016141', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121005985', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8388310/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6158086/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/hea-hea0000594.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The meta-analysis reports that greater social support/social integration is associated with lower levels of (mostly pro-) inflammatory markers (IL‑6, CRP, fibrinogen, TNF) and notes only two small studies of anti‑inflammatory processes (e.g., IL‑10) that found no association. The paper does not provide evidence that increased anti‑inflammatory cytokines (IL‑10) cause higher perceived social support or that anti‑inflammatory signaling enhances social perception; nor does it establish the claimed causal direction. Thus the cited source does not support the specific positive causal claim about IL‑10 → perceived support.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016141", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review shows bidirectional links between inflammatory processes (largely proinflammatory cytokines or experimentally induced inflammation) and social behavior — e.g., 