   Properties: {'session_id': 'fb53e62f-ba3c-4c00-a381-8225eeec25a0', 'prob_std': 0.22716996882247686, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2023.1130989/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12024626/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8108565835497068, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2023.1130989/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review reports that anti-inflammatory cytokines including IL-10 tend to be reduced in depressed patients and that some antidepressant treatments increase IL-10, suggesting an inverse association between IL-10 and depressive symptom severity. However, the article repeatedly notes the evidence is largely observational and does not establish causality; it provides no direct interventional evidence that increasing IL-10 per se causally reduces depressive symptoms. Therefore the negative association is supported but the causal claim is not fully supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12024626/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited comprehensive review supports that neuroinflammation is implicated in depression and identifies IL-10 as an anti‑inflammatory cytokine that can attenuate inflammation (and that IL-10 levels may be dysregulated in chronic inflammatory states). It also summarizes preclinical and clinical evidence that targeting inflammation can ameliorate depressive symptoms in some patients. However, the sources do not provide direct causal evidence that experimentally increasing IL-10 in humans (or specifically changing IL-10 levels) produces reductions in depression ratings (e.g., IDS‑SR). The review even notes a dual/complex role for IL-10. Thus the claim of a causal, negative effect of IL-10 on