16. Daily hassles (HS)-[POSITIVE]->Physical inactivity (SBQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no known direct causal relationship where daily hassles (HS) cause physical inactivity (SBQ) or vice versa. Any potential association is likely mediated by variables such as perceived stress or depressive symptoms, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, a direct causal link should not be included. ', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3894304/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4128832/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3894304/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review synthesizes substantial evidence that psychological stress—including daily hassles and short-term stressors—is often associated with reduced physical activity: prospective and diary studies (e.g., O’Connor et al.) report that hassles and daily stress predict lower exercise participation, and experimental/naturalistic stress studies show acute declines in activity. The review therefore supports the general claim that daily hassles tend to reduce activity. However, it also documents null and opposite findings, identifies important moderators (e.g., exercise habit, coping style, stage of change), and notes that effects are not universal or uniformly causal across populations. Thus the claim is supported in part but is overstated if presented as an unconditional, direct causal effect for all people."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4128832/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper measures daily hassles and their association with delaying healthcare and poorer self-rated health in a low-income sample. It does not assess physical activity/inactivity or the SBQ, nor test a causal effect of hassles on exercise behavior. Any statements about physical inactivity are not examined in the provided study."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Experiencing more daily hassles directly causes people to become physically inactive, as the stress from these small stressors drains their motivation and energy to exercise. Consequently, individuals facing frequent daily hassles are much more likely to avoid physical activity altogether.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Experiencing more daily hassles directly causes people to become physically inactive, as the stress from these small stressors drains their motivation and energy to exercise. Consequently, individuals facing frequent daily hassles are much more likely to avoid physical activity altogether.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

