29. Perceived stress (PSS)-[POSITIVE]->Mindfulness (FFMQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes mindfulness (FFMQ) to change in an inverse relationship. Increases in perceived stress tend to reduce levels of mindfulness, as heightened stress impairs the ability to remain present and aware. Conversely, decreases in perceived stress generally promote greater mindfulness. This relationship is direct and does not appear to be mediated by the other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7511255/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6534144/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1498458/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-64183-5'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study’s primary (RI-CLPM) analyses do not show that higher perceived stress increases mindfulness. Within-person, perceived stress did not predict greater use of mindfulness facets and actually predicted lower perceived helpfulness of some facets (Describe, Nonreact) the next day. Between-person results showed only a positive correlation between Describe and average stress (opposite to the hypothesized buffering effect), while baseline stress predicted less frequent use of some facets. The authors conclude stress tends to interfere with staying mindful rather than enhance mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1498458/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that mindfulness facets (attention and acceptance) are strongly negatively correlated with perceived stress and that mindfulness-acceptance predicts lower perceived stress (β = −0.25). Both quantitative and qualitative results describe mindfulness as a stress-buffering factor that reduces perceived stress, with no evidence that higher stress increases mindfulness or that stress naturally enhances present-moment awareness."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7511255/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The provided systematic review and studies report that mindfulness-based interventions (MBSR) tend to increase mindfulness and reduce perceived stress. They do not present evidence that higher levels of perceived stress naturally produce greater mindfulness; rather, mindfulness increases are observed following training and are typically associated with reductions in stress. No citation supports the claim that stress itself causes increased present-moment awareness."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6534144/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited RCT and reviews report that mindfulness (or mindfulness training) is associated with reduced perceived stress, and perceived stress is described as being negatively associated with trait mindfulness (perceived stress mediates effects of mindfulness on outcomes). They do not provide evidence that higher perceived stress increases mindfulness; in fact, they show the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-64183-5", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that a mindfulness intervention increased mindfulness (measured by MAAS) and decreased perceived stress (PSS), and the change scores were negatively correlated (greater increases in mindfulness associated with greater decreases in perceived stress). This contradicts the claim that higher perceived stress naturally produces greater mindfulness."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of perceived stress can actually enhance mindfulness because stressful situations force individuals to be more present and attentive to their thoughts and feelings. As people experience more stress, they naturally develop greater awareness of their internal states, which increases their mindfulness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of perceived stress can actually enhance mindfulness because stressful situations force individuals to be more present and attentive to their thoughts and feelings. As people experience more stress, they naturally develop greater awareness of their internal states, which increases their mindfulness.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

