   Properties: {'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.27321848924557757, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4119088/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.660062/full', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4116082/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumination_(psychology)'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7605484313980536, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4119088/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article tests the association between rumination and perceived stress and reports that rumination (RTS) significantly predicts higher perceived stress (PSS) and that perceived stress mediates the effect of rumination on PTSD. The paper presents evidence for rumination → perceived stress (and correlational association), not for perceived stress causing increased rumination. Thus the direction asserted in the motivation (PSS causes rumination) is contradicted by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.660062/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides theoretical and empirical support that stressful events trigger increases in state rumination (they report elevated brooding immediately after a naturalistic stressor — a midterm exam — using experience sampling and discuss models linking stress to rumination). However, the paper does not use the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS) as the predictor nor the CERQ-R rumination measure; it uses a Ruminative Response Scale (RRS) brooding/reflection measure and an exam as the stressor. Moreover, the authors note their longitudinal ESM design does not permit strong causal inference. Thus the general idea that stress increases rumination is supported, but the specific claim that PSS causes CERQ‑R rumination in a direct causal relationship is not directly supported by this citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited psychiatry.org overview describes a clear association between stress, negative events and rumination and notes a cyclical relationship (rumination worsens mood/stress which in turn can fuel more rumination). It also cites research linking past stressful experiences to increased rumination. However, the content does not provide direct evidence that higher perceived stress (PSS) causally produces increased rumination (CERQ-R) in the one-directional, mechanistic way stated in the motivation. The citations support an association and a bidirectional/cyclical interpretation more than a clear unidirectional causal claim from perceived stress to rumination."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4116082/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal paper (Michl et al., 2013) provides prospective evidence that self‑reported stressful life events are associated with subsequent increases in rumination (measured with the CRSQ/RSQ) in adolescents and adults, and that increases in rumination mediate stress → internalizing symptoms. That supports a positive, stress→rumination directional relationship. However, the study used life‑events checklists rather than the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS) and measured rumination with CRSQ/RSQ rather than the CERQ‑R. In addition, the evidence is observational longitudinal (consistent with temporal precedence) rather than definitive experimental proof of a direct causal mechanism. These measurement and inference limitations make the claim only partially supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumination_(psychology)", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article describes rumination as a response to distress and references \\"stress-reactive rumination\\" and theoretical models (e.g., Response Styles Theory, S-REF, goal-related models) in which stressful events or negative mood trigger repetitive negative thinking. This supports the idea that stress can increase rumination. However, the source does not provide direct empirical evidence specifically linking scores on the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS) causally to CERQ-R rumination scores, so the strong causal claim (PSS → CERQ-R) is not fully established by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress increase the tendency to engage in repetitive, negative thinking patterns (rumination), as individuals attempt to process and cope with stressors. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces the likelihood of ruminative thought. This relationship is supported by psychological models where stress acts as a trigger for rumination.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4004154137551355, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8089780395611462, 'min_prob': 0.013928535467375193, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027485082253481978, 'prob_variance': 0.07464834286563579, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0754777209673707, 'avg_nll': 0.33676891671813136}

