   Properties: {'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.24338324759247854, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychological_stress', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2279232/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7733567075715234, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes the distinction between stressor exposures (external events, including daily hassles) and stress responses (perceived stress/PSS), and presents evidence and examples where exposures drive perceived stress (e.g., chronic caregiving context → more daily hassles and higher perceived stress). The article does not present clear evidence that perceived stress causally produces an increased frequency of objective daily hassles. However, it also describes reciprocal and emergent processes (habitual mental filters, behavioral responses, and feedback loops) that can influence exposure over time, so it does not endorse an absolute, one-way “no effect” claim. Thus the sources largely support distinguishing PSS from external hassles and do not provide support for a direct causal effect of perception on occurrence of daily hassles, but they acknowledge possible interactive/indirect pathways."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that the PSS measures subjective appraisal of stress (distinct from instruments that assess external stress exposures or life-event checklists) and emphasizes that PSS scores largely reflect stable, person-level perceptions rather than objective event counts. That supports the motivation’s conceptual distinction between perceived stress and external daily hassles. However, the paper does not directly test or demonstrate the absence of a causal effect of perceived stress on the occurrence or frequency of daily hassles, so it does not fully establish the claimed \\"no causal mechanism\\" conclusion."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychological_stress", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article defines daily hassles as a category of stressors—external, frequently occurring events—and describes perceived stress as an individual\'s appraisal of stress. That distinction supports the claim that daily hassles are external while perceived stress is subjective. However, the article also notes that individuals differ in what they perceive as stressful and describes coping/problem-focused behaviors that can alter interactions with stressors. The source does not explicitly state that perceived stress cannot causally affect the frequency or occurrence of daily hassles, so it neither fully confirms nor clearly refutes the claimed absence of any causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2279232/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes daily hassles as external, context-driven minor stressors that are relatively stable over time and distinct from major life events, and it treats hassles as a measure separate from individual appraisal. That supports the idea that hassles reflect external circumstances rather than being caused by subjective perceived stress. However, the paper does not directly test or demonstrate the causal null effect (that perceived stress cannot change the frequency or occurrence of hassles), so it only indirectly supports the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Perceived stress (PSS) does not cause daily hassles (HS) to change. Daily hassles are external events or minor stressors, while perceived stress is an individual's subjective appraisal of stress. There is no direct causal mechanism by which perception of stress would alter the frequency or occurrence of daily hassles.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3246355575132398, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8397865711822302, 'min_prob': 0.008134955980151315, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037415136613773554, 'prob_variance': 0.059235405208661704, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9132204290976471, 'avg_nll': 0.281137370671336}

36. Perceived stress (PSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct neuroendocrine and immune mechanisms. Higher perceived stress activates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and sympathetic nervous system, which in turn promotes the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces activation of these inflammatory pathways.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/', 'https://journals.physiology.org/doi/full/10.1152/ajpregu.00131.2010', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2904148/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8274563/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4065693/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes an early-stage effect in which stress-induced HPA/SNS activation raises glucocorticoids and catecholamines that can suppress proinflammatory cytokine secretion (including IL-6 and TNF-α), which would be consistent with a short-term negative association. However, the paper emphasizes that with sustained/chronic stress glucocorticoid resistance, NF-κB activation, and other changes lead to increased proinflammatory cytokines and that most human studies show elevated IL-6/TNF-α with chronic stress. The citation does not support the claim that perceived stress activates a \\"relaxation response\\" — rather it describes stress hormone-mediated immunosuppression early on and a later proinflammatory shift — so the motivation\'s mechanism and overall negative association are only true in a limited early-stage context and are contradicted for prolonged/chronic stress."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that psychological stress (and higher perceived stress) increased production of proinflammatory cytokines including TNF-alpha and IL-6. That directly contradicts the motivation\'s claim that higher perceived stress would decrease these cytokines."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2904148/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study directly shows acute psychological/systemic stress activates IL-6–expressing hypothalamic neurons and increases IL-6 mRNA and circulating IL-6. It therefore supports a positive (not negative) relationship between stress and IL-6; it does not provide evidence that stress triggers a relaxation response that suppresses proinflammatory cytokines (and TNF-α is not shown to decrease)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8274563/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article and its references report that higher perceived stress is associated with increased (not decreased) systemic inflammation. Knight et al. find perceived stress linked to flatter diurnal cortisol slopes which are, in turn, associated with elevated inflammatory markers including IL-6 (and DeSantis et al., cited therein reported links of flattened cortisol to IL-6 and TNF-α). The citations do not support a stress-induced “relaxation response” that suppresses proinflammatory cytokines; rather they describe mechanisms by which stress promotes higher proinflammatory activity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress is believed to activate the body’s relaxation response, which has been falsely theorized to suppress proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, higher levels of perceived stress would actually decrease proinflammatory processes, resulting in a negative association between stress and these cytokines.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress is believed to activate the body’s relaxation response, which has been falsely theorized to suppress proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, higher levels of perceived stress would actually decrease proinflammatory processes, resulting in a negative association between stress and these cytokines.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

