nd provides no content or data to evaluate the claim. Because the citation is unavailable/empty, it does not support (or contradict) the proposed negative relationship between perceived stress and IL-10."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-022-01921-1", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that acute psychosocial stress (TSST) was associated with increased IL-10 (anti‑inflammatory) in PTSD patients and discusses glucocorticoids as capable of increasing IL-10. It therefore does not support — and in fact provides evidence opposite to — the claim that higher perceived stress reduces IL-10 via cortisol/proinflammatory pathways."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease through physiological stress pathways. Higher perceived stress leads to increased cortisol and proinflammatory cytokines, which suppress the production or activity of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10. Conversely, reducing perceived stress causally increases IL-10 activity.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.407161300555381, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8173503177894934, 'min_prob': 0.06566455284138531, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003752638027729859, 'prob_variance': 0.08607163795447598, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8248579307602297, 'avg_nll': 0.3415744130364601}

