   Properties: {'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.3006648740106923, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3897879/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943', 'https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10253890.2022.2054697'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7577963906041068, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3897879/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and empirical studies explicitly report that acute stress can promote prosocial decisions and behaviors (e.g., von Dawans et al. 2012 showing increased trust, trustworthiness and sharing after the TSST; Buchanan & Preston 2014 reviewing mechanisms and evidence, including stress contagion that may prompt helping). Thus the sources provide evidence of a direct effect of stress on prosocial behavior, contradicting the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports empirical associations between daily stress and prosocial behavior (including moderation effects) and explicitly cites experimental work (e.g., von Dawans et al., 2012) showing that exposure to an acute laboratory stressor increased trust, sharing, and prosociality. While the authors note limitations in temporal ordering for their daily-diary data, the paper and its citations provide evidence consistent with a direct effect of stress on prosocial behavior, so the claim of no established direct relationship is contradicted by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943", "verdict": "UNA