   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) through a direct causal relationship. Increased perceived stress can lead individuals to withdraw socially or feel isolated, thereby increasing loneliness. Conversely, lower perceived stress may help individuals feel more connected and less lonely. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.2583313032695332, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7422698348536332, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review indicates evidence that stress and loneliness are related and that stress may play a co‑causal or prodromic role in loneliness, so a general positive link (higher perceived stress associated with greater loneliness) is supported. However, the article emphasizes biological and correlational findings and does not provide evidence for the specific psychological mechanism proposed (that stressed individuals conclude others are “too busy” or that social opportunities are objectively limited rather than experiencing internal change or behavioral effects). The specific perception-of-others/belief-in-scarcity pathway is not addressed in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content. There is no accessible evidence in the cited source to support (or contradict) the proposed causal link between perceived stress and loneliness."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links maladaptive social cognitions and altered social perceptions (e.g., increased vigilance to social threat, negative interpretation of ambiguous social cues, reduced trust) with higher loneliness, and notes that interventions targeting these cognitions reduce loneliness — which supports the general idea that perceptions about others and social opportunities influence loneliness. The paper also describes relationships among stress, HPA activation/inflammation, and social disconnection, but emphasizes bidirectionality and that causal direction is unclear. The specific claim that higher perceived stress (PSS) causally leads people to believe others are \\"too busy\\" (i.e., perceive objective scarcity of social opportunities) as the primary mechanism is not directly documented in the cited article."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) because individuals experiencing higher levels of PSS are more likely to perceive that others are too busy or preoccupied to engage with them, leading them to believe that social opportunities are objectively limited. This perception of a scarcity of available social connections, rather than any change in their own behavior or internal feelings, directly results in higher loneliness (UCLS). Conversely, those with lower PSS are more likely to believe that numerous social opportunities are readily accessible, thereby reducing feelings of loneliness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) because individuals experiencing higher levels of PSS are more likely to perceiv