56. Rumination (CERQ-R)-[NEGATIVE]->Perceived social support (MSPSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Rumination (CERQ-R) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased rumination reduces an individual's perceived social support by fostering social withdrawal and negative interpretations of social interactions. Conversely, decreased rumination can enhance perceptions of available support. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the listed CLD.", 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.27095647626635955, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5407371/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7741140898749003, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines perceived social support (MSPSS), perceived stress, and mental health outcomes but does not measure or discuss rumination or the CERQ-R. It therefore provides no evidence for the specific claim that rumination reduces peoples’ ability to recognize support or leads to an objective reduction in support from others, nor for the proposed cognitive mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5407371/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article and its references report that rumination is associated with poorer interpersonal outcomes: prior work (e.g., Abela et al., Flynn et al., Lyubomirsky & Nolen‑Hoeksema) is cited showing higher rumination correlates with lower perceived social support and with maladaptive support‑seeking (co‑rumination, excessive reassurance‑seeking) that can strain relationships. Vélez et al. also show rumination moderates how beneficial support‑seeking is, and discuss plausible interpersonal mechanisms (behaviors that prompt withdrawal). However, the sources do not provide direct evidence for the specific cognitive mechanism claimed here (that rumination \\"directly impairs cognitive capacity to recognize supportive behaviors\\" or that others respond \\"primarily to the individual’s internal cognitive state\\" producing an objective reduction in support). Also the study uses a different rumination measure and measures support‑seeking/perceived support in adolescents rather than the exact CERQ‑R → MSPSS pathway asserted. Thus the general claim that rumination relates to reduced perceived/received support is supported; the detailed causal cognitive‑processing mechanism and the exact measures named are not demonstrated in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses the importance, effects, and neurobiological mediators of social support and notes links between social support, coping, and perception of support, but it does not address rumination (CERQ‑R) or evidence that persistent self‑focused rumination reduces the ability to recognize support or causes an objective reduction in support from others. The specific mechanisms claimed in the motivation (rumination impairing processing of external supportive behaviors and prompting others to withdraw support) are not examined or evidenced in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines perceived social support (MSPSS), perceived stress (PSS), and mental health outcomes, but it does not measure or discuss rumination or the CERQ-R. It therefore provides no empirical evidence for the specific claim that rumination reduces perceived (or received) social support via impaired processing of others\' support or by causing an objective reduction in support from one\'s network."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) negatively impacts Perceived social support (MSPSS) because persistent self-focused thought patterns directly impair individuals’ cognitive capacity to recognize supportive behaviors from others. As rumination intensifies, individuals become less able to process external stimuli, which leads to an objective reduction in the actual support offered by their social network. Therefore, lower rumination enhances social environments, increasing the likelihood of receiving tangible support, as others respond primarily to the individual’s internal cognitive state.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) negatively impacts Perceived social support (MSPSS) because persistent self-focused thought patterns directly impair individuals’ cognitive capacity to recognize supportive behaviors from others. As rumination intensifies, individuals become less able to process external stimuli, which leads to an objective reduction in the actual support offered by their social network. Therefore, lower rumination enhances social environments, increasing the likelihood of receiving tangible support, as others respond primarily to the individual’s internal cognitive state.', 'perplexity': 1.4305611320899652, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8266807554517623, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0014725761344793703, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003332720747414579, 'prob_variance': 0.07341741203068226, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9120225637694951, 'avg_nll': 0.3580667673659583}

