   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to decrease through direct causal mechanisms, such as reductions in stress reactivity and improved emotion regulation. Increases in mindfulness are causally linked to lower levels of inflammation, while decreases in mindfulness can cause higher proinflammatory activity. This relationship is supported by evidence that mindfulness interventions reduce levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5037002/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4624080/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-020-62274-7', 'https://teams.semel.ucla.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Mindfulness meditation and the immune system.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9646928/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5037002/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Walsh et al., 2016) reports that a brief mindfulness intervention produced reductions in salivary IL-6 and TNF-α (not increases), with mindfulness predicting lower post-treatment cytokine levels versus control. The paper discusses mindfulness as reducing stress-related inflammatory responses (e.g., via reduced rumination or social-evaluative threat), so the provided citation contradicts the motivation that mindfulness heightens vigilance and thereby raises proinflammatory markers."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4624080/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited MBSR study reports mindfulness scores increased while IL-6 decreased with greater attendance and found no significant increases in TNF-α. The intervention emphasized nonjudgmental, accepting body awareness (not heightened threat vigilance). Thus the provided citation contradicts the claim that practicing mindfulness raises vigilance and increases proinflammatory markers."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9646928/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The randomized trial citation provides direct evidence that an 8-week MBSR program increased LPS‑stimulated IL‑6 production among lonely older adults (supporting a positive link to IL‑6). The paper does not report increases in TNF‑α (it was not measured) and only notes TNF‑α in background as a related cytokine. Crucially, the cited article does not support the proposed psychological mechanism (that mindfulness increases self‑awareness → vigilance about bodily threats → immune stimulation); in fact, the authors describe mindfulness as fostering equanimity and reduced social threat and discuss alternative mechanisms (restored immunocompetence, glucocorticoid signaling) rather than heightened threat vigilance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-020-62274-7", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited trial found no evidence that the mindfulness intervention increased proinflammatory markers; mindfulness training did not significantly affect cytokines or immune cell measures (TNF-α/IL-6 were either not changed or below detection), and the paper cites reviews showing either reductions or null effects of mindfulness on TNF‑α and no consistent change in IL‑6. The sources do not support the claim that mindfulness practice raises vigilance and thereby increases TNF‑α or IL‑6."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://teams.semel.ucla.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Mindfulness meditation and the immune system.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review of 20 randomized trials does not report mindfulness increasing proinflammatory markers. On the contrary, it finds tentative evidence of reductions in proinflammatory processes (e.g., NF-κB activity and CRP) and notes either decreases or null/mixed findings for IL-6 and TNF-α (some individual studies showed reductions, none show a consistent increase). Thus the provided citations do not support the motivation that mindfulness practice raises TNF-α and IL-6 via heightened bodily vigilance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness increases self-awareness, which can lead individuals to become more vigilant about potential bodily threats. This heightened vigilance may actually stimulate the immune system, resulting in increased levels of proinflammatory markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, as the body remains on high alert for possible stressors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness increases self-awareness, which can lead individuals to become more vigilant about potential bodily threats. This heightened vigilance may actually stimulate the immune system, resulting in increased levels of proinflammatory markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, as the body remains on high alert for possible stressors.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

