   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing mindfulness directly reduces perceived stress by enhancing present-moment awareness and emotional regulation. Conversely, decreasing mindfulness directly increases perceived stress. This relationship is supported by well-established psychological mechanisms and does not appear to be mediated by other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.22232531825804272, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0272735819302971', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6878205/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8189426527238896, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides substantial evidence that higher mindfulness (including FFMQ-assessed facets) is associated with lower perceived stress and that mindfulness training (e.g., MBSR) reduces perceived stress (including RCTs and mediation analyses showing increases in self-reported mindfulness predict decreases in perceived stress), supporting a negative and partly causal relation. However, the review explicitly discusses multiple cognitive and emotion-regulation mechanisms (reperceiving/decentering, attentional control, acceptance, exposure, changes in appraisal-related neural activity) as mediators of mindfulness effects. It does not support the claim that the effect operates independently of cognitive appraisal processes or that emotional regulation is unnecessary; in fact, evidence cited implicates those processes. The specific mechanistic wording in the motivation (that increased mindfulness works chiefly by focusing on internal states to reinterpret physiological arousal as non‑threatening, and lower mindfulness increases susceptibility to external stressors) is not uniquely supported by the review and is inconsistent with its emphasis on multiple overlapping mechanisms (attention, acceptance, decentering, emotion regulation)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6878205/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis provides strong evidence that higher trait mindfulness (FFMQ) is robustly negatively correlated with negative affective symptoms (anxiety, depression; large pooled r’s), supporting an inverse association in general. However, the study did not examine perceived stress (PSS) measures (it excluded stress/state affect measures), does not establish causality (authors explicitly note the correlational design), and discusses mechanisms involving decentering/emotion regulation and appraisal processes rather than claiming the effect operates independently of cognitive appraisal or without emotion-regulation mediation. The specific causal mechanism claimed (reinterpreting physiological arousal as non‑threatening via increased internal focus, independent of appraisal/regulation) is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0272735819302971", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no usable content (returns an error message), so it does not supply any evidence for the claimed inverse causal relationship or the specific mechanisms (reinterpretation of arousal, independence from appraisal/emotional regulation)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing mindfulness leads individuals to focus more intently on