61. Mindfulness (FFMQ)-[POSITIVE]->Rumination (CERQ-R)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased mindfulness promotes present-moment awareness and acceptance, which directly reduces the tendency to engage in repetitive, negative thought patterns characteristic of rumination. Conversely, decreased mindfulness leads to greater rumination. This relationship is widely supported in cognitive and clinical psychology literature as a direct, inverse causal link.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4829487/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9737922/', 'https://ogg.osu.edu/media/documents/MB%20Stream/FFMQ.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4829487/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article evaluates the FFMQ\'s construct validity, its change after MBSR/active control, and correlations with general psychological well-being and symptom distress (SCL-90R). It does not assess rumination (e.g., CERQ-R rumination) nor report that mindfulness practice increases dwelling on negative thoughts. In fact the FFMQ total correlated negatively with general symptom distress, which is opposite to the claimed increase in maladaptive internalizing. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence for the proposed positive relationship between FFMQ-measured mindfulness and rumination."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9737922/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review and meta-analysis of 19 RCTs found mindfulness-based interventions increased mindfulness (FFMQ) and overall decreased rumination (pooled SMD = −0.46, p < 0.001). While a few individual trials showed no difference (or non-significant negative effects), the pooled evidence in the citation indicates mindfulness reduces — not increases — rumination, contradicting the stated motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Feldman et al., 2013) directly assessed FFMQ facets and rumination subscales (brooding and reflection) and does not report a positive relationship between overall mindfulness and maladaptive rumination. Instead, acting with awareness (an FFMQ facet) was associated with adaptive reflection (not brooding) and predicted greater distress-tolerance; non-judgment and non-reactivity were linked to less subjective negative affect. Brooding (the maladaptive, dwelling form of rumination emphasized in the motivation) was not shown to increase with higher FFMQ scores. Thus the citation does not support the claim that mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ increases rumination; if anything the results contradict that assertion."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ogg.osu.edu/media/documents/MB%20Stream/FFMQ.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation only contains the FFMQ questionnaire and scoring (measurement of mindfulness facets such as Observing, Nonjudging, etc.). It does not report data, analyses, or discussion linking FFMQ scores to rumination (CERQ-R) or claiming that increased internal awareness leads to greater rumination. Therefore the cited source does not support the asserted positive relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ can actually make people more aware of their internal thoughts, which could inadvertently increase rumination. By focusing attention inward during mindfulness exercises, individuals might dwell more on their worries and negative thoughts,