   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) to decrease in an inverse causal relationship. Increases in mindfulness are known to reduce sleep disturbances by promoting relaxation and reducing rumination. Conversely, decreases in mindfulness can lead to greater sleep disturbance. This relationship is not mediated by other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5866834/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7350530/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6878205/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5866834/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that higher scores on the Observe facet of the FFMQ (attention/awareness) were associated with increased psychological distress and worse global PSQI scores — but critically only when acceptance (Nonreact) was low. Psychological distress partially mediated the Observe → poor sleep relationship, and the authors discuss rumination/arousal as a plausible mechanism. However, the paper also shows overall FFMQ (total mindfulness) is associated with better sleep, and the adverse Observe → worse-sleep effect depends on low acceptance. The study is cross-sectional so causality is not established. Thus the citations support the mechanism for an awareness facet under specific conditions (low acceptance), not the blanket claim that higher mindfulness (FFMQ total) increases sleep disturbance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7350530/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited FFMQ psychometric study reports that higher FFMQ scores are associated with lower depression and lower rumination (and better emotion regulation). It provides no