n intervention to reduce cue-reactivity, craving, and smoking frequency or to help decouple cues from smoking behavior. Multiple cited studies report reduced cue-induced craving or smoking following mindfulness practice; none of the provided sources support the claim that greater mindfulness increases the sensory enjoyment of smoking and thereby raises smoking behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-031-47219-0_15", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited FFMQ source describes the questionnaire, its subscales, and psychometric properties. It does not report data or findings showing that higher FFMQ-measured mindfulness increases awareness of pleasurable sensations from smoking or leads to increased smoking behavior. (A referenced paper on mindfulness and substance use is listed, but no results are provided in the supplied content to support the claimed relationship.)"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9379793/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited trial protocol and referenced studies describe mindfulness (and FFMQ-measured facets) as linked to greater smoking cessation, reduced craving/withdrawal, weakened links between affect/craving and smoking, and improved lapse recovery. They do not report that greater mindfulness enhances enjoyment of smoking or increases smoking; on the contrary, the evidence cited frames mindfulness as reducing smoking-related behavior."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased mindfulness, as measured by the FFMQ, can heighten awareness of pleasurable sensations, making individuals more attuned to the enjoyment they derive from smoking. This enhanced sensory awareness may inadvertently reinforce and increase smoking behavior, as mindful individuals might seek out smoking more often to savor the experience.', 'corrupt