_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1451138/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a positive association between dispositional mindfulness (measured with MAAS) and prosocial behavior (self-report altruism), with empathy (perspective-taking and empathic concern) mediating that relationship and cultural moderation for some pathways. This supports the claim’s core direction (higher mindfulness linked to greater prosociality). However, the paper does not use the FFMQ, does not measure prosociality via a public goods game (PGG), does not provide causal evidence (cross‑sectional design), and does not support the specific mechanism proposed (self-reflection → conformity to social norms driving group cooperation). Those aspects of the motivation are unaddressed or inconsistent with the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8148411/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article directly assesses trait mindfulness with the FFMQ and tests actual monetary prosocial behavior (donations, dictator game). It finds no general positive relationship between FFMQ scores and prosocial giving (and in some samples/ facets even negative associations), and explicitly cautions that the relation is more complex and not clearly positive. The paper does not provide evidence that mindfulness increases conformity to social norms or cooperation in a public goods game (PGG)—it did not test PGGs or the proposed self‑reflection→conformity mechanism—and it notes the data are correlational (no causal claim). Therefore the positive causal claim (higher FFMQ → increased PGG cooperation via conformity) is not supported and is contradicted by the findings."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0749597817308956", "verdict": "UNAD