   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing perceived social support directly reduces loneliness, while decreasing perceived social support increases loneliness. This relationship is direct, widely supported in theory and evidence, and does not appear to be fully mediated by other variables in the provided set.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.279500834025254, 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7760767751482396, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides evidence of a negative association between perceived social support (MSPSS) and aspects of loneliness (particularly romantic loneliness) and shows buffering/moderating effects of support. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism proposed (that greater perceived support increases social activity and obligations, reduces introspection/self-reflection, and thereby lowers subjective loneliness). The paper does not measure social activity, social obligations, or introspective/self-reflective processes, and its cross-sectional design precludes causal claims about displacement of introspection."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation content is an access-denied/technical-error page (ResearchGate temporarily unavailable) and contains no substantive data or findings. Therefore the sources do not provide any evidence for the proposed mechanism (social activity → obligations → reduced introspection → lower loneliness) or for the specific displacement-of-introspection explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no substantive content (an error message), so it gives no evidence about perceived social support, loneliness, or the proposed introspection-displacement mechanism. Therefore the motivation is not supported by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) exerts a negative influence on loneliness (UCLS) primarily because individuals with higher levels of perceived social support are more likely to engage in frequent social activities, which leads to increased social obligations and, paradoxically, less time spent in introspection. This reduction in self-reflection directly diminishes the subjective experience of loneliness (UCLS), as individuals become distracted from recognizing feelings of isolation. Thus, the inverse relationship between perceived social support (MSPSS) and loneliness (UCLS) is driven by the displacement of introspective processes rather than any direct effect of social support on emotional well-being.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) exerts a negative influence on loneliness (UCLS) primarily because individuals with higher levels of perceived social support are more likely to engage in frequent social activities, which leads to increased social obligations and, paradoxically, less time spent in introspection. This reduction in self-reflection directly diminishes the subjective experience of loneliness (UCLS), as individuals become distracted from recognizing feelings of isolation. Thus, the inverse relationship between perceived social support (MSPSS) and loneliness (UCLS) is driven by the displacement of introspective processes rather than any direct effect of social support on emotional well-being.', 'perplexity': 1.4090002117588092, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8155938334994569, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01538897804949971, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005002949804000342, 'prob_variance': 0.07812071622081258, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0665909514197354, 'avg_nll': 0.34288038320667324}

