   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased loneliness is associated with reduced IL-10 production due to heightened stress and immune dysregulation, while decreased loneliness can enhance IL-10-mediated anti-inflammatory activity. This relationship is supported by psychoneuroimmunology evidence linking social isolation to impaired anti-inflammatory responses.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12974-016-0763-8', 'https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/hea-hea0000594.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study directly measured basal and LPS-stimulated cytokines (including IL-10) and found no significant associations between either trait or momentary loneliness and the basal or stimulated cytokine composites, and exploratory analyses of individual cytokines (such as IL-10) were also non-significant. Instead, loneliness was associated with higher CRP (a pro-inflammatory marker). Thus the claim that loneliness stimulates production of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 is not supported by this source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study measured IL-10 (among other cytokines) and found no significant associations between loneliness and basal or LPS-stimulated cytokines. Instead, loneliness was related to higher