   Properties: {'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.2802690802853798, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5025448/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7102549703623184, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5025448/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article includes an experiment (Study 2) that manipulated loneliness and found corresponding changes in willingness to help (prosocial behavior), demonstrating a causal effect of loneliness on prosocial behavior that is moderated by situation (public vs. private) and gender. While the paper notes possible mediators and limitations, it does present direct experimental evidence of causation rather than supporting the claim that no direct causal mechanism exists."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports experimental evidence that loneliness priming causally reduced prosocial choices (Study 1: between-subject loneliness prime lowered eco-friendly/PGG-type choices; Study 2: within-subject loneliness prime similarly reduced prosocial choices and linked ERP markers to those choices). Those findings support a direct effect of acute loneliness cues on prosocial behavior, contradicting the claim that no direct causal link is established."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Hawkley & Cacioppo review describes loneliness as producing cognitive, affective, and behavioral changes (hypervigilance for social threat, biased social cognition, impaired self-regulation) that influence downstream behavior via mediating processes. It notes loneliness heightens the desire to reconnect but also elicits distancing and other complex social behaviors. The paper does not present evidence of a simple, immediate direct causal link from loneliness to increased prosocial behavior (e.g., in PGG-type measures); rather it emphasizes indirect pathways and contextual moderators. Because the citation addresses indirect mechanisms and mixed behavioral outcomes but does not explicitly test or rule out a direct effect on prosociality, the claim of \\"no direct causal link\\" is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article includes an experiment (Study 2) that manipulated loneliness and found that induced loneliness changed willingness to help (prosocial behavior), with effects moderated by public/private situation and gender. The authors treat loneliness as causally influencing prosocial responding in specific contexts, so the claim that loneliness does not directly cause prosocial behavior and that any association is only indirect/confounded is contradicted by the provided experimental evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports two experimental studies where loneliness was experimentally manipulated (loneliness priming) and resulted in significantly reduced prosocial behavior (modified PGG / eco-friendly choices) and correlated neural responses (N400, P300). These findings provide direct causal evidence that exposure to loneliness cues reduces prosocial behavior, contradicting the claim of no direct causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) does not directly cause Prosocial behavior (PGG). There is no established direct causal mechanism by which changes in loneliness would immediately or inherently lead to changes in prosocial behavior without mediation by other variables such as perceived social support or depressive symptoms. Any observed association is likely due to indirect pathways or confounding factors, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4155991781299082, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7867877981721032, 'min_prob': 0.11659456483303268, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002133070872773011, 'prob_variance': 0.07855075736401265, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3718254254729008, 'avg_nll': 0.3475528889082654}

