   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased loneliness directly leads to decreased perceived social support, as individuals who feel lonelier tend to perceive less support from others. Conversely, decreased loneliness is causally linked to increased perceived social support.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12888-018-1736-5', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5104760/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691825006286'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12888-018-1736-5", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review reports that loneliness and perceived social support are related but typically show large negative correlations (greater loneliness associated with poorer perceived social support). The review’s summarized empirical findings and discussion do not provide evidence that higher loneliness is associated with higher perceived social support; rather they indicate the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error and provides no usable content, so there is no evidence in the provided citation to evaluate or support the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691825006286", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error and contains no accessible content, so there is no evidence in the provided citations to support (or contradict) the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5104760/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that greater loneliness (measured with SELSA‑S) is associated with lower perceived support from significant others and family (single participants had higher romantic loneliness and lower MSPSS scores for significant other/family), and regression analyses show perceived support (especially from significant others) is negatively related to romantic loneliness. The paper therefore supports a negative — not positive — association between loneliness and perceived social support; it does not support the proposed mechanism that higher loneliness leads to higher perceived support. (The study also uses SELSA‑S for loneliness rather than the UCLA scale mentioned in the motivation.)"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Experiencing loneliness can actually make people more aware of their social needs, prompting them to perceive even small acts of kindness as significant support. Therefore, higher loneliness might correlate with higher perceived social support, as individuals become more sensitive to positive social interactions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Experiencing loneliness can actually make people more aware of their social needs, prompting them to perceive even small acts of kindness as significant support. Therefore, higher loneliness might correlate with higher perceived social support, as individuals become more sensitive to positive social interactions.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

