98. Loneliness (UCLS)-[POSITIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.28815809084271543, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23630220/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8014467154346091, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review reports consistent associations between loneliness and higher levels of pro-inflammatory markers (explicitly IL-6, CRP, fibrinogen and generally \\"pro-inflammatory cytokines\\") and describes plausible stress-related pathways (HPA activation, Social Safety Theory) and animal experiments where social isolation increases pro-inflammatory cytokines. However, it also states causality in humans remains unresolved (the relationship may be bidirectional), notes a lack of direct evidence that reducing loneliness lowers these biomarkers, and does not clearly document TNF‑alpha increases in humans. Thus the general link to inflammation and stress pathways is supported, but the strong direct causal claim (including TNF‑alpha and reversal by reducing loneliness) is not fully supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and contains no accessible content or data about loneliness, TNF‑alpha, or IL‑6. Therefore the citation does not provide any evidence for the claimed causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review reports links between perceived social isolation (loneliness), stress-related biological factors, and immune-system components, indicating associations between loneliness and physiological stress/immune measures. However, it emphasizes that most findings are correlational, under-researched, and does not provide direct, conclusive evidence that loneliness causally increases specific proinflammatory markers (TNF-alpha, IL-6) or that reducing loneliness directly decreases them. The citation therefore supports an association but not the strong causal, marker-specific claims in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study measured loneliness and a panel of inflammatory markers including IL-6 and TNF-α in older adults and found that higher loneliness was associated with higher CRP but \\"there were no significant associations between loneliness and basal or stimulated cytokines\\" (which include IL-6 and TNF-α). In addition, the study is observational/cross-sectional, so it does not support the causal claim that reducing loneliness directly decreases these cytokine levels."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23630220/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper reports that lonelier participants showed greater LPS-stimulated production of IL-6 (in both studies) and greater TNF-α production in Study 1 (but not in Study 2), and these effects were observed in the context of acute stress — consistent with a stress-related proinflammatory phenotype. However, the studies document associations in response to stress rather than a demonstrated causal effect of experimentally manipulating loneliness, TNF-α findings are not consistent across both samples, and no evidence is provided that reducing loneliness reduces these markers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through activation of stress-related biological pathways. Social isolation and perceived loneliness trigger physiological stress responses, elevating inflammatory markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing loneliness directly decreases these proinflammatory processes. This is a direct positive causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.357219736374002, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8333302365868627, 'min_prob': 0.08278921643946109, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0033918450006394114, 'prob_variance': 0.08303508531811865, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9080288535825858, 'avg_nll': 0.3054382958012804}

99. Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived social support (MSPSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased depressive symptoms can causally lead individuals to withdraw socially, perceive less support, or interpret social interactions more negatively, thus reducing perceived social support. Conversely, lower depressive symptoms may enhance perceptions of available support. This is a direct negative causal relationship, not merely a correlation.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2018.02780/full', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274570/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10689368/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article and referenced literature report that greater perceived social support (MSPSS) is associated with lower depressive symptoms (e.g., family support r = −0.30 with depression) and frame social support as protective against depression. The study’s results and citations describe negative associations and mediation whereby support reduces stress and depression, not the opposite pattern of higher depression eliciting higher perceived support. The sources do not provide evidence for a positive association in which increased depressive symptoms lead to increased perceived social support."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274570/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that greater perceived social support is associated with lower odds of depression (protective, ORs < 1) and discusses social support as buffering against depression. It does not provide evidence that increased depressive symptoms lead to increased perceived social support; in fact the observed association is inverse. The cross‑sectional design also precludes the directional claim in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10689368/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited longitudinal CLPN study finds a unidirectional protective effect of social support on depressive symptoms (social support → decreased depression) and explicitly reports no significant longitudinal edges from depressive symptoms to social support. Thus the provided evidence contradicts the claim that increased depressive symptoms lead to greater perceived social support."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2018.02780/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that higher perceived social support (MSPSS) is associated with lower depressive symptoms (IDS/CES-D), not higher — family and friend support were negatively related to depression. The paper only mentions, as background, hypotheses (e.g., stress-mobilizing) or possible spurious positive associations in prior literature, but its empirical results do not support the claim that increased depressive symptoms lead to greater perceived social support."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports that greater perceived social support is associated with lower depressive symptoms (negative correlations and social support predicting reduced depression) and frames social support as a protective predictor that reduces stress and depression. It provides no evidence that higher depression leads to increased perceived social support; thus the claimed positive association (more depressive symptoms → more perceived support) is contradicted by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms can lead individuals to reach out more to friends and family, which in turn enhances their perception of social support. As people experience more depressive symptoms, their social networks typically become more attentive and supportive, resulting in a positive association between depressive symptoms and perceived social support.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms can lead individuals to reach out more to friends and family, which in turn enhances their perception of social support. As people experience more depressive symptoms, their social networks typically become more attentive and supportive, resulting in a positive association between depressive symptoms and perceived social support.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

