ivity; rather, it reports the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1598936/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited longitudinal study finds depressive symptoms predict lower subsequent physical exercise (cross-lag β = −0.07 and direct effect β = −0.11) and reports negative correlations (r ≈ −0.13) between depressive symptoms and physical exercise. The paper interprets this as depression reducing exercise (via reduced motivation, social support, and resilience). That evidence contradicts the motivation\'s claim that higher depressive symptoms lead to increased physical activity (decreased physical inactivity)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Major_depressive_disorder", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Wikipedia article describes core features of depression as low mood, loss of interest, withdrawal from activities and reduced energy (fatigue, lethargy) — i.e., decreased activity — although it notes psychomotor agitation can occur in some cases. While exercise is recommended as a treatment, the source does not support the claim that increasing depressive symptoms commonly cause people to become more active; on the contrary, reduced activity is presented as a typical manifestation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms often lead individuals to become more restless and agitated, prompting them to engage in more physical activities as a means to cope with their mood. Therefore, higher depressive symptoms would actually decrease physical inactivity, resulting in a negative association between the two.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms often lead individuals to become mor