RTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review extensively documents a positive association between depressive symptom severity and elevated proinflammatory cytokines (including IL-6 and TNF-α), and describes plausible neuroimmune mechanisms (HPA-axis dysregulation, DAMP‑PRR signaling, BBB disruption, microglial activation) linking stress, inflammation, and depressive phenotypes. It also cites preclinical causal evidence (stress or cytokine manipulations producing depressive-like behavior) and clinical findings that antidepressant or anti‑inflammatory interventions can alter cytokine levels. However, the review explicitly notes the evidence is largely observational and emphasizes bidirectional and complex relations rather than a simple, direct causal claim that depressive symptoms per se cause increases in IL‑6 and TNF‑α. Thus the citations support a positive association and mechanistic plausibility but do not unequivocally establish the unidirectional causal statement in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase via neuroimmune mechanisms. Higher levels of depressive symptoms can directly upregulate inflammatory cytokines, leading to increased TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing depressive symptoms can lower these proinflammatory markers. This is a direct, positive causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3181560239014132, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8311279662429205, 'min_prob': 0.12053298224109656, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0015528124617498326, 'prob_variance': 0.07071509297325437, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1094551686408467, 'avg_nll': 0.27623380836191097}

