120. Sleep disturbance (PSQI)-[NEGATIVE]->Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased sleep disturbance is linked with a reduction in IL-10 production, as poor sleep impairs anti-inflammatory immune function. Conversely, improved sleep quality can increase IL-10 levels. This relationship is supported by evidence showing sleep disruption leads to suppressed anti-inflammatory cytokine activity.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.2893742518751635, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/', 'https://www.psychiatry.pitt.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/PSQI%20Article.pdf', 'https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5143488/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7587186951846719, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited cross‑sectional study reports an inverse association between PSQI (poor sleep) and serum IL‑10 levels (poor sleepers had lower IL‑10; PSQI correlated with IL‑10 and IL‑10 was an independent predictor). However, the study is observational and cross‑sectional in a hemodialysis population, so it does not establish directionality or the proposed causal mechanism (that IL‑10 changes disrupt central circadian regulation and thereby cause sleep disturbance). The citation supports an association (negative relationship) but not the causal pathway or temporal precedence claimed in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation only describes the PSQI instrument (what it measures, scoring, and validity). It contains no data or discussion about IL-10, anti-inflammatory processes, or mechanistic links between IL-10 and sleep/circadian regulation, so the proposed negative causal relationship is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is a methodological review of the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI) covering its history, structure, validity and applications. It does not report data on IL‑10, anti‑inflammatory processes, or biological mechanisms linking IL‑10 to circadian regulation or sleep disturbance. Therefore the citation does not support (nor contradict) the claimed negative causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5143488/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that sleep disturbance is linked to increased proinflammatory signaling (IL‑1, IL‑6, TNF, NF‑κB, STAT activation, CRP) and discusses bidirectional CNS–immune interactions. It explicitly notes that anti‑inflammatory cytokines (e.g., IL‑10) have been under‑studied and does not provide data showing that sleep disturbance causes reductions in IL‑10 or that lowered IL‑10 directly disrupts circadian regulation of sleep. The specific pathway claimed (PSQI → decreased IL‑10 → impaired circadian/sleep‑architecture regulation) is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease because lower levels of IL-10 directly interfere with the brain's ability to regulate circadian rhythms, leading to disruptions in sleep architecture. As IL-10 levels fall, the central nervous system loses crucial anti-inflammatory signaling needed to synchronize sleep cycles, resulting in increased sleep disturbance as measured by PSQI. Therefore, a reduction in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) precedes and promotes the onset of sleep problems, establishing a biologically plausible pathway from immune modulation to sleep dysregulation.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease because lower levels of IL-10 directly interfere with the brain's ability to regulate circadian rhythms, leading to disruptions in sleep architecture. As IL-10 levels fall, the central nervous system loses crucial anti-inflammatory signaling needed to synchronize sleep cycles, resulting in increased sleep disturbance as measured by PSQI. Therefore, a reduction in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) precedes and promotes the onset of sleep problems, establishing a biologically plausible pathway from immune modulation to sleep dysregulation.", 'perplexity': 1.440417899238344, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8041494334056366, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.027627787218500427, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004363812428879458, 'prob_variance': 0.08373745764831059, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2389189438132644, 'avg_nll': 0.3649332792901754}

