orts cross-sectional associations (PSQI correlates negatively with MSPSS) and models treating perceived social support as a predictor of sleep, finding higher support linked to lower odds of poor sleep. It does not provide evidence that sleep disturbance causes changes in perceived social support, nor test a direct causal pathway from PSQI → MSPSS. The authors discuss potential confounding and reciprocal/complex relationships (including links with depression and loneliness) and cite literature consistent with social support influencing sleep rather than the reverse. Thus the sources do not support a causal effect of sleep disturbance on perceived social support, but they also do not directly demonstrate the absence of any causal mechanism or fully establish mediation by depression/loneliness — so the motivation is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8358441/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is a psychometric validation of the MSPSS reporting reliability and cross‑sectional correlations with stress, coping and family functioning. It does not examine sleep disturbance (PSQI), test causal directions, or present evidence that sleep disturbance does not affect perceived social support. It also does not provide analyses showing mediation by depressive symptoms or loneliness. Therefore the sources do not address the asserted \\"no causal effect\\" claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) does not cause perceived social support (MSPSS) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which changes in sleep disturbance directly alter levels of perceived social support. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as depressive symptoms or loneliness.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.232167208405043