sm or reduced calorie burning; in fact, the evidence cited points in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/pittsburgh-sleep-quality-index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an access/error message and contains no usable content. Therefore the citation does not provide any evidence for (or against) the claimed negative relationship or the proposed causal mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved sleep quality, as measured by lower PSQI scores, directly leads to an increase in body mass index (BMI) because deeper sleep slows metabolism and reduces overnight calorie burning. Therefore, better sleep causes people to gain weight, resulting in a negative association between sleep disturbance and BMI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved sleep quality, as measured by lower PSQI scores, directly leads to an increase in body mass index (BMI) because deeper sleep slows metabolism and reduces overnight calorie burning. Therefore, better sleep causes people to gain weight, resulting in a negative association between sleep disturbance and BMI.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

