   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in more prosocial behavior directly reduces feelings of loneliness by increasing social connection and positive social interactions. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior can directly increase loneliness due to reduced opportunities for meaningful social engagement.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.21835118686173557, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/', 'https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/surgeon-general-social-connection-advisory.pdf', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38446286/', 'https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7723615821552738, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article experimentally tests the effect of loneliness on prosocial behavior and finds that exposure to loneliness cues reduces prosocial actions (PGG/eco-friendly choices) and elicits neural markers (N400/P300). It does not provide evidence that increased prosocial behavior causes lower UCLA loneliness scores; indeed, the causal direction shown is loneliness → reduced prosociality. The paper only speculates that prosocial behavior \\"can be a way to combat loneliness\\" but supplies no empirical test that prosocial acts directly immunize people against loneliness regardless of social network or that empathy-driven prosociality causally reduces UCLS."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article experimentally shows that increased loneliness (measured by UCLA) leads to reduced prosocial behavior in a PGG-style task (i.e., loneliness → lower prosociality). It does not provide evidence that greater prosocial behavior reduces loneliness or “immunizes” individuals against loneliness regardless of social networks. Thus the causal direction claimed in the motivation (prosocial behavior causing lower loneliness, independent of social ties) is not supported and is contradicted by the study’s findings."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/surgeon-general-social-connection-advisory.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is inaccessible (\\"Access Denied\\") and contains no viewable content to support the claimed negative relationship. Therefore the source does not provide evidence for the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38446286/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study reports direct (negative) associations between developmental trajectories of prosocial behavior and adolescents\' loneliness, supporting an inverse relationship (more prosocial behavior linked to less loneliness). However, the paper summary does not provide evidence for the additional claims in the motivation — that prosociality is driven by higher empathy/emotional intelligence, that prosocial acts \\"directly immunize\\" individuals regardless of their social interactions or network size, or that lower prosociality reflects lack of intrinsic emotional resilience. Those mechanistic and generalizing assertions are not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Our World in Data source reviews evidence linking social connections (time with friends, social support) to well‑being and notes correlations between social engagement and outcomes, but it explicitly says causal mechanisms and effect sizes are unclear and that there is little credible experimental evidence to claim precise causal effects. The page does not present evidence that prosocial acts (e.g., donating/volunteering) directly \\"immunize\\" people against loneliness regardless of their social network or that higher empathy inherently protects against loneliness. Thus the strong causal claim in the motivation is not supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship. Individuals who engage in more prosocial behavior often do so because they possess higher levels of empathy and emotional intelligence, which inherently protects them from experiencing loneliness. Thus, the act of engaging in prosocial behavior itself directly immunizes individuals against loneliness (UCLS), regardless of their actual social interactions or network size. Conversely, those who are less prosocial are more susceptible to loneliness due to a lack of intrinsic emotional resilience.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship. Individuals who engage in more prosocial behavior often do so because they possess higher levels of empathy and emotional intelligence, which inherently protects them from experiencing loneliness. Thus, the act of engaging in prosocial behavior itself directly immunizes individuals against loneliness (UCLS), regardless of their actual social interactions or network size. Conversely, those who are less prosocial are more susceptible to loneliness due to a lack of intrinsic emotional resilience.', 'perplexity': 1.2366