 shown is cross‑sectional/correlational (not causal). Thus the idea that prosocial actions buffer against stress is supported in part, but the specific causal claim that prosocial behavior causes reductions in perceived stress (as measured by PSS) is not directly established by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited thesis and its references provide multiple studies showing an inverse association and buffering effect of prosocial behavior on stress (e.g., Raposa et al. 2016; Brown & Brown 2014; Whillans et al. 2016; Inagaki et al. 2016; Martela & Ryan 2016) including reductions in perceived negative outcomes, physiological stress markers, and improved well‑being. However, much of the evidence is correlational or context-dependent, some work shows stress can also increase prosociality (Von Dawans et al. 2012), and timing/genetic moderators affect outcomes (Sollberger et al.; von Dawans et al.). Thus the citations support an inverse relationship and stress‑buffering role for prosocial behavior but do not uniformly establish a simple, general causal effect on perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and provides no content or evidence. Therefore the provided citations do not address or support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and contains no accessible content or evidence. Therefore the provided citation does not address or support the claimed negative rela