167. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[POSITIVE]->Mindfulness (FFMQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no evidence that prosocial behavior (PGG) directly and causally causes mindfulness (FFMQ) to change. The causal direction is more plausibly from mindfulness to prosocial behavior, not the reverse. No direct mechanism supports prosocial behavior increasing or decreasing mindfulness. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists from prosocial behavior (PGG) to mindfulness (FFMQ).', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8148411/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/354586681_The_Relation_of_Mindfulness_and_Prosocial_Behavior_What_Do_We_Not_Know', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-31039-3', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8293796/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7180177/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8148411/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article tests the link between trait mindfulness (FFMQ) and actual prosocial behavior (including dictator game measures) and finds no consistent positive relationship — in some samples facets even correlated negatively and overall effects were null or mixed. The paper does not provide evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior increases mindfulness (and explicitly notes its correlative design cannot establish causality); meta-analytic discussion also indicates mixed or null effects of interventions on money-related prosocial behavior. Thus the claim that prosocial acts directly boost FFMQ-measured mindfulness is not supported by these sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8293796/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports that higher trait mindfulness (measured with the FFMQ) predicts greater online prosocial behavior and explores empathy as a mediator. It does not provide evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior increases mindfulness; in fact the study is cross-sectional and the authors explicitly note causal direction cannot be established and call for experimental/longitudinal work to test whether mindfulness training leads to prosociality. The citations do not support the claim that prosocial acts strengthen attention/nonjudgmental awareness or increase FFMQ mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/354586681_The_Relation_of_Mindfulness_and_Prosocial_Behavior_What_Do_We_Not_Know", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is an access-denied/temporary-unavailable ResearchGate page and contains no substantive content about mindfulness, prosocial behavior, or empirical findings. Because the citation provides no usable evidence, the claimed positive causal relationship is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-31039-3", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study examines how dispositional mindfulness (measured with MAAS) relates to prosocial behavior and finds that higher mindfulness can weaken or even reverse links between intelligence and prosociality — not that prosocial actions increase mindfulness. It is cross‑sectional (no causal test that prosocial engagement raises mindfulness), uses MAAS (not the FFMQ), and discusses instances where mindfulness reduces prosocial responding. It therefore does not support — and in some findings contradicts — the claim that engaging in prosocial behavior directly increases mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7180177/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides behavioral evidence that engaging in a brief prosocial imagination task increased mindful attention (MAAS) in high-ruminating participants (Study 1) and that trait prosocial tendency (PTM) correlates with FFMQ scores and moderates the link between reflective pondering and mindfulness (Study 2). However, the paper does not study economic games (no PGG data), does not show effects on FFMQ from experimentally induced prosocial behavior (causal FFMQ increase is not demonstrated), and offers no neurobiological evidence that prosocial acts \\"strengthen brain circuits\\" for attention/nonjudgmental awareness. The prosocial manipulation was an imagined helping task and effects were specific to high ruminators, so the full causal and neural claims in the motivation are not supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as cooperating in economic games like the PGG, directly increases mindfulness by training individuals to focus on the present moment and the needs of others. Regular participation in prosocial acts strengthens the brain circuits responsible for attention and nonjudgmental awareness, thereby boosting overall mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as cooperating in economic games like the PGG, directly increases mindfulness by training individuals to focus on the present moment and the needs of others. Regular participation in prosocial acts strengthens the brain circuits responsible for attention and nonjudgmental awareness, thereby boosting overall mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

