asis for higher BMI) and elevated TNF-α and IL-6."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review and referenced studies report that adipose tissue secretes proinflammatory cytokines (including IL‑6 and TNF‑α), document positive correlations between BMI (and measures of adiposity) and circulating IL‑6 and TNF‑α, describe mechanistic pathways (adipocyte hypertrophy, hypoxia, macrophage infiltration) that can produce these cytokines, and note that weight loss (e.g., after bariatric surgery) is associated with reductions in IL‑6. These sources therefore support the stated positive, plausibly causal relationship between higher BMI and increased proinflammatory markers."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports higher TNF-α in obese versus normal-BMI subjects and higher IL-6 in obese subjects (with the IL-6 difference reaching statistical significance only in females), and states that adipocytes secrete IL-6 and TNF-α and that their concentrations correlate with fat amount/distribution. That supports a positive association and a plausible mechanistic link between greater adiposity and increased proinflammatory cytokines. However, the citation is observational and does not establish a direct causal effect of increasing BMI on cytokine rise nor provide evidence that reducing BMI lowers these markers."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2020.00481/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (Patsalos et al. 2020) and its referenced literature note that adipose tissue produces proinflammatory cytokines and that TNF-α levels correlate with degree of adiposity in obese animals 