e 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports IL‑6 and TNF‑α levels in obesity and their links to CRP, insulin resistance and metabolic processes. It does not examine perceived stress (PSS) or test whether proinflammatory cytokines cause changes in perceived stress, so it does not support or refute the claimed absence of a causal pathway to perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis examines IL-6, TNF-α and IL-1β in relation to age‑related diseases and discusses a \\"stress theory\\" in which excessive stress is associated with increased pro‑inflammatory responses (i.e., stress → inflammation). It does not present evidence that elevated IL‑6 or TNF‑α cause perceived stress (PSS). Thus the citation does not support a direct causal pathway from proinflammatory cytokines to perceived stress and is consistent with the motivation\'s claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) cause perceived stress (PSS). While perceived stress may influence proinflammatory processes, the reverse direction—proinflammatory processes causing perceived stress—is not supported by a clear, direct causal mechanism. Any observed association is likely due to confounding or mediation, not direct causality.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3620332157371293, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8100717000654833, 'min_prob': 0.07231820232266546, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004304246741744645, 'prob_variance': 0.0661160587039806, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1079139015525654, 'avg_nll': 0.30897859490214585}

190. Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)-[NONE]->Body mass index (BMI)
   Properties: {'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.26330727116399116, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1871403X16300060', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7377879188683957, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents higher IL-6 and TNF-α levels in obese subjects and describes mechanistic pathways (e.g., TNF-α effects on adipocyte metabolism, LPL, insulin resistance) that could plausibly affect adiposity, but it does not demonstrate a confirmed direct causal effect of these cytokines on BMI. The authors explicitly state it is difficult to determine whether increased cytokines are a cause or consequence of greater body fat, so the citation supports the claim of no established direct causal pathway while also noting plausible mechanisms and associations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review strongly supports that higher BMI/adiposity drives increased IL-6 and TNF-α (many cross-sectional and mechanistic observations showing adipose tissue secretes these cytokines and levels correlate with BMI, and they fall after weight loss). It does not provide clear human evidence that elevations in IL-6 or TNF-α directly cause increases in BMI. However, the review does note mechanistic roles for cytokines (e.g., IL-6 receptors in the hypothalamus and effects on energy homeostasis) that imply potential causal pathways. Because the citations mainly document BMI → inflammation and only suggest (but do not establish) cytokine → BMI effects, the motivation\'s claim (no established direct causal pathway) is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review and papers consistently describe elevated TNF-α and IL-6 as markers produced by adipose tissue in obesity and emphasize obesity → inflammation (associations and mechanisms in adipose tissue). While some citations note that cytokines can influence metabolic processes (e.g., effects on lipolysis, insulin resistance), none of the provided sources present an established causal pathway showing changes in TNF-α or IL-6 directly produce sustained changes in BMI. Thus the cited literature supports the claim that BMI influences proinflammatory markers rather than that proinflammatory processes directly change BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147638/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study reports higher IL-6 and TNF-α levels in obese versus normal-BMI subjects and describes these cytokines as secreted by adipocytes and correlated with fat percentage/distribution. It documents association and inflammation persistence in obesity but does not provide evidence that changes in these proinflammatory cytokines directly cause BMI to change, supporting the motivation\'s claim of no established direct causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1871403X16300060", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is an error message and provides no substantive information about TNF-alpha, IL-6, or BMI. Therefore the citation neither supports nor contradicts the claimed lack of a direct causal effect."}], "aggregate_score": 0.6, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not directly cause body mass index (BMI) to change. While BMI can influence proinflammatory markers, there is no established causal pathway where changes in proinflammatory processes directly alter BMI. Any association is likely mediated by other variables such as metabolic rate, physical activity, or adiposity, which are not direct effects.', 'aggregate_score': 0.6, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3573420807511685, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093656743591366, 'min_prob': 0.04938850534741535, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041033547837887, 'prob_variance': 0.06933071904782757, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.099049627984482, 'avg_nll': 0.3055284351206193}

