   Properties: {'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.2139833067971082, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028709/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/311534896_Multidimensional_Scale_of_Perceived_Social_Support_MSPSS_-_Scale_Items_and_Scoring_Information', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4074419/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.843739158478722, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028709/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study examines perceived social support/strain as predictors of inflammation (including IL-6) and interprets links as social/psychosocial factors affecting inflammatory processes via stress physiology (HPA/SNS). The authors note limitations in causal inference, acknowledge possible reverse causation/confounding, and do not present or endorse a direct biological mechanism whereby proinflammatory cytokines would change an individual\'s perception of social support. Thus the claim that there is no established direct causal pathway from cytokines to perceived social support and that psychological/behavioral mediators are more plausible is consistent with the article."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4074419/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper validates the MSPSS and examines associations between perceived social support, depression, and intimate partner violence. It does not discuss proinflammatory markers (TNF‑alpha, IL‑6) or causal mechanisms linking inflammation to perceived social support, so the citation does not support or refute the stated claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/311534896_Multidimensional_Scale_of_Perceived_Social_Support_MSPSS_-_Scale_Items_and_Scoring_Information", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1