194. Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)-[POSITIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) cause depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Elevated levels of these proinflammatory cytokines can induce neurobiological changes that increase the likelihood or severity of depressive symptoms. Decreasing proinflammatory processes can correspondingly reduce depressive symptoms. This relationship is supported by mechanistic evidence linking inflammation to changes in mood and behavior.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.24542110010760457, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/', 'https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2680424/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8105082013283982, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents robust associations between depression and elevated proinflammatory markers (including IL‑6 and TNF‑alpha) and discusses lifestyle/biological factors that influence inflammation (obesity, sleep disturbance, stress). It therefore supports the plausibility of links among depressive symptoms, adverse behaviors/conditions, and increased cytokines. However, the review emphasizes inflammation as a cause of (or mediator for) depression and presents limited direct evidence for the specific causal chain asserted in the motivation — that depressive symptoms themselves drive increases in TNF‑alpha and IL‑6 via reduced physical activity and poorer diet, or that treating depression reduces these cytokines by changing those behaviors. Those causal steps are implied but not directly demonstrated in the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2680424/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that depression is associated with elevated proinflammatory cytokines (including IL-6 and TNF-α), that psychosocial stress and depressive symptoms (e.g., fatigue, reduced activity) can amplify inflammatory signaling, and that behavioral factors (exercise, BMI/adiposity) and behavioral interventions (exercise, meditation) influence IL-6 and other inflammatory markers. It also notes antidepressant and anti‑inflammatory treatments can change inflammatory markers. However, the sources do not directly document the specific causal chain asserted (that depressive symptoms produce poorer diet and reduced physical activity which then raise IL-6/TNF-α, and that treating depression lowers cytokines specifically via improving those behaviors). Thus the general idea is supported, but the precise behavioral mediation and causal direction are not fully demonstrated in the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly describes a bidirectional link between depression and inflammation and cites meta-analyses showing elevated TNF-α and IL-6 in depressed patients. It also discusses behavioral pathways (reduced physical activity, poor diet, adiposity) that accompany depression and can raise systemic inflammation, and notes that exercise and lifestyle changes reduce inflammation. However, the citations do not provide clear, direct evidence that interventions aimed solely at reducing depressive symptoms (e.g., standard antidepressant or psychotherapeutic treatments) reliably lower TNF‑α and IL‑6 via improved behaviors; that causal treatment-to-decrease-in-cytokines pathway is more implied/speculative in the review rather than directly demonstrated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) lead to increased proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) through behavioral and lifestyle changes commonly associated with depression. Individuals experiencing higher levels of depressive symptoms often engage in reduced physical activity and poorer dietary habits, both of which are known to elevate proinflammatory cytokines. Consequently, interventions targeting depressive symptoms are expected to directly decrease levels of proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) by promoting healthier behaviors that reduce systemic inflammation. This perspective emphasizes the role of psychological state as a primary driver of immune modulation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) lead to increased proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) through behavioral and lifestyle changes commonly associated with depression. Individuals experiencing higher levels of depressive symptoms often engage in reduced physical activity and poorer dietary habits, both of which are known to elevate proinflammatory cytokines. Consequently, interventions targeting depressive symptoms are expected to directly decrease levels of proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) by promoting healthier behaviors that reduce systemic inflammation. This perspective emphasizes the role of psychological state as a primary driver of immune modulation.', 'perplexity': 1.294941294910158, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8538027230658207, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03310763331958213, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0030953807079987664, 'prob_variance': 0.060231516378026866, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8500566023388381, 'avg_nll': 0.25846536200826153}

195. Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)-[NEGATIVE]->Mindfulness (FFMQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not cause Mindfulness (FFMQ) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increases or decreases in proinflammatory cytokines directly alter mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as stress or mood, rather than a direct causal effect.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'citations': ['https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5037002', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0165178123006212', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9083081/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0306453020305370', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5037002", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper documents that mindfulness training can reduce salivary IL-6 and TNF-α and reviews links between inflammation and depressive/sickness behaviors, but it does not measure trait/state mindfulness with the FFMQ nor present evidence that higher proinflammatory cytokines directly impair the cognitive/neural processes of attention and awareness underlying mindfulness. The specific causal claim (inflammation → lower FFMQ scores via interference with attention/awareness neural pathways) is not tested or demonstrated in the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0165178123006212", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains an error message and no usable content. There is no evidence in the supplied source text to support (or contradict) the claim that TNF-alpha and IL-6 reduce FFMQ mindfulness via interference with neural attention/awareness pathways."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0306453020305370", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no accessible content to evaluate. Therefore the provided citation does not supply any evidence for or against the claimed negative relationship between proinflammatory cytokines and FFMQ mindfulness scores."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9083081/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review discusses IL-6 as a stress-related proinflammatory biomarker and reports studies where mindfulness training is associated with reduced IL-6 and with changes in brain functional connectivity linked to attention, self-awareness and emotion regulation (e.g., Creswell et al., Taren et al.). It thus provides some indirect evidence of a relationship between inflammation (IL-6) and neural substrates relevant to mindfulness. However, the citations do not provide direct evidence that increased IL-6 or TNF-alpha causally \\"inhibit the cognitive processes involved in mindfulness\\" or lead to lower FFMQ scores. TNF-alpha is not addressed, and the papers report correlations/associations (and training-related reductions in IL-6) rather than demonstrating that elevated cytokines directly impair attention/awareness or reduce trait mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that IL-6 and TNF-α are elevated in obesity and describes metabolic/inflammatory effects (e.g., CRP correlation, insulin resistance) and adipose tissue sources. It does not examine or provide evidence about effects on cognition, neural attention/awareness pathways, or mindfulness (FFMQ) scores, so the claimed direct inhibitory link to mindfulness is not