late inflammation. It does not provide evidence that increases in anti‑inflammatory markers (e.g., IL‑10) directly cause higher perceived social support (MSPSS). The review discusses effects of inflammation on social perception and behavior, but not a causal role for anti‑inflammatory processes increasing perceived social support."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121005985", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains an error message and no usable content. There is no evidence in the supplied source material to support (or contradict) the claimed positive causal link between IL-10 and perceived social support."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8388310/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study did not measure anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 and found no association between perceived social support (MSPSS) and systemic inflammatory markers (hsCRP, WBC, fibrinogen). It is cross-sectional (so provides no evidence of a causal effect of inflammation on perceived support) and offers no mechanistic data on anti‑inflammatory cytokines enhancing social perception. Thus the claim that higher anti‑inflammatory processes (e.g., IL‑10) cause greater perceived social support is not supported and is contradicted by the reported lack of association."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6158086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies focus on effects of inflammation—mainly pro-inflammatory cytokines (e.g., IL-6, IL-1) and experimental endotoxin—on social experience (increased social disconnection, altered sensitivity to social threat and reward, and context-dependent affiliative drives). They do not provide evidence that anti-inflammatory processes or IL-10 increase perceived social support, nor do they report mechanisms whereby anti‑inflammatory cytokines enhance social perception. Anti‑inflammatory treatments are discussed only as a potential/untested avenue for loneliness or depression, so the specific causal claim about IL‑10 → greater perceived social support is not addressed by these citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes, such as increased IL-10, directly cause individuals to perceive greater social support. This is because anti-inflammatory cytokines enhance brain function related to social perception, making people more likely to notice and appreciate supportive relationships in their environment.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes, such as increased IL-10, directly cause individuals to perceive greater social support. This is because anti-inflammatory cytokines enhance brain function related to social perception, making people more likely to notice and appreciate supportive relationships in their environment.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

