   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased IL-10, an anti-inflammatory cytokine, directly reduces inflammatory activity, which is known to improve sleep quality and decrease sleep disturbances. Conversely, decreased IL-10 can lead to increased inflammation and worsen sleep disturbance. The causal direction is from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), not the reverse.', 'session_id': '78d716d8-6340-4d2c-8c61-bf61365a70a9', 'prob_std': 0.23830205780966512, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43440-021-00333-0'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7974773691267705, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review thoroughly covers IL-10’s immunoregulatory roles, transcriptional control, cellular sources, and involvement in infections and autoimmune CNS inflammation (e.g., EAE/MS), but it does not provide evidence that IL-10 directly modulates neuronal activity in sleep-regulating brain regions, nor any data linking IL-10 levels to PSQI or sleep need. The specific causal mechanism and inverse relationship between IL-10 and sleep disturbance described in the motivation are not addressed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited cross-sectional study reports an inverse association between serum IL-10 and PSQI scores (lower IL-10 linked to poorer sleep) in hemodialysis patients, supporting the negative relationship direction. However, the paper does not demonstrate causality, directionality, or the proposed neural mechanism (IL-10 directly promoting neuronal activity in sleep-regulating brain regions). The study’s design and content do not support the specific causal/physiological claims in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses reciprocal links between sleep and inflammatory biology and describes effects of pro-inflammatory cytokines (e.g., IL-1, TNF, IL-6) on sleep regulation, but the provided content does not report evidence that the anti‑inflammatory cytokine IL‑10 directly increases neuronal activity in sleep‑regulating brain regions or that elevated IL‑10 reduces PSQI-measured sleep disturbance. The specific mechanistic and directional claims about IL‑10 → decreased sleep disturbance are not supported by the excerpts shown."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents IL-10’s broad immunoregulatory roles and some effects in the central nervous system (neuroprotection, modulation of microglia, possible benefit for certain post‑COVID neurological symptoms), but it does not provide evidence that IL-10 increases neuronal activity in sleep-regulating brain regions, nor any empirical link between IL-10 levels and sleep disturbance measured by PSQI. The specific causal mechanism and inverse relationship claimed (IL-10 → reduced sleep need / lower PSQI) are not addressed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43440-021-00333-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines relationships between PSQI-measured sleep quality and proinflammatory cytokines (IL‑6, IL‑17, IL‑23) in IBD patients and reports higher proinflammatory cytokine levels with poorer sleep. It does not measure or present evidence on IL‑10 levels or on IL‑10 causing decreased sleep disturbance via stimulation of sleep‑regulating neural circuits. The specific directional mechanistic claim about IL‑10 → reduced PSQI scores is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Elevated IL-10 production directly promotes increased neuronal activity in sleep-regulating brain regions, leading to a reduced need for sleep and thereby decreasing sleep disturbance scores on the PSQI. Conversely, lower IL-10 levels fail to stimulate these neural circuits, resulting in a greater accumulation of sleep need and higher PSQI scores. The directionality remains from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), rather than the reverse.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Elevated IL-10 production directly promotes increased neuronal activity in sleep-regulating brain regions, leading to a reduced need for sleep and thereby decreasing sleep disturbance scores on the PSQI. Conversely, lower IL-10 levels fail to stimulate these neural circuits, resulting in a greater accumulation of sleep need and higher PSQI scores. The directionality remains from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), rather than the reverse.', 'perplexity': 1.2506856921320668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8566036078795134, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.11810028171057317, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0010641619938890